\par\par \b \uc2 INSTANT VIEW 4-Shirakawa says BOJ spent much time debating yen\b0\par\par\uc2 771 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 August 2010\par 09:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  (For more stories on the Japanese economy, click [ID:nECONJP])\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 10 (Reuters) - Bank of Japan Governor Masaaki Shirakawa said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's board spent much time debating the recent rise in the yen and how it could affect business sentiment.  *************************************************************\par \par \uc2  KEY POINTS:\par \par \uc2  -- "I acknowledge that yen rises pose a downside risk to business sentiment," Shirakawa told a news conference. "We need to examine the impact on the overall economy in a balanced way."\par \par \uc2  -- The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's board earlier left interest rates at 0.1 percent by a unanimous vote and held off on new policy steps.\par \par \uc2  -- It also kept its economic assessment unchanged.\par \par \uc2  -- The BOJ said it needed to watch how fiscal problems in some European countries could, through developments in global financial markets, affect the Japanese and global economies.\par \par \uc2  COMMENTARY:\par \par \uc2  HIDEKI MATSUMURA, SENIOR ECONOMIST, JAPAN RESEARCH INSTITUTE\par \par \uc2  "Overall he sounded like he was weighing downside risks more than last time.\par \par \uc2  "He made more comments on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  and is cautious about it. But current \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels have not reached a point to pose a major risk.\par \par \uc2  "Attention needs to be paid to the domestic real economy, especially capital spending and production-related data such as machinery orders and industrial output.\par \par \uc2  "If the yen appreciates rapidly due to the outcome of the Fed policy meeting and it firms towards 80 yen (vs the dollar), calls from the government for the Bank of Japan to do something are expected to increase."\par \par \uc2  - - - -\par \par \uc2  ADRIAN FOSTER, HEAD OF FINANCIAL MARKETS RESEARCH, RABOBANK INTERNATIONAL, HONG KONG\par \par \uc2  "If you go back to 2003 there were a lot of people who were unhappy with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's swashbuckling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  "The BOJ have half an eye on what people in the United States are thinking. The United States is focused on China now and I don't think Japan wants to compete on that stage.\par \par \uc2  "Dollar/yen is getting crushed by U.S. interest rates. I think relative to expectations, the Federal Reserve is going to be on the hawkish side.\par \par \uc2  "They have to discuss the downturn, but they will look at the housing market and say it's going to take a couple more months for it to find its feet.\par \par \uc2  "If the Fed does a light version of quantitative easing, that puts the spotlight on the BOJ, but my main scenario is for all major central banks to be on hold."\par \par \uc2  - - - -\par \par \uc2  AKITSUGU BANDOU, SENIOR ECONOMIST, OKASAN SECURITIES\par \par \uc2  "The BOJ is presuming that the domestic economy will basically continue to recover gradually, and based on such thinking it probably isn't very eager to take steps such as lowering interest rates further.\par \par \uc2  "That may change if there is some type of crisis such as if credit risks were to increase globally or the yen were to rise further.\par \par \uc2  "The recent rise in the yen has more to do with a fall in the dollar rather than yen strength. Unless the United States moves there is little that can be done.\par \par \uc2  "Unless the factors behind the dollar's fall are resolved, there will probably be little impact even if the BOJ makes some  efforts."\par \par \uc2  - - - -\par \par \uc2  TAKASHI MINAMI, CHIEF ECONOMIST, NORINCHUKIN RESEARCH INSTITUTE\par \par \uc2  "The BOJ is aware of the negative impact of the stronger yen but it hasn't made any policy changes. It seems they still want to wait and see before taking any action, in particular they want to see the outcome of the FOMC meeting.\par \par \uc2  "They seem to be a little more optimistic about the economy than market consensus."\par \par \uc2  MARKET REACTION:\par \par \uc2  -- For yen updates click <FXNEWS>; for prices click <JPY=>\par \par \uc2  -- For JGB updates, click <MMNEWS>, for prices click <2JGBv1>\par \par \uc2  -- For stocks click [.T], for the Nikkei share average click <.N2v25>\par \par \uc2  BACKGROUND:\par \par \uc2  -- The BOJ has held its key interest rate at 0.1 percent since late 2008 although it set up a facility offering cheap funds to banks at an emergency meeting in December, which it then expanded in March.\par \par \uc2  -- Signs of U.S. economic weakness have pushed the dollar towards a 15-year low against the yen, clouding the outlook for Japan's export-reliant growth and putting pressure on the BOJ to loosen its already very easy monetary policy.\par \par \uc2  -- Japan's economy likely slowed considerably in the April-June quarter after expanding an annualised 5 percent in the first quarter, a Reuters poll showed, as export growth moderated and a boost to consumer spending from government incentives faded.  (Reporting by Leika Kihara, Tetsushi Kajimoto, Rie Ishiguro)     \par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/SHIRAKAWA (INSTANT VIEW 4)|LANGEN|J|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|U|NAT   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100813e68a000l4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
