\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-UPDATE 1-Yen's rise too rapid - ex-BOJ official\b0\par\par\uc2 398 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 August 2010\par 11:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Hard to convince other nations on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Timing not ripe for fresh action by BOJ\par \par \uc2 * BOJ's response to downside risks in focus (Adds comments)\par \par \uc2 By Rie Ishiguro\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Aug 9 (Reuters) - The yen's current rise is too rapid and could prompt the Japanese authorities to step up their verbal warnings, a former senior Bank of Japan official said on Monday, as the yen approached 15-year highs against the dollar .\par \par \uc2 But Eiji Hirano, who attended Group of Seven meetings while at the BOJ, also said Tokyo is unlikely to be able to convince the United States and Europe to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to weaken the yen.\par \par \uc2 "If the pace of the yen's rise is too fast, Japan may try to check it by showing its readiness to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Hirano, who was assistant governor at the BOJ until 2006, said in an interview with Reuters.\par \par \uc2 "But current \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels are the natural reflection of changing monetary policies in the United States and Europe and the fact that Japan has not changed its policy. Japan is thus unlikely to obtain other nations' consent for launching a solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 On the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy review on Tuesday, Hirano said the time is not ripe for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to take fresh action.\par \par \uc2 "It cannot be said that the economy's gradual recovery will fall to pieces because of the yen's appreciation ... But the focus will be how the BOJ judges the economy's outlook and how it will respond to downside risks in the future," Hirano said.\par \par \uc2 He said downside risks are growing not only because of developed economies' poor performances but also emerging economies' recent policy tightening.\par \par \uc2 Hirano, who has close ties with central bankers across the globe, is now executive vice president of Toyota Financial Services, the financial arm of \uc2 Toyota Motor Corp\par \par \uc2 The dollar has bounced back to around 85.50 yen after hitting an eight-month trough of 85.02 yen on Friday, but traders say it has room to fall again on expectations the Federal Reserve could ease monetary policy further even as early as this week. The Fed meets on Tuesday, with a decision expected around 1815 GMT. (Editing by Michael Watson)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/YEN (INTERVIEW, UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100812e689000tq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
