\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Euro Recoups Early Losses; Dollar Stays Firm\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicholas Hastings   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 599 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 2010\par 11:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--The euro rebounded from earlier lows Thursday ahead of the latest European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy meeting as investors waited to see just how cautious the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be about growth prospects.\par \par \uc2 The dollar, meanwhile, continued to find support after strong U.S. data Wednesday reduced recent speculation that the Fed will need to ease monetary policy further.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the yen was undermined by the latest warning from Tokyo that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength is posing a risk to the country's recovery.\par \par \uc2 Trade Minister Masayuki Naoshima said he was "really concerned about the yen's recent rise" and that Japanese firms are facing "huge \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks."\par \par \uc2 The minister said that "we may need to take some sort of action in the future."\par \par \uc2 However, Naoshima didn't elaborate and he left financial markets guessing whether the Japanese government would sanction \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the yen's advance.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the release of stronger-than-expected U.S. economic data, including the latest ADP private-sector employment report and the non-manufacturing Institute of Supply Management survey, had lifted the dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had previously been suffering from suggestions that the Fed will need to ease policy further.\par \par \uc2 However, analysts said any improvement in market sentiment is likely to be limited ahead of the release of the latest U.S. non-farm payrolls employment data Friday, in case these figures prove disappointing.\par \par \uc2 The consensus forecast is for a fall of 60,000 in July, following a 125,000 decline in June.\par \par \uc2 The euro fell sharply against the dollar after the release of the U.S. data Wednesday and has remained under pressure ahead of the ECB meeting that ends at 1145 GMT.\par \par \uc2 See the euro's sharp drop against the dollar after the data:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4370"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4370}}}\par \par \uc2 The ECB is widely expected to leave interest rates unchanged but there will be considerable market interest in what its president Jean Claude Trichet has to say about policy.\par \par \uc2 Recent economic data out of the euro zone have come in stronger than expected but there are concerns that as fiscal austerity plans are introduced, the ECB may have to keep policy accommodative for longer than has been anticipated.\par \par \uc2 "The ECB press conference may weaken euro money markets," said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists at the \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 By 0925 GMT, the euro had rebounded to $1.3195 from $1.3169 late Wednesday in New York, according to EBS. The euro had at one point fallen as far as $1.3118.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was down at Y86.25 from Y86.30, but paring losses after an earlier slide to Y85.99.\par \par \uc2 The euro was at Y113.80 from Y113.65 while the dollar was down at CHF1.0470 from CHF1.0523.\par \par \uc2 The pound was down at $1.5895 from $1.5901 as investors wait for confirmation that the Bank of England will also leave its interest rates unchanged.\par \par \uc2 -By Nicholas Hastings, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 20 7842 9493; nick.hastings@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkBackEurope@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 05-08-10 0928GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1035 28038 4163 44462   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100805e68500146\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
