\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: 40-Yr JGBs Highest Tender Yield Eyed 1.83%-1.84%\b0\par\par\uc2 1,621 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 2010\par 05:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0336 GMT [Dow Jones] Highest accepted yield for 40-year JGBs at 0345 GMT auction result likely around 1.830%-1.840%, traders say. Latest issue reopens No. 3 issue sold in May, carrying 2.2% coupon. Despite low yield, analysts expect smooth auction as supply/demand conditions remain favorable in superlong sector, with life insurance firms expected to remain strong buyers. 40-year JGBs sold in Dutch-style auction. Lead September JGB futures down 0.13 at 142.16. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0335 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds mostly tad lower, tracking losses in U.S. Treasurys overnight on rising risk appetite after better-than-expected U.S. services sector in July tempered concerns over faltering U.S. economy. "Investors already priced in policy rate hikes to 2.00% by the year-end, so I think yields may not go so far," says bond dealer. Market widely expects BOT to raise key rate by 25 bps to 1.50% at meeting later this month. Investors keeping focus on U.S. non-farm payrolls due Friday for potential cues. Yields expected to stay at current levels. Bid/offer yields of issue due May 2015 at 3.03%/3.00% vs 3.02%/3.00% late yesterday, November 2016 at 3.17%/3.15% vs 3.15%/3.12%, June 2019 at 3.27%/3.25% vs 3.27%/3.24%.(oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0331 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ government bond tender much better bid that last week, says local bond trader; "However, it got bid down quite a bit into the tender and it didn't go at the level that it was at when we went to into it." NZ DMO sold NZ$100 million of April 2013 bond at 4.12% vs 4.10% pre-tender; NZ$100 million of NZ May 2021 bond sold for 5.33% vs 5.32%. Trader adds, higher NZ 2Q unemployment numbers caused conjecture prior to auction that it wouldn't go that well; "but they have underperformed against the swap and that has attracted some attention from buyers on that basis." (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0331 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian government bonds continue rising, with 10-year yield falling to fresh record low, as foreign investors continue buying after Bank Indonesia yesterday left interest rates unchanged, dealers say. "Foreign inflows continue as Indonesian yields are still relatively higher among peer countries in the region," dealer says; adds USD/IDR slight rebound so far hasn't dampened appetite for Indonesian sovereign notes; 10-year yield down to 7.93% vs 7.99% yesterday, while 20-year yield down to 8.95% vs 9.05%. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0329 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares last down 0.1% at 486.05 after touching 489.2 earlier with some investors selling amid rising concerns \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will act slowly in boosting cash flows into the economy, and hopes for a quick increase of credit supply are waning. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already fixed its benchmark rate at 8% for this month, and if it wants to ease borrowing costs for companies, we will have to wait for September, and weak inflows will certainly pull down share prices in the coming days," says trader. Volume thin with 23 million shares valued at VND700 billion traded. Among decliners, \uc2 PetroVietnam Finance\uc2  (PVF.VH) down 2.2% at VND22,300, Masan (MSN.VH) down 1.0% at VND52,000, \uc2 Hoang Anh Gia Lai (HAG.VH) \uc2 down 0.6% at VND78,500. Tips immediate support at 485. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0329 GMT [Dow Jones] RBNZ may pause current rate hike cycle as early as September, according to snap poll of 13 economists after today's weaker-than-expected 2Q labor data; economists now see 60% chance of rate hike, 40% chance of pause; in previous poll all economists tipped September rate hike. While September pause may be on cards, majority still expecting pause in October with two tipping December, while \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  reiterates no pause expected this year. Previously four had said \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would keep hiking for next three meetings at least. "Today's report combined with the weaker commodity price news of late clearly favours our view that the next policy review on September 16 will likely see the last rate hike for this year," says Darren Gibbs, \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  Chief Economist. Adds "with even that hike likely to be called into question if economic and other developments continue to disappoint in coming weeks." (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0326 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares last +0.4% at 870.62 in active trade. Upside likely limited as upward GDP revision largely priced in; buying in big caps subsides after benchmark up 16% since end May on back of strengthening economy, says Far East Securities analyst Weerachai Krongsamsri. Adds profit taking expected to cap SET Index at 875, but support at 860 should hold as overall sentiment remains positive. Among top actives, \uc2 Sri Trang Agro Industry\uc2  (STA.TH) +4.2% at THB22.50, Thai Rubber Latex Corporation (TRUBB.TH) +6.7% at THB9.60, \uc2 True Corporation\uc2  (TRUE.TH) +5.9% at THB5.40, \uc2 Charoen Pokphand Foods\uc2  (CPF.TH) +1.3% at THB24.10, TMB Bank (TMB.TH) flat at THB2.12. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0317 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex called to open about 25-45 points higher, tracking positive trade in most Asian markets and as undertone remains upbeat on sustained foreign inflows, good monsoon rains recently, says dealer at local brokerage. Adds, ample liquidity, announcement from index heavyweight \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2  (500325.BY) it's forming shale gas JV with U.S. firm \uc2 Carrizo Oil & Gas\uc2  to further support. Foreign funds have bought shares worth over INR21.7 billion this month, were buyers of about INR103.6 billion in July. Still, dealer reckons Sensex to find strong resistance at 18,320 for session, as valuations stretched in most index stocks after over 9.6% rally since June 9. Adds, outperformance last 3 months vs most emerging markets could also lead to some consolidation near term. Sensex ended +0.6% at 18,217.44 yesterday, has gained about 2.0% so far this week. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0317 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR tipped tad higher tracking Asian cues, but gains may not sustain as custodial banks expected to buy local unit, shares eyed opening in positive territory, says trader with foreign bank. Pair may open around 46.20 vs 46.17 late yesterday. "Some portfolio inflows are seen and the rupee may appreciate a tad but dollar may gain later if risk-averse participants worry that tomorrow's nonfarm payroll data will be weak." Tips pair in 46.10-46.35 band. Foreign funds net buyers of provisional INR6.89 billion worth of local stocks yesterday, 1-month NDFs quoting at 46.39/46.46. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0308 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY now down due to selling by Japan exporters, but may be supported around 85.70 later in day as investors could start to shy away from active trading ahead of July U.S. jobs data tomorrow, says Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Ueda Harlow senior dealer Yuzo Sakai. Notes "many investors remain cautious about whether the dollar may keep falling. But they cannot sell it actively before the release of U.S. jobs data for fear that the results could turn out better than expected and the dollar may rise back sharply next week." But adds if pace of gains in Asia shares slows later, that could raise demand for safe-haven JPY across the board, in turn pushing USD/JPY down further. USD/JPY now at 86.11 vs session high 86.46. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0301 GMT [Dow Jones] Japanese finance minister's shift in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market rhetoric indicates authorities starting to worry about JPY's rise. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unlikely for now, but eyes on any further change in tone as could signal Tokyo closer to stepping into market for 1st time since March 2004. Wednesday morning, Yoshihiko Noda said he was "keeping an eye" on market, suggesting little displeasure with JPY's rise, helped push USD/JPY to 8-month low of 85.40. But Wednesday afternoon, changed tone to "the (yen's) current movement is a little one-sided... I will monitor (exchange rates) more closely." The "one-sided" label suggests officials think speculation driving market, rather than economic fundamentals. Tone more serious if they begin describing moves as "volatile" or "sharp." And watch out if they start threatening to take "appropriate action;" as is sign \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  under serious consideration. Another notch higher to "ready to take bold and decisive action" means \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be imminent. See Money Talks: \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Noda Escalates Verbal Warnings On Yen Rise (takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0259 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds tipped lower on overnight falls in U.S. Treasurys as risk sentiment improves, while traders may avoid heavy positions ahead of INR130 billion auction tomorrow, says trader with state-run bank. "Traders cut positions in the last leg of Wednesday's session to make room for new bonds at tomorrow's debt sale and I expect the sell-off will continue today." Adds market eyes weekly inflation data on food, fuel and primary articles due 0600 GMT. Tips benchmark 7.80% 2020 bond in 99.50-100.00 band vs 99.70 last close. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com) [ 08-04-10 2336ET ]\par \par \uc2 