ty price news of late clearly favours our view that the next policy review on September 16 will likely see the last rate hike for this year," says Darren Gibbs, \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  Chief Economist. Adds "with even that hike likely to be called into question if economic and other developments continue to disappoint in coming weeks." (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0326 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares last +0.4% at 870.62 in active trade. Upside likely limited as upward GDP revision largely priced in; buying in big caps subsides after benchmark up 16% since end May on back of strengthening economy, says Far East Securities analyst Weerachai Krongsamsri. Adds profit taking expected to cap SET Index at 875, but support at 860 should hold as overall sentiment remains positive. Among top actives, \uc2 Sri Trang Agro Industry\uc2  (STA.TH) +4.2% at THB22.50, Thai Rubber Latex Corporation (TRUBB.TH) +6.7% at THB9.60, \uc2 True Corporation\uc2  (TRUE.TH) +5.9% at THB5.40, \uc2 Charoen Pokphand Foods\uc2  (CPF.TH) +1.3% at THB24.10, TMB Bank (TMB.TH) flat at THB2.12. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0317 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex called to open about 25-45 points higher, tracking positive trade in most Asian markets and as undertone remains upbeat on sustained foreign inflows, good monsoon rains recently, says dealer at local brokerage. Adds, ample liquidity, announcement from index heavyweight \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2  (500325.BY) it's forming shale gas JV with U.S. firm \uc2 Carrizo Oil & Gas\uc2  to further support. Foreign funds have bought shares worth over INR21.7 billion this month, were buyers of about INR103.6 billion in July. Still, dealer reckons Sensex to find strong resistance at 18,320 for session, as valuations stretched in most index stocks after over 9.6% rally since June 9. Adds, outperformance last 3 months vs most emerging markets could also lead to some consolidation near term. Sensex ended +0.6% at 18,217.44 yesterday, has gained about 2.0% so far this week. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0317 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR tipped tad higher tracking Asian cues, but gains may not sustain as custodial banks expected to buy local unit, shares eyed opening in positive territory, says trader with foreign bank. Pair may open around 46.20 vs 46.17 late yesterday. "Some portfolio inflows are seen and the rupee may appreciate a tad but dollar may gain later if risk-averse participants worry that tomorrow's nonfarm payroll data will be weak." Tips pair in 46.10-46.35 band. Foreign funds net buyers of provisional INR6.89 billion worth of local stocks yesterday, 1-month NDFs quoting at 46.39/46.46. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0308 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY now down due to selling by Japan exporters, but may be supported around 85.70 later in day as investors could start to shy away from active trading ahead of July U.S. jobs data tomorrow, says Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Ueda Harlow senior dealer Yuzo Sakai. Notes "many investors remain cautious about whether the dollar may keep falling. But they cannot sell it actively before the release of U.S. jobs data for fear that the results could turn out better than expected and the dollar may rise back sharply next week." But adds if pace of gains in Asia shares slows later, that could raise demand for safe-haven JPY across the board, in turn pushing USD/JPY down further. USD/JPY now at 86.11 vs session high 86.46. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0301 GMT [Dow Jones] Japanese finance minister's shift in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market rhetoric indicates authorities starting to worry about JPY's rise. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unlikely for now, but eyes on any further change in tone as could signal Tokyo closer to stepping into market for 1st time since March 2004. Wednesday morning, Yoshihiko Noda said he was "keeping an eye" on market, suggesting little displeasure with JPY's rise, helped push USD/JPY to 8-month low of 85.40. But Wednesday afternoon, changed tone to "the (yen's) current movement is a little one-sided... I will monitor (exchange rates) more closely." The "one-sided" label suggests officials think speculation driving market, rather than economic fundamentals. Tone more serious if they begin describing moves as "volatile" or "sharp." And watch out if they start threatening to take "appropriate action;" as is sign \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  under serious consideration. Another notch higher to "ready to take bold and decisive action" means \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be imminent. See Money Talks: \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Noda Escalates Verbal Warnings On Yen Rise (takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0259 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds tipped lower on overnight falls in U.S. Treasurys as risk sentiment improves, while traders may avoid heavy positions ahead of INR130 billion auction tomorrow, says trader with state-run bank. "Traders cut positions in the last leg of Wednesday's session to make room for new bonds at tomorrow's debt sale and I expect the sell-off will continue today." Adds market eyes weekly inflation data on food, fuel and primary articles due 0600 GMT. Tips benchmark 7.80% 2020 bond in 99.50-100.00 band vs 99.70 last close. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0256 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  says recent pick-up in INR "is unlikely to be sustained, as a widening current account deficit and overvaluation on an REER basis set the stage for further underperformance." Notes, debt-creating capital inflows -- such as bank capital raisings, commercial borrowings, FDI -- set to rise in coming months as domestic borrowing costs increase; "we do not expect this to trigger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation given the likelihood that the RBI would step in to absorb the inflows." Adds, while INR looks set to rise near term, house expects it to decline, with USD/INR at 47 in 3 months, 47.5 in 6 months; keeps 12 month forecast at 46.5. Pair at 46.18 late Wednesday. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com) [ 08-04-10 2335ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100805e6850002t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
