so lead to some consolidation near term. Sensex ended +0.6% at 18,217.44 yesterday, has gained about 2.0% so far this week. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0317 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR tipped tad higher tracking Asian cues, but gains may not sustain as custodial banks expected to buy local unit, shares eyed opening in positive territory, says trader with foreign bank. Pair may open around 46.20 vs 46.17 late yesterday. "Some portfolio inflows are seen and the rupee may appreciate a tad but dollar may gain later if risk-averse participants worry that tomorrow's nonfarm payroll data will be weak." Tips pair in 46.10-46.35 band. Foreign funds net buyers of provisional INR6.89 billion worth of local stocks yesterday, 1-month NDFs quoting at 46.39/46.46. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0308 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY now down due to selling by Japan exporters, but may be supported around 85.70 later in day as investors could start to shy away from active trading ahead of July U.S. jobs data tomorrow, says Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Ueda Harlow senior dealer Yuzo Sakai. Notes "many investors remain cautious about whether the dollar may keep falling. But they cannot sell it actively before the release of U.S. jobs data for fear that the results could turn out better than expected and the dollar may rise back sharply next week." But adds if pace of gains in Asia shares slows later, that could raise demand for safe-haven JPY across the board, in turn pushing USD/JPY down further. USD/JPY now at 86.11 vs session high 86.46. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0301 GMT [Dow Jones] Japanese finance minister's shift in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market rhetoric indicates authorities starting to worry about JPY's rise. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unlikely for now, but eyes on any further change in tone as could signal Tokyo closer to stepping into market for 1st time since March 200