\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY May Be Supported Around 85.70 - Dealer\b0\par\par\uc2 1,737 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 2010\par 05:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0308 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY now down due to selling by Japan exporters, but may be supported around 85.70 later in day as investors could start to shy away from active trading ahead of July U.S. jobs data tomorrow, says Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and Ueda Harlow senior dealer Yuzo Sakai. Notes "many investors remain cautious about whether the dollar may keep falling. But they cannot sell it actively before the release of U.S. jobs data for fear that the results could turn out better than expected and the dollar may rise back sharply next week." But adds if pace of gains in Asia shares slows later, that could raise demand for safe-haven JPY across the board, in turn pushing USD/JPY down further. USD/JPY now at 86.11 vs session high 86.46. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0301 GMT [Dow Jones] Japanese finance minister's shift in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market rhetoric indicates authorities starting to worry about JPY's rise. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unlikely for now, but eyes on any further change in tone as could signal Tokyo closer to stepping into market for 1st time since March 2004. Wednesday morning, Yoshihiko Noda said he was "keeping an eye" on market, suggesting little displeasure with JPY's rise, helped push USD/JPY to 8-month low of 85.40. But Wednesday afternoon, changed tone to "the (yen's) current movement is a little one-sided... I will monitor (exchange rates) more closely." The "one-sided" label suggests officials think speculation driving market, rather than economic fundamentals. Tone more serious if they begin describing moves as "volatile" or "sharp." And watch out if they start threatening to take "appropriate action;" as is sign \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  under serious consideration. Another notch higher to "ready to take bold and decisive action" means \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be imminent. See Money Talks: \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Noda Escalates Verbal Warnings On Yen Rise (takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0259 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds tipped lower on overnight falls in U.S. Treasurys as risk sentiment improves, while traders may avoid heavy positions ahead of INR130 billion auction tomorrow, says trader with state-run bank. "Traders cut positions in the last leg of Wednesday's session to make room for new bonds at tomorrow's debt sale and I expect the sell-off will continue today." Adds market eyes weekly inflation data on food, fuel and primary articles due 0600 GMT. Tips benchmark 7.80% 2020 bond in 99.50-100.00 band vs 99.70 last close. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0256 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  says recent pick-up in INR "is unlikely to be sustained, as a widening current account deficit and overvaluation on an REER basis set the stage for further underperformance." Notes, debt-creating capital inflows -- such as bank capital raisings, commercial borrowings, FDI -- set to rise in coming months as domestic borrowing costs increase; "we do not expect this to trigger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation given the likelihood that the RBI would step in to absorb the inflows." Adds, while INR looks set to rise near term, house expects it to decline, with USD/INR at 47 in 3 months, 47.5 in 6 months; keeps 12 month forecast at 46.5. Pair at 46.18 late Wednesday. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0247 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 1.3% at 9609.30 midday on U.S. stocks' gains and weaker yen, but intraday 9688.87 peak thus far likely to be high for day due to lack of further buying catalysts, traders say. "Short-covering was done in the morning. For the rest of the day, investors may stay sidelined," Japanese brokerage manager says, putting Nikkei resistance at 9700. Also says volume may stay low (TSE 1st section volume just 739 million shares) as investors remain concerned about U.S. economy before jobs data is out on Friday, they may not want to take large positions before then. After relatively quick start after 1Q earnings reports Toyota (7203.TO) now up just 1.1% at Y3,125, while Isuzu (7202.TO) up 6.8% at Y266; Honda (7267.TO) also up 2.1% at Y2,838. Higher USD/JPY (now 86.17) helping major tech exporters such as Fanuc (6954.TO), up 2.7% at Y10,280. Real estate leading sectoral gainer, with \uc2 Mitsui Fudosan\uc2  (8801.TO) up 3.1% at Y1,307. \uc2 Renesas Electronics\uc2  (6723.TO) off 5.2% to Y691 after \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  cuts rating to Neutral. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0248 GMT [Dow Jones] The Philippines' stable July inflation data of 3.9% gives \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility to keep rates steady. But \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  economist Prakriti Sofat says "there's a need for some policy normalization to ensure that low inflation expectations become entrenched." She adds, "with the policy rate unchanged since July 2009, the real policy rate has come down sharply-currently running well below past averages." Adds, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to begin a rate hike in 4Q with 25 bps and another hike in 2Q next year. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0242 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares up 0.9% at 3009.602, as investors buying back most bank, consumer-, commodity-related stocks after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left key interest rate unchanged at 6.5% yesterday, traders sa