\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ UPDATE: Japan's Finance Minister Steps Up Warnings Vs Yen Rises\b0\par\par\uc2 500 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2010\par 11:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Adds \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  comments from \uc2 Nissan Motor\uc2 's chief operating officer, plus more background.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \par    OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's finance minister Wednesday escalated his warnings against a rising yen, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reached a fresh eight-month high versus the dollar prompting Japanese exporters to cry out for government help.\par \par \uc2 But the latest input from Yoshihiko Noda had little impact on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Lack of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reaction suggests investors still don't expect any imminent yen-selling/dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or they expect that even if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  takes such action, its impact would be limited given the greenback's broad declines fueled by weakness in the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 Noda's divergence from his previous commentary--that he was just "closely watching" the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market--comes after the dollar touched Y85.32, its lowest since Nov. 27.\par \par \uc2 "I feel the (yen's) current movement is a little one-sided," Yoshihiko Noda told reporters at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "I will monitor (yen exchange rates) more closely."\par \par \uc2 Decline in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  versus the yen make Japanese products more expensive in the U.S. and other dollar-linked markets. Such exchange-rate movement could exacerbate a recent slowdown in Japan's export-led recovery and also deepen its deflationary woe. The government's concern over the economy is growing to the extent that on Tuesday, Prime Minister Naoto Kan said he would examine whether additional stimulus measures are needed to escape the economic hardship.\par \par \uc2 "In any case, our fundamental stance is that any excessive fluctuation or disorderly movement in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates could have an adverse effect on the stability of economies and financial markets, and so are not desirable," he said.\par \par \uc2 Noda's sharpened rhetoric coincided with pleas from Japanese manufacturers being threatened by a strong yen.\par \par \uc2 "With the current rate...there would be an impact on our orders for export," \uc2 Nissan Motor Co\uc2 .'s (7201.TO) chief operating officer Toshiyuki Shiga told reporters earlier in the day. "I hope each country will cooperate to minimise the impact of the yen's strength, and I hope the (Japanese) government will make such efforts."\par \par \uc2 But Noda declined to comment on what the government might do if the yen were to keep strengthening, suggesting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't yet on the horizon.\par \par \uc2 In the past, when the Liberal Democratic Party was in government, Japanese officials often threatened to take "appropriate action" before stepping into the markets. While it is not clear yet whether the current Democratic Party of Japan-led government will follow the same procedure, Noda's refusal to hint at any action suggests he remains cautious about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The dollar stood around Y85.44 at 0910 GMT.\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2781; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Yoshio Takahashi contributed to this report.)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 04, 2010 05:38 ET (09:38 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100804e684001wj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
