\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ MONEY TALKS: Japan Noda Escalates Verbal Warnings On Yen Rise\b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi  \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires Column  \par \uc2 655 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2010\par 13:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders should take note of the shift in rhetoric by Japan's finance minister--the yen's ascent is starting to worry the authorities.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  yet, even as the surge in the yen threatens to hurt exports and worsen deflation.\par \par \uc2 But investors would be advised to watch out for any further changes in tone that could signal Tokyo is getting closer to stepping into the market for the first time since March 2004.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday morning, Yoshihiko Noda said he was "keeping an eye" on the market, a comment that seemed to signal little displeasure with the yen's rise and helped push the dollar down to an eight-month low of Y85.40.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday afternoon, he changed his tune: "I feel the (yen's) current movement is a little one-sided... I will monitor (exchange rates) more closely."\par \par \uc2 That he thinks the moves were "one-sided" and that he's watching "more" closely now are important signals.\par \par \uc2 Japanese officials tend to choose their words carefully, hardening or softening their rhetoric as circumstances change in order to signal how displeased--or pleased--they may be with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements.\par \par \uc2 Generally, finance officials won't comment on the market when things are going their way.\par \par \uc2 If the yen starts to get out of line, they might say they're "closely watching" the market. That's still nothing to worry about, though, because at that point on the rhetoric scale they're still miles away from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But when they start describing moves as "volatile" or "sharp," they're starting to get serious. Labeling moves as "one-sided" suggests they think speculation may be driving the market, rather than economic fundamentals -- and they hate that.\par \par \uc2 If they start threatening to take "appropriate action," it's time to watch out. A warning like that doesn't mean \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is certain, but it's a sign they are considering it seriously.\par \par \uc2 Another notch higher to "ready to take bold and decisive action" means \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be imminent.\par \par \uc2 There's no guarantee Noda will follow past verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  conventions.\par \par \uc2 They were crafted by \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  bureaucrats when Japan was ruled by the Liberal Democratic Party, which was ousted by the Democratic Party of Japan last autumn. Back then, bureaucrats often wrote scripts for the finance minister giving clear guidance on what to say publicly when asked about the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 But the DPJ has been trying to change such bureaucracy-led policy-making. When Prime Minister Naoto Kan held the finance portfolio earlier this year, he sometimes diverted radically from official mantras and called openly for a weaker yen.\par \par \uc2 "If we had been writing scripts for him, he wouldn't have said such things," a finance ministry official told Dow Jones Newswires. "It's 100000000% wrong to think we controlled his statement."\par \par \uc2 Yet, Noda listens more attentively to MOF officials than Kan, including top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bureaucrat Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Rintaro Tamaki, according to some government officials and a senior DPJ lawmaker.\par \par \uc2 Before he stepped up his warning Wednesday, Noda discussed the yen's continued rise with MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials, according to a person familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 Paying attention to Noda's choice of words might pay off.\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6895-7558; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 04, 2010 07:28 ET (11:28 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100804e684002jd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
