\par\par \b \uc2 TIMELINE-Yen edges towards 15-yr high on dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 1,137 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2010\par 05:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 Aug 4 (Reuters) - The dollar hit an eight-month low against the yen on Wednesday as weak U.S. data and talk of the Federal Reserve embarking on further policy easing pushed down Treasury yields.\par \par \uc2 The dollar fell as low as 85.40 yen, its lowest since late November. A fall below a November low of 84.82 yen would take the pair to its lowest level in 15 years.\par \par \uc2 Here are some milestones in the yen's 138-year history:\par \par \uc2 1871 - The yen becomes Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as part of the Meiji Restoration, which marked the start of Japan's modernisation and opening to the rest of the world. Japan adopts the gold standard.\par \par \uc2 1949 - After World War Two the dollar's fixed rate is set at 360 yen via the Bretton Woods system, partly to help stabilise prices in the Japanese economy.\par \par \uc2 1959 - The dollar/yen exchange rate is liberalised. The margin of fluctuation is set at 0.5 percent on either side of its dollar parity.\par \par \uc2 1963 - The margin of fluctuation is widened to 0.75 percent.\par \par \uc2 1971 - United States abandons gold standard. The end of Bretton Woods system of fixed exchange rates forces a realignment of world \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dec. 1971 - Smithsonian Agreement sets the dollar/yen exchange rate at 308 yen, and allows it to fluctuate in a wider band between 301.07 yen and 314.93 yen.\par \par \uc2 1973 - Japanese monetary authorities decide to let the yen float freely against the dollar, and the yen appreciates as far as 263 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 1978 - The yen pushes through 200 to the dollar for the first time, strengthening as far as 177.\par \par \uc2 1980 to 1985 - Yen's appreciation halts and partially reverses despite Japan's big trade surpluses. Higher U.S. interest rates see Japanese investors put money in dollar assets.\par \par \uc2 1985 - The Group of Five industrial nations, the predecessor to the G7, sign the Plaza Accord in which they agree the dollar is overvalued and to weaken it. The yen climbs from its pre-accord level of around 240 to 211 in October and 200 in November, a 20 percent rise in just a few months.\par \par \uc2 1986 - The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  falls further to around 190 yen in January, 167 yen in April and 153 yen in August.\par \par \uc2 1987 - In February, six of the G7 nations sign the Louvre Accord, which aims to stabilise \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and halt the dollar's broad decline. The dollar still falls from near 153 to 137 in April and 120.80 by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 1988 - On Jan. 4, the dollar falls to a post-war low of 120.45 yen in Tokyo trade, a level that holds as the low for more than five years. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars and sell yen that day on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Aug. 17, 1993 - The dollar declines to a new post-war low of 100.40 yen in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 June 21, 1994 - The dollar falls through the key 100 yen level and touches a record postwar low of 99.85 yen in New York trade before finishing at 100.30 yen.\par \par \uc2 April 19, 1995 - The dollar hits a record postwar low at 79.75 yen after U.S.-Japanese trade frictions spark heavy selling. By the end of the year it is near 103.40.\par \par \uc2 1998 - Asian financial crisis sees yen weaken to nearly 148 yen vs dollar in August, even after U.S. authorities join the Bank of Japan to buy yen, spending $833 million, in June.\par \par \uc2 In October, dollar tumbles from near 136 yen to 111.50 yen, as carry trades unwind following the near-collapse of hedge fund major \uc2 Long-Term Capital Management\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 1999 - The yen strengthens further despite repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , reaching 102 in November.\par \par \uc2 2001 - Following the Sept 11 attacks on the United States, Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen for dollars.\par \par \uc2 2003 - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  begins massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the yen's rise against the dollar, partly to shield Japanese exporters as the economy remains stuck in its post-bubble slump and deflation. The MOF spends 20.4 trillion yen ($200 billion) over the year, nearly all of it to buy dollars and sell yen.\par \par \uc2 2004 - The MOF spends 14.8 trillion yen ($145 billion) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first quarter of the year, including 1.67 trillion yen buying dollars on Jan. 9 alone. But the MOF ceases \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March and has never since resumed.\par \par \uc2 2005 - The yen hits a high of 101.67 yen in January but then falls, hitting 121.40 in December. Yen carry trades and Japanese investors shifting funds into foreign assets drive the slide.\par \par \uc2 June 2007 - The dollar hits a 4-1/2-year high of 124.14 yen.\par \par \uc2 July 2007 - Yen's broad depreciation takes it to a 22-year low on a real effective exchange rate (REER) basis. Since January 2005 the yen loses 25 percent of its value on a REER basis.\par \par \uc2 March 13, 2008 - The yen hits a 12-year high of 99.77.\par \par \uc2 Oct. 24, 2008 - Yen hits 13-year high of 90.87 vs the dollar. Also sets an all-time high of 55.11 against the Australian dollar, which loses almost a third of its value in just a month on a massive unwind of carry trades.\par \par \uc2 Oct. 27, 2008 - The yen's surge prompts the G7 to issue statement singling out the yen in warning on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market volatility.\par \par \uc2 Dec. 12, 2008 - The dollar falls through 90 yen for the first time in 13 years after a bill to rescue U.S. automakers fails in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Jan. 22, 2009 - Hits fresh 13-year high of 87.10 against dollar, driven up by risk aversion and option-led dollar selling.\par \par \uc2 Sept. 28, 2009 - Marks 8-month high of 88.23 against greenback, but later loses ground as Japan's finance minister tries to tone down earlier comments suggesting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely.\par \par \uc2 Oct. 7, 2009 - Yen reaches fresh 8-month high versus the greenback of 88.01 as market players probe how far Japanese authorities will allow the yen to rise.\par \par \uc2 Nov. 27, 2009 - Hits 14-year high of 84.82 on trading platform EBS.\par \par \uc2 Aug. 4, 2010 - Dollar hits eight-month low of 85.40 yen as weak U.S. data and talk of the Federal Reserve embarking on further policy easing pushed down Treasury yields. Japan's finance minister reiterates that he is closely watching \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves. Sources: Reuters, Bank of Japan, Bank of England (Writing by Mathew Veedon and Eric Burroughs, editing by Joseph Radford))\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /YEN TIMELINE|LANGEN|NAT|J|SI|RBN|UKI|AUF|ND|ABN|M|E|D|U|G|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100804e6840004p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
