\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECT: WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Tumbling Dollar Helps Euro, Yen, Pound Higher\b0\par\par\uc2 807 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 2010\par 11:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 ("=WORLD \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Tumbling Dollar Helps Euro, Yen, Pound Higher," at 0911 GMT, misstated the time frame surrounding the dollar's move against the yen in the second paragraph. The correct version follows:)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par    By Nicholas Hastings \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--Fears of further monetary easing in the U.S. combined with indications that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might not want to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  sent the dollar reeling Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The euro rallied through $1.32, taking it to a new three-month high, while the dollar sank under Y86, breaking last Friday's level to hit a new low for 2010.\par \par \uc2 The pound was also boosted, helping the U.K. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to flirt with $1.60, a level it last attained in February.\par \par \uc2 The selloff in the dollar gained momentum overnight as financial markets reacted to an article in the Wall Street Journal claiming that the U.S. Federal Reserve is considering a compromise where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would buy Treasuries to replace expiring mortgage bonds. This would keep the size of the Fed's balance sheet steady rather than allowing its portfolios to shrink gradually and so keep liquidity high at a time when deflationary fears have resurfaced.\par \par \uc2 The negative impact on the market was compounded by Fed Chairman's Ben Bernanke's earlier warning that there is a "considerable way to go" before the U.S. achieves full recovery.\par \par \uc2 The dollar selling really took off, however, after Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda appeared to suggest that the Japanese authorities aren't so keen on market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the yen from climbing any further.\par \par \uc2 Noda said that disorderly \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements could hurt economic activities. But, he added, "regarding how to respond to (recent yen rises), our fundamental stance is that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates are something that should basically be set by the market."\par \par \uc2 His comments helped allay some market fears that the Bank of Japan would start selling the yen if the dollar fell under Y85.00.\par \par \uc2 See how the dollar fell against the yen after Noda's comments:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4352"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4352}}}\par \par \uc2 The dollar's fall has raised fears that it is now entering a 'lose-lose' situation, where it suffers either because of poor U.S. growth prospects or because of strong data diminishing the safe-haven bid that has supported the dollar in the past.\par \par \uc2 "Such behaviour was exhibited in reverse for the USD not so long ago, when it rallied on good U.S. data because of the growth dynamic and rallied on weak U.S. data because of the safe haven angle," said Daragh Maher, deputy head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Credit Agricole in London.\par \par \uc2 Concern that the dollar could remain weak for lower than anticipated was also evident in shifting euro/dollar forecasts. \uc2 UBS\uc2 , for example, lifted its one-month forecast for the euro to $1.28 from $1.20. The pound also benefited from the dollar's sell off.\par \par \uc2 Apart from recent stronger-than-expected data out of the U.K., sterling was getting support from strong merger and acquisitions flows as foreign companies position themselves to take over U.K. entities, including \uc2 Tomkins PLC\uc2  and some assets owned by \uc2 BP PLC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Although positive global risk sentiment helped the U.S. and then Asian stock markets to rally, European stocks didn't fare so well, with most showing small losses at the start.\par \par \uc2 Overall sentiment may have been damaged by events in Australia where the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  left rates unchanged, largely as expected, but new retail sales figures for June showed only a 0.2% increase, less than the 0.3% rise that had been forecast.\par \par \uc2 This helped to push the Australian dollar lower, even though commodity prices in general were on the rise.\par \par \uc2 By 0930 GMT, the euro was up at $1.3226 from $1.3172 late on Monday in New York, according to EBS.\par \par \uc2 The dollar also fell to Y85.98 from Y86.51 while the euro slipped to Y113.74 from Y113.87.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was also down at CHF1.0370 from CHF1.0399 while the pound rallied to $1.5924 from $1.5889.\par \par \uc2 The Aussie was down at $0.9118 from $0.9136. At one stage it had fallen as far as $0.9066.\par \par \uc2 -By Nicholas Hastings, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 20 7842 9493; nick.hastings@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkBackEurope@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 03-08-10 0953GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1035 28038 4163 44462   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100803e683000ws\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
