\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Declines Against Yen After US Spending, Income Data\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bradley Davis   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 775 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 2010\par 14:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar fell against the euro and yen Tuesday after flat U.S. consumer- and personal-spending figures led to increasing concerns the U.S. recovery could be running out of steam.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. data comes after a Wall Street Journal report that said the Federal Reserve could consider changing the way it manages its massive portfolio, effectively preventing a tightening in monetary policy. The speculation helped to send the dollar to its lowest level in nearly eight months against the yen, while the euro notched a fresh three-month high against the greenback.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday morning, the euro was at $1.3241 from $1.3172 late Monday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y85.75 from Y86.51, while the euro was at Y113.55 from Y113.87. The U.K. pound was at $1.5948 from $1.5889. The dollar was at CHF1.0364 from CHF1.0399.\par \par \uc2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at 80.523 from 80.930.\par \par \uc2 U.S. consumer spending was flat in June as incomes stopped growing and prices remained subdued, pointing to a weaker economy.\par \par \uc2 "It's more bad news for the dollar," said Brian Dolan, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .com in Bedminster, N.J. "It's just another indication that U.S. consumers continue to retrench, highlighting the unfolding slowdown in the U.S. recovery," he said.\par \par \uc2 Consumer spending, a key driver of economic growth, was unchanged last month after growing a revised 0.1% in May, the Commerce Department said in its monthly report Tuesday. The May figure was originally reported as a 0.2% rise.\par \par \uc2 Personal incomes were also flat in June as private wages and salaries fell. Incomes in May rose by 0.3%, slightly less than what the government had previously reported.\par \par \uc2 Economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires had expected to see a 0.1% increase in spending and a 0.2% rise in income for June. Some analysts said they expected\par \par \uc2 The dollar earlier fell to a near-eight month low against the yen on the discussion the Fed could consider a change in the management of its massive securities portfolio, as well as comments from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister that suggested \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets was not on the table for now, even as the strong yen puts pressure on Japanese exporters.\par \par \uc2 Japan's finance minister said Tuesday he will in principle let market forces set the yen's exchange rate, suggesting the government isn't considering action to curb the yen's renewed strength to defend the nation's slowing export-driven economy.\par \par \uc2 "Regarding how to respond to (recent yen rises), our fundamental stance is that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates are something that should basically be set by the market," Noda said during a session of the Lower House's fiscal and financial committee.\par \par \uc2 Although Noda reiterated his distaste for excessively volatile exchange rates and the government's intention to head off a too-strong a yen, he declined as usual to say whether he has any plans to order yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal article reported discussion within the Fed centered on whether to use cash the Fed receives when its mortgage-bond holdings mature to buy new mortgage or Treasury bonds, instead of allowing its portfolio to shrink gradually, as it is expected to do in the months ahead. Any change--only four months after the Fed ended its massive bond-buying program--would signal deepening concern about the economic outlook. If the Fed's forecast deteriorates significantly, it could also be a precursor to bigger efforts to pump money into the economy.\par \par \uc2 Moving to stop the Fed's portfolio from shrinking would prevent monetary policy from slightly tightening in the face of a weakening recovery.\par \par \uc2 Separately, Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as expected left interest rates on hold at 4.5% for the third month in a row in August, signaling it remains vigilant for inflation in the recovering economy, but probably won't have to act again for some months, perhaps even until next year.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar, which was hobbled slightly also by weaker-than-expected domestic data, declined about 0.10% against the dollar by early New York trading.\par \par \uc2 -By Bradley Davis, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2654;\par \par \uc2 bradley.davis@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Jon Hilsenrath, Takashi Nakamichi in Tokyo and James Glynn in Sydney contributed to this article.) [ 08-03-10 0853ET ]\par \par \uc2 1033 1074 1158 1863 1865 20714 28038 4164   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100803e6830008g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
