\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Key political risks to watch in Japan\b0\par\par\uc2 By Linda Sieg   \par \uc2 2,210 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 August 2010\par 15:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Aug 2 (Reuters) - Japan's government has vowed to cap new bond issuance and spending for the year from next April, but meeting the target will be tough given rising social security costs and wounded political leadership after the ruling Democratic Party was thrashed in a July upper house election.\par \par \uc2 Japan's 5-year sovereign credit default swap spread has tightened to about 70 basis points from almost 100 bps in June as worries about a global slowdown fueled demand for Japanese government bonds and as European sovereign CDS spreads have tightened after bank stress tests. Following is a summary of key political risks to watch:\par \par \uc2 * FISCAL DILEMMA\par \par \uc2 Government ministries will submit by the end of the month their initial budget requests for the fiscal year starting next April.\par \par \uc2 The government has already decided to keep spending excluding debt-servicing costs at 71 trillion yen and cap new bond issuance at around 44 trillion yen, both the same as this year. It also plans to earmark 1 trillion yen or more to fund campaign pledges and policies in the government's growth strategy.\par \par \uc2 But some investors expect new bond issuance to exceed the cap by a few trillion yen or more, partly because welfare costs of an ageing population rise around 1.25 trillion yen a year. Prime Minister Naoto Kan has yet to present a plan to lower the burden. Spending cuts elsewhere are unlikely to make up the difference.\par \par \uc2 Economists say raising the 5 percent sales tax, perhaps to as high as 20 percent, is crucial. Kan made doubling the sales tax the focus of an upper house election last month, but the Democrats suffered a stinging defeat partly because he failed to send a coherent message on how the extra revenue would be used and how the government's policies would maintain economic growth.\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- Early indications of the size of each ministry's budget request. The Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry is of interest because their bureaucrats compiled much of the growth strategy. The health, transport and education ministries are also known big spenders.\par \par \uc2 -- Whether cabinet ministers will explicitly support scaling back some of the Democrats' spending plans once they see how tough it will be to draft initial budget requests.\par \par \uc2 -- Comments or downgrades by ratings agencies. \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  has already warned it could cut Japan's sovereign rating due to political gridlock.\par \par \uc2 * POLICY TENSION WITH \b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 The small opposition Your Party, which made strides in the July election, wants to submit a bill to revise the Bank of Japan law so the government and the BOJ set a shared inflation target of 2 percent.\par \par \uc2 There are also rumblings among some of the Democrats' rank and file MPs that the BOJ should do more to end deflation and try to weaken the yen.\par \par \uc2 An inflation target alone won't affect Japan's price trend, economists say, but Your Party's agenda could gain ground among Democrats who want to look proactive on the economy.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ has argued against inflation targeting, saying policy could be swayed by short-term price fluctuations and lead central bankers to overlook other important factors such as asset prices.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ has also offered the government help with a loan scheme to redirect money to industries with growth potential, a move many say is intended more to shield the bank from future government criticism.\par \par \uc2 The risk of Kan's cabinet revising the BOJ law is very low. But senior Democrats could become more prone to lean on the BOJ if the economy stalls temporarily at the end of the year and the country's debt burden rules out expansionary fiscal policy.\par \par \uc2 Though the BOJ can set its policy independently, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is required to work closely with the government to align policy and has in the past caved in to government pressure.\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- Comments by Yoshimi Watanabe, head of the Your Party. Investors will need to see how far Watanabe cooperates with the Democrats in enacting policy and what he might get in return.\par \par \uc2 -- If the economy does weaken at year-end, the BOJ may be more inclined to oblige government pressure in order to prevent debate about revising the BOJ law from gaining traction.\par \par \uc2 * YEN \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 Kan is widely regarded by \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders as favouring a weaker yen, though that view of him has faded somewhat as neither he nor other top government officials have been very vocal in speaking out against the yen's strength.\par \par \uc2 Even so, comments from some lawmakers in the DPJ and other parties whose cooperation is likely to be needed on various policies have kept \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders cautious.\par \par \uc2 Japanese Deputy Finance Minister Motohisa Ikeda said on July 22 he wants to avoid excessive yen rises. Before assuming that post, he had called for a policy of pushing the yen down towards 120 per dollar -- a level it has not traded at since 2007.\par \par \uc2 Watanabe of Your Party, said on July 26 the government needs to weaken the yen and adopt more expansionary monetary policies to beat deflation.\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- Comments by government officials about sudden \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves and possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Picking a level that would trigger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is tricky. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the yen surged beyond its 14-year high of 84.82 per dollar set in November, some analysts say. But \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be hard for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the G7 is encouraging flexibility in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, and especially after China in June vowed more flexibility in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime.\par \par \uc2 -- A more likely way of countering a surge in yen strength could be for the government to pressure the Bank of Japan into taking more easing steps, as it did in December after the yen hit a 14-year high.\par \par \uc2 * DPJ LEADERSHIP RACE, POLITICAL HORSETRADING\par \par \uc2 The ruling DP and its tiny coalition partner lost their upper house majority in the July 11 election after Kan floated the idea of raising the 5 percent sales tax to curb debt and struggled to convince voters he had a plan for fixing Japan's economic ills.\par \par \uc2 The election loss has wounded Kan -- Japan's fifth premier in three years -- ahead of a DPJ leadership race in early September, and speculation is simmering that party powerbroker Ichiro Ozawa may mount a challenge, although a funding scandal along with intra-party manoeuvring is clouding the outlook.\par \par \uc2 Another change at the top would be a further blow to Japan's image and clout abroad at a time when China's influence is rising and it is unclear whether replacing Kan would give the DPJ a boost among voters. No general election need be held until 2013.\par \par \uc2 The loss of the coalition's upper house majority means the Democrats, who retain control of the more powerful lower chamber, need opposition support to enact laws, including bills needed to implement the budget for 2011/12.\par \par \uc2 DPJ leaders say they will seek policy-based cooperation with opposition parties including the Buddhist-backed New Komeito, which stresses social welfare, and the Your Party, a proponent of market-friendly steps such as deregulation and more aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action to fight deflation. Cooperation with the main opposition Liberal Democrats on matters such as tax reform is also theoretically possible, since it supports a sales tax rise.\par \par \uc2 But negotiating bill-by-bill deals will be time-consuming and finding a new ally to join the coalition would be even tougher.\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- Comments from Ozawa or lawmakers close to him will help in assessing the likelihood of a challenge to Kan in a DPJ leadership race expected to take place on Sept. 11.\par \par \uc2 -- Opposition party comments about cooperation should also be watched for indications that deals are in the works.\par \par \uc2 * FUTENMA AIRBASE ROW\par \par \uc2 Tokyo and Washington basically agreed in May to implement a 2006 agreement to shift the U.S. Marines' Futenma airbase to the less crowded Henoko area of Japan's southern island of Okinawa, host to about half the U.S. troops in the country.\par \par \uc2 But the outlook for implementing the deal is cloudy after then-prime minister Yukio Hatoyama first raised and then dashed hopes of moving the base off Okinawa entirely, angering local residents, who associate U.S. bases with noise, pollution and crime. Hatoyama resigned abruptly in June after his perceived mishandling of the issue sharply eroded his voter ratings.\par \par \uc2 The dispute over the airbase has distracted the two security allies and could dent the 50-year-old alliance as they try to cope with an unpredictable North Korea and a rising China.\par \par \uc2 Doubts persist over how much progress can be made ahead of U.S. President Barack Obama's visit to Japan for a Nov 13-14 Asia-Pacific leaders' summit.\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- Whether U.S. and Japanese experts can work out details, such as the location and construction method of the relocation site for Futenma, by the end of August.\par \par \uc2 -- A September local assembly election in Nago, the site of the proposed relocation facility, will likely reflect the degree of local opposition, while a Nov. 28 election for governor of Okinawa could affect the plans since the governor's approval is needed if building the new base involves a maritime landfill. (Additional reporting by Yoko Kubota, Rika Otsuka and Stanley White; editing by Andrew Marshall) [20100802 132803 GMT]\par \par \uc2 * DPJ LEADERSHIP RACE, POLITICAL HORSETRADING\par \par \uc2 The ruling DP and its tiny coalition partner lost their upper house majority in the July 11 election after Kan floated the idea of raising the 5 percent sales tax to curb debt and struggled to convince voters he had a plan for fixing Japan's economic ills.\par \par \uc2 The election loss has wounded Kan -- Japan's fifth premier in three years -- ahead of a DPJ leadership race in early September, and speculation is simmering that party powerbroker Ichiro Ozawa may mount a challenge, although a funding scandal along with intra-party manoeuvring is clouding the outlook.\par \par \uc2 Another change at the top would be a further blow to Japan's image and clout abroad at a time when China's influence is rising and it is unclear whether replacing Kan would give the DPJ a boost among voters. No general election need be held until 2013.\par \par \uc2 The loss of the coalition's upper house majority means the Democrats, who retain control of the more powerful lower chamber, need opposition support to enact laws, including bills needed to implement the budget for 2011/12.\par \par \uc2 DPJ leaders say they will seek policy-based cooperation with opposition parties including the Buddhist-backed New Komeito, which stresses social welfare, and the Your Party, a proponent of market-friendly steps such as deregulation and more aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action to fight deflation. Cooperation with the main opposition Liberal Democrats on matters such as tax reform is also theoretically possible, since it supports a sales tax rise.\par \par \uc2 But negotiating bill-by-bill deals will be time-consuming and finding a new ally to join the coalition would be even tougher.\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- Comments from Ozawa or lawmakers close to him will help in assessing the likelihood of a challenge to Kan in a DPJ leadership race expected to take place on Sept. 11.\par \par \uc2 -- Opposition party comments about cooperation should also be watched for indications that deals are in the works.\par \par \uc2 * FUTENMA AIRBASE ROW\par \par \uc2 Tokyo and Washington basically agreed in May to implement a 2006 agreement to shift the U.S. Marines' Futenma airbase to the less crowded Henoko area of Japan's southern island of Okinawa, host to about half the U.S. troops in the country.\par \par \uc2 But the outlook for implementing the deal is cloudy after then-prime minister Yukio Hatoyama first raised and then dashed hopes of moving the base off Okinawa entirely, angering local residents, who associate U.S. bases with noise, pollution and crime. Hatoyama resigned abruptly in June after his perceived mishandling of the issue sharply eroded his voter ratings.\par \par \uc2 The dispute over the airbase has distracted the two security allies and could dent the 50-year-old alliance as they try to cope with an unpredictable North Korea and a rising China.\par \par \uc2 Doubts persist over how much progress can be made ahead of U.S. President Barack Obama's visit to Japan for a Nov 13-14 Asia-Pacific leaders' summit.\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- Whether U.S. and Japanese experts can work out details, such as the location and construction method of the re