\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Focus: Bank Of Japan Should Leave The Yen Alone\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicholas Hastings   \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN   \par \uc2 832 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 August 2010\par 09:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--What the Bank of Japan needs now is patience.\par \par \uc2 As the Japanese economic recovery stumbles and as the yen rises to a new 2010 high against the dollar, pressure for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to become extreme.\par \par \uc2 But, if the bank waits long enough, the market may well do the job for it and push the yen back down again of its own accord.\par \par \uc2 The big test for the Bank of Japan isn't far away.\par \par \uc2 As the dollar falls towards Y85, Japanese exporters are likely to suffer, putting the Japanese economy at even greater risk and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy under deeper scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 See the dollar's recent slide against the yen:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4340"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4340}}}\par \par \uc2 For diplomatic reasons alone, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would prefer not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The last thing Tokyo wants to do is justify \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation to Washington, where U.S. officials are still pressing the Chinese to let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise. An official from the ruling DJP has already been floating the idea of easing monetary policy rather than employing market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the yen's rise.\par \par \uc2 The problem for Tokyo, though, is that the yen's rally is rapidly gaining momentum.\par \par \uc2 Last week, confidence in the U.S. economic recovery took a hard knock, with even one of the more hawkish governors of the Federal Reserve, James Bullard, warning that the risks of deflation have risen in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 This coincided with more bad news from Japan, showing that the recovery is even more reliant on export growth than before. Industrial production fell in June, while unemployment rose and consumer prices fell for the sixteenth month in a row.\par \par \uc2 The net result was a sharp drop in U.S. Treasury yields and a further narrowing of the yield spread with Japanese government bonds.\par \par \uc2 Given the recent renewed appetite for carry trades and an increase in the correlation between yield spreads and the dollar/yen's performance to nearly 95%, the dollar's fall has picked up momentum against the yen.\par \par \uc2 A similar rise in the yen against the euro means there will be little headwind to stop the move.\par \par \uc2 So while the dollar might hesitate if it falls to Y85, with market players looking to see if the Bank of Japan is going to jump in to stop it, chances are that the dollar could fall as far as Y82, a level last hit in May 1995.\par \par \uc2 It is at that point that the Bank of Japan will have to show its mettle.\par \par \uc2 Despite all indications, the dollar is unlikely to slip into a free fall.\par \par \uc2 First, the current bearish outlook for the U.S. economy could well prove overdone and yield spreads could soon bounce back in favor of the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Also, the mere threat of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains a powerful weapon that could convince many players to take long dollar positions as the yen falls on the assumption that they want to be on the side of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Take into account the fact that the skew in dollar/yen risk reversals still isn't stretched and there is even is less reason for the Bank of Japan to move.\par \par \uc2 So, even though the yen's rise may be nasty and the political fallout may be messy, the Bank of Japan will probably find that if it keeps its nerve and just waits the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will fall into reverse with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  never having to employ its ultimate weapon for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  control.\par \par \uc2 Early Monday in Europe, the yen was easing back a little as strong Asian stock markets and relief that China's purchasing managers' index for manufacturing didn't fall under 50, indicating economic contraction, helped to lift risk sentiment.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was able to rise to Y86.61 by 0645 GMT from Y86.41 late on Friday in New York, according to EBS.\par \par \uc2 The euro was also up at Y113.22 from Y112.76 and at $1.3070 from $1.3053.\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg TNI FRX POV\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Reuters   USD/DJ \par    Thomson   P/1066 or P/1074 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Nick Hastings has covered the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets and industry for over 20 years. Apart from his written commentary and analysis, he also appears on Fox Business News and CNBC television in Europe, Asia and the U.S. He can be contacted on +44-20-7842-9493 or by email: nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkBackEurope@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 02-08-10 0719GMT ]\f28 \par \par 