\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Thanachart Tips SET Index To Hit 1,000 In 2011\b0\par\par\uc2 1,572 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juli 2010\par 08:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0603 GMT [Dow Jones] Thailand's SET Index forecast to reach 1,000 points in 2011 on back of economic growth, expected fund inflows following trend of strengthening local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says \uc2 Thanachart Securities\uc2 . "We don't believe in a super-strong earnings growth scenario for Thailand, but rather a more sustainable one...There's also a clear case for sustained high dividend yield," house says. Notes, as majority of foreign funds don't rate Thailand at Overweight, downside risk of further share unloading reduced. "At the same time, it offers significant upside if foreign funds want to increase their weightings in the Thai market." Adds, political risk remains, but market increasingly neutral to local Thai political uncertainty. SET Index last +0.1% at 855.20 at midday break. Benchmark has risen 14% since end of May, when political unrest ended with casualties, loss of business opportunities. SET Index last topped 1,000 points in 1996. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0600 GMT [Dow Jones] Direct impact from Japan FSA's new regulation on margin \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trade's leverage should be limited, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Masafumi Yamamoto. Starting Monday, anyone who trade with leverage of more than 50X their margin money must unwind positions, prompting market speculation that unwinding flows--mostly JPY-buying--could cause yen to rise sharply. But Yamamoto says, "traders who will be affected by the new regulation are likely to be very short-term oriented, intraday traders," meaning there won't be very many outstanding positions that must be squared. Pair last at 86.46, dealers tip today's support at 85.00. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0555 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW trading lower at 1,183.8 vs 1,186.4 late yesterday in Seoul, but off an earlier low of 1,182.1 on suspected USD-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , 3 Seoul-based traders say. Local bank trader doesn't expect pair to move sharply either way from current levels. "Exporters are continuously selling, but players will need to trim their (USD-short) positions," ahead of weekend, which will likely provide some support, says another trader. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0555 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares down 0.3% at 3086.669 in moderate volume, as investors take profits in bank, automotive and consumer related stocks after recent rally, traders say; tip support at 3070. Trader at Valbury Securities says losses in most regional markets also hurting sentiment. Among decliners: car maker Astra (ASII.JK) down 1.7% at IDR51,850, Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK) down 1.0% at IDR9,950 and food maker \uc2 Indofood\uc2  (INDF.JK) down 1.1% at IDR4,675. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0552 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan June housing starts +0.6% on year at 68,688, rising for first time in 2 months but below +1.5% forecast in Dow Jones, Nikkei poll of 24 economists. Housing starts fell 4.6% in May. Demand for condominium apartments, individual homes may increase, thanks to government's incentive program, recovery in employment, income situation, analysts say. But slight rise in June shouldn't obscure longer-term difficulties Japan's housing market faces, due in part to aging of population, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  chief Japan economist Kyohei Morita. As increasing proportion of Japan's population elderly, fewer younger buyers means housing construction may sag. That would overwhelm any gains aging society brings to residential, rental homes, Morita says. (miho.nakauchi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0549 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end down 0.5% at 7760.63, erasing most of this week's gains on losses in U.S., Asian markets, says Taiwan International Securities analyst Hanna Chang. Adds, "most (Taiwanese) companies that held investor conferences this week except HTC provided a conservative outlook over the third quarter. It could be a sign of an economic slowdown in the near future," she says. Tips Taiex in 7600-7900 band next week, with heavy upside cap at 7800. TSMC (2330.TW) drops 1.0% at NT$62.40 as investors take profit after company announced 2Q results late Thursday; 2Q net profit +65% on-year at record NT$40.28 billion but stock +4.0% month to yesterday, suggesting strong results may have been priced in. HTC (2498.TW) +3.2% at NT$588.00, highest close since September 2008, extending yesterday's 6.9% gain, after handset maker says late Thursday it expects 3Q revenue to more than double to record of about NT$70 billion. MediaTek (2454.TW) +1.5% at NT$433.50, ASE (2311.TW) down 1.2% at NT$25.15 ahead of their 2Q investor conferences. Taiex +5.9% month-to-date. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0548 GMT [Dow Jones] Switzerland's July PMI indicator is expected to come in at 64.7, an average of three economist studies compiled by DJN shows. The index, which has held above the critical expansion level of 50 for the past 10 months, is expected to slip from near its all-time high reached in May as uncertainties over the global economic outlook and the appreciation of the Swiss franc cloud the outlook for Switzerland. The data are due Monday, Aug. 2 at 0730 GMT. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0547 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume down 16.2% at HK$1.122 billion midday, only 3.7% of total market volume, vs 4.2% at Thursday's half-day, indicating view HSI's 0.5% pullback merely normal consolidation following 8-day winning streak. No stocks manage more than HK$100 million worth of shorts; Tencent (0700.HK) leads with HK$89.52 million, followed by Agricultural Bank of China (1288.HK) with HK$63.01 million, China Mobile (0941.HK) with HK$39.77 million. HK & China Gas (0003.HK), A50 China Tracker (2823.HK) round out top 5, with HK$37.12 million, HK$36.09 million respectively. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0545 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi last down 0.6% at 1760.28 on continued institutional, retail selling, despite foreign buying. "Despite series of domestic news including some big corporate earnings and other events, local investors are pocketing profits as the earnings were mostly inline with expectations," says MiraeAsset Securities' Lee Jin-woo. Analysts say investors' sentiment remains "sweet" with foreigners still buying, index may extend rise after release of some economic indicators such as 2Q GDP data in U.S. tonight, July trade data in Korea Sunday. Tip benchmark Kospi to move in 1750-1780 band next week before heading for 1800 level. \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) down 1.8% at KRW812,000 on profit-taking after posting record 2Q net profit but \uc2 Kia Motors\uc2  (000270.SE) up 0.8% at KRW30,450 following strong 2Q. \uc2 Woori Finance\uc2  (053000.SE) down 2.3% at KRW14,950 after government unveils privatization plan; but market concerned process will be prolonged given Woori's massive size. (jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0542 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds end up with key 5-year bond yield lower at 0.9231% vs 0.9404 yesterday as brokerage houses buy, says local bank trader. Thinks most active 5-year yield may test 0.9000% next Monday as "ample liquidity makes banks and investors desperate to find ways to invest," he says. Adds, Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans to mop up excess liquidity, but it is likely to do it in slow, gradual rate. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0541 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +0.1% at 855.20 at midday pause in active trade. Benchmark in tight range in consolidate mode. Gains led by firmer telecommunication stocks trimmed by profit-taking in banks, property plays, whose rises in earlier sessions fueled by positive outlook on upward revision to 2010 GDP, says \uc2 Thanachart Securities\uc2 ' Senior VP Pichai Lertsupongkit. Adds eyes on U.S. GDP release; 850-860 range tipped. Among top actives, \uc2 True Corp\uc2  (TRUE.TH) +1.9% at THB4.36, \uc2 Total Access Communication\uc2  (DTAC.TH) +2.8% at THB46.25, \uc2 Advanced Info Service\uc2  (ADVANC.TH) +0.5% at THB93, \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2  (SCB.TH) down 1.7% at THB88.75, \uc2 Kasikornbank\uc2  (KBANK.TH) down 2.9% at THB100.50. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0538 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead JGB September futures up 0.13 at 141.85 as JPY marking 8-month high vs USD drags on Nikkei, last down 1.3%; if JPY strength persists next week, "that will continue to support the JGB market," as many Japanese investors believe current JPY strength, while hurting share markets, not yet severe enough to trigger government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says RuiXue Xu, debt markets strategist at RBS. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Finance Minister Noda, asked about JPY strength earlier, repeats standard line that he's "watching the markets." Xu says comments underscore speculation that any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would require sharper JPY gains. "Because the dollar is still around Y86, we don't think the government or Bank of Japan will make any moves at current levels;" adds that any steps would only seem more likely if USD/JPY "falls to Y84 or Y85." USD/JPY last 86.44.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com) [ 07-30-10 0203ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100730e67u0003w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
