ld investor conferences this week except HTC provided a conservative outlook over the third quarter. It could be a sign of an economic slowdown in the near future," she says. Tips Taiex in 7600-7900 band next week, with heavy upside cap at 7800. TSMC (2330.TW) drops 1.0% at NT$62.40 as investors take profit after company announced 2Q results late Thursday; 2Q net profit +65% on-year at record NT$40.28 billion but stock +4.0% month to yesterday, suggesting strong results may have been priced in. HTC (2498.TW) +3.2% at NT$588.00, highest close since September 2008, extending yesterday's 6.9% gain, after handset maker says late Thursday it expects 3Q revenue to more than double to record of about NT$70 billion. MediaTek (2454.TW) +1.5% at NT$433.50, ASE (2311.TW) down 1.2% at NT$25.15 ahead of their 2Q investor conferences. Taiex +5.9% month-to-date. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0548 GMT [Dow Jones] Switzerland's July PMI indicator is expected to come in at 64.7, an average of three economist studies compiled by DJN shows. The index, which has held above the critical expansion level of 50 for the past 10 months, is expected to slip from near its all-time high reached in May as uncertainties over the global economic outlook and the appreciation of the Swiss franc cloud the outlook for Switzerland. The data are due Monday, Aug. 2 at 0730 GMT. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0547 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume down 16.2% at HK$1.122 billion midday, only 3.7% of total market volume, vs 4.2% at Thursday's half-day, indicating view HSI's 0.5% pullback merely normal consolidation following 8-day winning streak. No stocks manage more than HK$100 million worth of shorts; Tencent (0700.HK) leads with HK$89.52 million, followed by Agricultural Bank of China (1288.HK) with HK$63.01 million, China Mobile (0941.HK) with HK$39.77 million. HK & China Gas (0003.HK), A50 China Tracker (2823.HK) round out top 5, with HK$37.12 million, HK$36.09 million respectively. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0545 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi last down 0.6% at 1760.28 on continued institutional, retail selling, despite foreign buying. "Despite series of domestic news including some big corporate earnings and other events, local investors are pocketing profits as the earnings were mostly inline with expectations," says MiraeAsset Securities' Lee Jin-woo. Analysts say investors' sentiment remains "sweet" with foreigners still buying, index may extend rise after release of some economic indicators such as 2Q GDP data in U.S. tonight, July trade data in Korea Sunday. Tip benchmark Kospi to move in 1750-1780 band next week before heading for 1800 level. \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) down 1.8% at KRW812,000 on profit-taking after posting record 2Q net profit but \uc2 Kia Motors\uc2  (000270.SE) up 0.8% at KRW30,450 following strong 2Q. \uc2 Woori Finance\uc2  (053000.SE) down 2.3% at KRW14,950 after government unveils privatization plan; but market concerned process will be prolonged given Woori's massive size. (jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0542 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds end up with key 5-year bond yield lower at 0.9231% vs 0.9404 yesterday as brokerage houses buy, says local bank trader. Thinks most active 5-year yield may test 0.9000% next Monday as "ample liquidity makes banks and investors desperate to find ways to invest," he says. Adds, Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans to mop up excess liquidity, but it is likely to do it in slow, gradual rate. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0541 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +0.1% at 855.20 at midday pause in active trade. Benchmark in tight range in consolidate mode. Gains led by firmer telecommunication stocks trimmed by profit-taking in banks, property plays, whose rises in earlier sessions fueled by positive outlook on upward revision to 2010 GDP, says \uc2 Thanachart Securities\uc2 ' Senior VP Pichai Lertsupongkit. Adds eyes on U.S. GDP release; 850-860 range tipped. Among top actives, \uc2 True Corp\uc2  (TRUE.TH) +1.9% at THB4.36, \uc2 Total Access Communication\uc2  (DTAC.TH) +2.8% at THB46.25, \uc2 Advanced Info Service\uc2  (ADVANC.TH) +0.5% at THB93, \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2  (SCB.TH) down 1.7% at THB88.75, \uc2 Kasikornbank\uc2  (KBANK.TH) down 2.9% at THB100.50. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0538 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead JGB September futures up 0.13 at 141.85 as JPY marking 8-month high vs USD drags on Nikkei, last down 1.3%; if JPY strength persists next week, "that will continue to support the JGB market," as many Japanese investors believe current JPY strength, while hurting share markets, not yet severe enough to trigger government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says RuiXue Xu, debt markets strategist at RBS. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Finance Minister Noda, asked about JPY strength earlier, repeats standard line that he's "watching the markets." Xu says comments underscore speculation that any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would require sharper JPY gains. "Because the dollar is still around Y86, we don't think the government or Bank of Japan will make any moves at current levels;" adds that any steps would only seem more likely if USD/JPY "falls to Y84 or Y85." USD/JPY last 86.44.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0531 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei 225 September futures down 1.3% at 9560 after USD/JPY hits 8-month low (now 86.39); persistent concerns about U.S. economy also strong ahead of 2Q GDP release later today and key July jobs report due next week. "It's very hard to project the outlook for Japanese firms until the yen stops rising (on U.S. economic worries)," says Hideki Horikawa, chief analyst at Himawari Securities. Puts futures range for next week at 9200-9800. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0530 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold could test $1,170 at London open says Singapore-based trader at international bank. "There's some buying coming in and it looks like we've been building a small base around $1,157 in the past couple of sessions," he says. However, says busy day of data in U.S., Europe likely to decide direction. Strong support lies at $1,149, 200-day moving average and that has to hold to prevent extended sell-off. Spot gold at $1,167.90/oz, up $1.40 since NY close.(james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0529 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Westpac\uc2  more bearish on NZD/USD after RBNZ yesterday "provided surprisingly downbeat guidance on the projected cash rate trajectory." Notes, NZ rate markets have pushed 2-year swap yields around 25 bps lower, lingering interest from accrual-seekers should keep yields depressed over at least next week. "Over a multi-month horizon, we continue to believe the NZD will outperform other risk \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly the AUD, but shorter term, there is room for the NZD to weaken further in line with NZ yields." Given volatility of global risk appetite, bank prefers to sell NZD/USD on bounces, will go short at 0.7300 with stop-loss at 0.7420, take-profit target of 0.7000. Pair last at 0.7211. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com) [ 07-30-10 0155ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100730e67u0003t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
