 vs USD drags on Nikkei, last down 1.3%; if JPY strength persists next week, "that will continue to support the JGB market," as many Japanese investors believe current JPY strength, while hurting share markets, not yet severe enough to trigger government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says RuiXue Xu, debt markets strategist at RBS. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Finance Minister Noda, asked about JPY strength earlier, repeats standard line that he's "watching the markets." Xu says comments underscore speculation that any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would require sharper JPY gains. "Because the dollar is still around Y86, we don't think the government or Bank of Japan will make any moves at current levels;" adds that any steps would only seem more likely if USD/JPY "falls to Y84 or Y85." USD/JPY last 86.44.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0531 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei 225 September futures down 1.3% at 9560 after USD/JPY hits 8-month low (now 86.39); persistent concerns about U.S. economy also strong ahead of 2Q GDP release later today and key July jobs report due next week. "It's very hard to project the outlook for Japanese firms until the yen stops rising (on U.S. economic worries)," says Hideki Horikawa, chief analyst at Himawari Securities. Puts futures range for next week at 9200-9800. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0530 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold could test $1,170 at London open says Singapore-based trader at international bank. "There's some buying coming in and it looks like we've been building a small base around $1,157 in the past couple of sessions," he says. However, says busy day of data in U.S., Europe likely to decide direction. Strong support lies at $1,149, 200-day moving average and that has to hold to prevent extended sell-off. Spot gold at $1,167.90/oz, up $1.40 since NY close.(james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0529 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Westpac\uc2  more bearish on NZD/USD after RBNZ yesterday "provided surprisingly downbeat guidance on the projected cash rate trajectory." Notes, NZ rate markets have pushed 2-year swap yields around 25 bps lower, lingering interest from accrual-seekers should keep yields depressed over at least next week. "Over a multi-month horizon, we continue to believe the NZD will outperform other risk \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly the AUD, but shorter term, there is room for the NZD to weaken further in line with NZ yields." Given volatility of global risk appetite, bank prefers to sell NZD/USD on bounces, will go short at 0.7300 with stop-loss at 0.7420, take-profit target of 0.7000. Pair last at 0.7211. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0512 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan's Finance Minister Noda reminds investors he's watching markets closely after USD/JPY hits 8-month low of 86.25 on EBS, but tone, content of his remarks suggest nation's chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policymaker doesn't see need for imminent JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Noda's latest remarks no different from comments made earlier today when pair hovering around 86.60; last 86.37. "The chances of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  appear to be slim," says \uc2 Daiwa Institute of Research\uc2  chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst Yuji Kameoka. "We can't call the yen's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise as excessively sharp. It's rather gradual," so hard to evoke G-7 stance against excessive volatility, he says. Rapid falls below key 80.00 mark may be needed for Japan to justify action, he says. Moreover, "What we're seeing isn't broad yen strength but broad dollar weakness due to the U.S. economy's relative weakness. Even if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in this circumstance, it may prove ineffective," Kameoka says. (takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com ) [ 07-30-10 015