\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Data Preview: Swiss July PMI Seen Easing To 64.7\b0\par\par\uc2 1,492 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juli 2010\par 07:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0548 GMT [Dow Jones] Switzerland's July PMI indicator is expected to come in at 64.7, an average of three economist studies compiled by DJN shows. The index, which has held above the critical expansion level of 50 for the past 10 months, is expected to slip from near its all-time high reached in May as uncertainties over the global economic outlook and the appreciation of the Swiss franc cloud the outlook for Switzerland. The data are due Monday, Aug. 2 at 0730 GMT. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0547 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume down 16.2% at HK$1.122 billion midday, only 3.7% of total market volume, vs 4.2% at Thursday's half-day, indicating view HSI's 0.5% pullback merely normal consolidation following 8-day winning streak. No stocks manage more than HK$100 million worth of shorts; Tencent (0700.HK) leads with HK$89.52 million, followed by Agricultural Bank of China (1288.HK) with HK$63.01 million, China Mobile (0941.HK) with HK$39.77 million. HK & China Gas (0003.HK), A50 China Tracker (2823.HK) round out top 5, with HK$37.12 million, HK$36.09 million respectively. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0545 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi last down 0.6% at 1760.28 on continued institutional, retail selling, despite foreign buying. "Despite series of domestic news including some big corporate earnings and other events, local investors are pocketing profits as the earnings were mostly inline with expectations," says MiraeAsset Securities' Lee Jin-woo. Analysts say investors' sentiment remains "sweet" with foreigners still buying, index may extend rise after release of some economic indicators such as 2Q GDP data in U.S. tonight, July trade data in Korea Sunday. Tip benchmark Kospi to move in 1750-1780 band next week before heading for 1800 level. \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) down 1.8% at KRW812,000 on profit-taking after posting record 2Q net profit but \uc2 Kia Motors\uc2  (000270.SE) up 0.8% at KRW30,450 following strong 2Q. \uc2 Woori Finance\uc2  (053000.SE) down 2.3% at KRW14,950 after government unveils privatization plan; but market concerned process will be prolonged given Woori's massive size. (jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0542 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds end up with key 5-year bond yield lower at 0.9231% vs 0.9404 yesterday as brokerage houses buy, says local bank trader. Thinks most active 5-year yield may test 0.9000% next Monday as "ample liquidity makes banks and investors desperate to find ways to invest," he says. Adds, Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans to mop up excess liquidity, but it is likely to do it in slow, gradual rate. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0541 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +0.1% at 855.20 at midday pause in active trade. Benchmark in tight range in consolidate mode. Gains led by firmer telecommunication stocks trimmed by profit-taking in banks, property plays, whose rises in earlier sessions fueled by positive outlook on upward revision to 2010 GDP, says \uc2 Thanachart Securities\uc2 ' Senior VP Pichai Lertsupongkit. Adds eyes on U.S. GDP release; 850-860 range tipped. Among top actives, \uc2 True Corp\uc2  (TRUE.TH) +1.9% at THB4.36, \uc2 Total Access Communication\uc2  (DTAC.TH) +2.8% at THB46.25, \uc2 Advanced Info Service\uc2  (ADVANC.TH) +0.5% at THB93, \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2  (SCB.TH) down 1.7% at THB88.75, \uc2 Kasikornbank\uc2  (KBANK.TH) down 2.9% at THB100.50. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0538 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead JGB September futures up 0.13 at 141.8