\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Taiwan Bonds End Up; 5-Yr Yld May Test 0.90% Mon\b0\par\par\uc2 1,543 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juli 2010\par 07:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0542 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds end up with key 5-year bond yield lower at 0.9231% vs 0.9404 yesterday as brokerage houses buy, says local bank trader. Thinks most active 5-year yield may test 0.9000% next Monday as "ample liquidity makes banks and investors desperate to find ways to invest," he says. Adds, Taiwan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  plans to mop up excess liquidity, but it is likely to do it in slow, gradual rate. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0541 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +0.1% at 855.20 at midday pause in active trade. Benchmark in tight range in consolidate mode. Gains led by firmer telecommunication stocks trimmed by profit-taking in banks, property plays, whose rises in earlier sessions fueled by positive outlook on upward revision to 2010 GDP, says \uc2 Thanachart Securities\uc2 ' Senior VP Pichai Lertsupongkit. Adds eyes on U.S. GDP release; 850-860 range tipped. Among top actives, \uc2 True Corp\uc2  (TRUE.TH) +1.9% at THB4.36, \uc2 Total Access Communication\uc2  (DTAC.TH) +2.8% at THB46.25, \uc2 Advanced Info Service\uc2  (ADVANC.TH) +0.5% at THB93, \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2  (SCB.TH) down 1.7% at THB88.75, \uc2 Kasikornbank\uc2  (KBANK.TH) down 2.9% at THB100.50. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0538 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead JGB September futures up 0.13 at 141.85 as JPY marking 8-month high vs USD drags on Nikkei, last down 1.3%; if JPY strength persists next week, "that will continue to support the JGB market," as many Japanese investors believe current JPY strength, while hurting share markets, not yet severe enough to trigger government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says RuiXue Xu, debt markets strategist at RBS. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Finance Minister Noda, asked about JPY strength earlier, repeats standard line that he's "watching the markets." Xu says comments underscore speculation that any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would require sharper JPY gains. "Because the dollar is still around Y86, we don't think the government or Bank of Japan will make any moves at current levels;" adds that any steps would only seem more likely if USD/JPY "falls to Y84 or Y85." USD/JPY last 86.44.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0531 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei 225 September futures down 1.3% at 9560 after USD/JPY hits 8-month low (now 86.39); persistent concerns about U.S. economy also strong ahead of 2Q GDP release later today and key July jobs report due next week. "It's very hard to project the outlook for Japanese firms until the yen stops rising (on U.S. economic worries)," says Hideki Horikawa, chief analyst at Himawari Securities. Puts futures range for next week at 9200-9800. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0530 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold could test $1,170 at London open says Singapore-based trader at international bank. "There's some buying coming in and it looks like we've been building a small base around $1,157 in the past couple of sessions," he says. However, says busy day of data in U.S., Europe likely to decide direction. Strong support lies at $1,149, 200-day moving average and that has to hold to prevent extended sell-off. Spot gold at $1,167.90/oz, up $1.40 since NY close.(james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0529 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Westpac\uc2  more bearish on NZD/USD after RBNZ yesterday "provided surprisingly downbeat guidance on the projected cash rate trajectory." Notes, NZ rate markets have pushed 2-year swap yields around 25 bps lower, lingering interest from accrual-seekers should keep yields depressed over at least next week. "Over a multi-month horizon, we continue to believe the NZD will outperform other risk \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly the AUD, but shorter term, there is room for the NZD to weaken further in line with NZ yields." Given volatility of global risk appetite, bank prefers to sell NZD/USD on bounces, will go short at 0.7300 with stop-loss at 0.7420, take-profit target of 0.7000. Pair last at 0.7211. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0512 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan's Finance Minister Noda reminds investors he's watching markets closely after USD/JPY hits 8-month low of 86.25 on EBS, but tone, content of his remarks suggest nation's chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policymaker doesn't see need for imminent JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Noda's latest remarks no different from comments made earlier today when pair hovering around 86.60; last 86.37. "The chances of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  appear to be slim," says \uc2 Daiwa Institute of Research\uc2  chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst Yuji Kameoka. "We can't call the yen's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise as excessively sharp. It's rather gradual," so hard to evoke G-7 stance against excessive volatility, he says. Rapid falls below key 80.00 mark may be needed for Japan to justify action, he says. Moreover, "What we're seeing isn't broad yen strength but broad dollar weakness due to the U.S. economy's relative weakness. Even if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in this circumstance, it may prove ineffective," Kameoka says. (takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com )\par \par \uc2 0449 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD still suffering after RBNZ Governor Bollard's comments yesterday that "pace and extent of further" rate hikes likely more moderate than projected in June, says \uc2 HIFX\uc2  Director of Trading Mike Hollows. Pair last 0.7218. But notes EUR/USD, AUD/USD are still very strong and "Kiwi is going to struggle to fall against USD in that environment." Says eyes now on U.S. 2Q GDP due later in global trading day. Tips support to hold at 0.7175, resistance at 0.7250. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0449 GMT [Dow Jones] Base metals lower with copper hurt by some selling out of China as Shanghai copper remains at discount to equivalent LME contract. Trader in Hong Kong says same pattern as yesterday; "we sell it down a bit here before they pay it back up as soon as London comes in," he says. Adds, outlook for later in the session not clear, likely to depend on reaction to U.S. 2Q advance GDP estimate due 1230 GMT (tipped +2.5% vs 1Q's +2.7%) as well as bevy of euro-zone data including unemployment and flash inflation estimate at 0900 GMT. LME 3-month copper at $7,209.50/oz, down $20.50 with 1,770 lots done. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0439 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore stocks lower at break, tracking pullback in other Asian markets as investors await more data -- including U.S. 2Q GDP later in global day -- for more indications on state of global economy. STI off 0.5% at 2983.40 vs early high of 3018.26 (+0.7%), likely to hold above 2950 rest of session. "Being the end of the week, we have to expect that there are some traders (who) will be closing their positions and booking their profits," says SIAS Research technical analyst Edmund Seow. Market breadth at 2 decliners for every gainer, with 806.7 million shares worth S$1.37 billion changing hands. Good mix of large, small caps dominating trade, including Genting Singapore (G13.SG), flat at S$1.27, \uc2 Berlian Laju Tanker\uc2  (B66.SG), +12.5% at S$0.045, \uc2 Golden Agri-Resources\uc2  (E5H.SG), off 0.9% at S$0.565, \uc2 SingTel\uc2  (Z74.SG), off 0.3% at S$3.10, \uc2 Kreuz Holdings\uc2  (5RK.SG), +3.0% at S$0.34. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0438 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI down 0.5% at 20,982.07 midday, pulling back after 8-day winning streak; volume modest at HK$30.05 billion. Taifook Securities says "a consolidation of the broader market is underway," tips rotational buying into undervalued plays, quality second-liners likely. Most blue chips lower but majority falling less than 1.0%, suggesting profit-taking pressure not terribly heavy. Chalco (2600.HK) bucks trend, +2.7% at HK$6.89 on business diversification into other metals; port operators outperforming perhaps benefiting from rotational buying: China Merchants (0144.HK) +1.8% at HK$29.00, Cosco Pacific (1199.HK) +1.6% at HK$10.46. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0437 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI down 0.1% at 1357.38, off earlier intraday high of 1360.31, a fresh 29-month high. Dealers say in absence of fresh leads, investors taking profits ahead of the weekend after market's 5-day winning streak; but market expected to resume uptrend next week as spate of corporate M&A activity recently continues to buoy investor interest. OSK Research says based on technical indicators, KLCI yet to reach overbought levels, "which suggests that room is still open for additional gains." Pegs strong resistance at 1395. Among decliners, AirAsia (5099.KU) down 2.6% at MYR1.49, CIMB (1023.KU) down 0.8% at MYR7.39; among gainers, Genting Malaysia (4715.KU) +1.4% at MYR2.80. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com) [ 07-30-10 0142ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100730e67u0003n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
