\par\par \b \uc2 WSJA(7/29) Strong Yen To Dent Profits\b0\par\par\uc2 936 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juli 2010\par 19:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par 1\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL ASIA) \par    By Daisuke Wakabayashi and Yoshio Takahashi \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO -- As Japan's exporters report quarterly results and issue annual earnings forecasts in coming days, the stubbornly strong Japanese yen is likely to overshadow signs that global demand is improving and restructuring is boosting profitability.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The yen has already strengthened beyond the trading levels assumed by most Japanese auto and electronics makers. If it holds at current levels or gains even more against the U.S. dollar and the euro, Japan's biggest exporters may have to scale back expectations of an earnings recovery. And if that happens, Japan's economic recovery, which has largely been fueled by rising demand for Japanese products abroad, could be at risk.\par \par \uc2 Both \uc2 Toyota Motor Corp\uc2 . and \uc2 Sony Corp\uc2 . have based earnings estimates for the full year ending in March on the assumption that the dollar will trade at 90 yen and the euro at 125 yen. As of Wednesday afternoon in Tokyo, the dollar was at about 88 yen and the euro was around 114.5 yen. While the U.S. and European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have regained some ground against the yen in recent weeks, analysts say they could fall again on any signs of a slowdown in the global economy.\par \par \uc2 Investors generally turn to yen-denominated assets because they're considered a safe place to park money during global economic uncertainty. In the April-June quarter, the first three months of Japan's financial year, the yen gained significantly against the euro and the greenback as Europe's debt crisis worsened and the global economy showed signs of slowing.\par \par \uc2 The strong yen is problematic for Japanese exporters because it makes their products more expensive overseas and eats into overseas sales sent back to Japan.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, \uc2 Canon Inc\uc2 . offered a glimpse of the type of announcements that may emerge from the spate of export-dependent companies like \uc2 Sony\uc2 , \uc2 Panasonic Corp\uc2 . and \uc2 Nintendo Co\uc2 . The company reported a sharp rise in net profit for the three months ended June 30 on robust demand for digital cameras and printers, helping to spark a rally among Tokyo stocks Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 But Canon kept its 2010 outlook unchanged despite signs of strength. "Had it not been for the strong yen, we would have raised our earnings forecasts," said Canon Executive Vice President \uc2 Toshizo Tanaka\uc2  at a news conference.\par \par \uc2 Canon adjusted its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  assumption for the euro to 110 yen from an earlier 125 yen. It kept its U.S. dollar assumption unchanged at 90 yen. Unlike most Japanese companies, which have fiscal years ending in March, Canon's ends in December.\par \par \uc2 When Canon started the year, it said each drop of one yen in the euro's average \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate for the full year would cost the company 6.4 billion yen (about $72.8 million at current rates) in annual sales and 4.6 billion yen in operating profit. Canon officials said the company plans to offset the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's impact with more aggressive cost cuts.\par \par \uc2 The euro's move against the yen has been particularly sharp. During the three months ended June 30, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell 14%, sliding from around 126.30 yen on April 1 to around 108.34 yen on June 30. The euro fell to an 8 1/2-year low against the yen when it dropped to 107.30 yen on June 29.\par \par \uc2 The dollar fell 5.3% against the yen during the same period, dropping from around 93.50 yen at the start of the quarter to around 88.50 yen on June 30. The greenback's low during the period came on May 6, when a sudden plunge in stock prices in the U.S. briefly sent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to about 88 yen.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has continued to surge against the dollar, which has been weighed down by weak U.S. economic indicators and falling Treasury yields. The dollar slid to a 7 1/2-month low of 86.27 yen July 16.\par \par \uc2 This has prompted politicians and business leaders to grumble about the yen's strength. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is in charge of making decisions on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has yet to order the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to step into markets. But the Japanese government hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ward off yen strength since 2004.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, Toshiyuki Shiga, chairman of the Japan Auto Manufacturers Association, urged the government to deal with the yen's strength, saying its persistently strength could sap profits and hurt the country's economy. Mr. Shiga is also the chief operating officer of \uc2 Nissan Motor Co\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mamoru Kato, an auto analyst at Tokai Tokyo Research Center, said auto makers will probably lower their euro assumption levels to somewhere around 110 yen. For Toyota, a one-yen fall in the euro's average rate for the full year would shave five billion yen off its operating profit for the period.\par \par \uc2 Mazda Motor Corp\uc2 . said earlier this month that the weak euro isn't likely to dent its profit in the six months through September, because the company has hedged itself with euro forward contracts at above 125 yen that cover 90% of its first-half \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Mazda\uc2  Chief Financial Officer \uc2 Kiyoshi Ozaki\uc2  said he doesn't see the yen's strength against the euro as a temporary phenomenon and the company is considering raising vehicle prices and slashing incentives in Europe.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Andrew Monahan and Juro Osawa contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 28, 2010 13:57 ET (17:57 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100728e67s004wb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
