\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: STI Flat;Earnings Key To Market Moves-NRA Capital\b0\par\par\uc2 1,758 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juli 2010\par 06:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0442 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore stocks struggle to build on initial gains, despite relief over outcome of European banks' stress test, as investors continue to look for more leads. STI flat at 2972.06 midday vs +0.2% at 2980.48 in early trade, with support expected at last week's 2926 low, resistance at 3000. Market breadth almost neutral vs 4 gainers for every decliner earlier. "Notwithstanding the views on the Eurobank tests, the key to stock markets coming out of their current doldrums is corporate earnings," says NRA Capital chairman Kevin Scully; notes if current earnings estimates validated during current 2Q10 reporting season, "this will be one of the catalysts to allow markets to resume their uptrend, albeit at a more modest pace compared to 2009." Overall volume light at 769.8 million shares worth S$671.6 million. Genting Singapore (G13.SG), +1.7% at S$1.23, Berlian Laju (B66.SG), +12.5% at S$0.045, Oakwell Engineering (584.SG), +15.4% at S$0.075, most active. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0439 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.4% at 1350.63 midday, takes out 1350 barrier for first time in 2010; dealers say benchmark index's ability to stay above 1350 would be key to near-term direction. "If it cannot sustain above 1350 by the close, we may see profit-taking setting in. Nevertheless, the medium-term uptrend is very much intact," dealer says. "We are dropping our bearish bias view now," OSK Research says, pegging next resistance at 1395. Among gainers, CIMB (1023) +1.4% at MYR7.36, AirAsia (5099.KU) +2.1% at MYR1.43, Scomi (7158.KU) +2.4% at 42.5 sen; among decliners, Mudajaya (5085.KU) down 4.3% at MYR5.63 on profit-taking after rising 18% last week. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0436 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end down 0.4% at 498.1 vs 502.7 intraday high, as local investors sell on concerns high borrowing costs will hurt companies' profitability in 2H. "Government officials said there is no immediate concern over inflation, but they haven't signalled cutting interest rates. More players prefer to stay on the sidelines and they are waiting to see whether government will announce any changes in its moneytary policy for August," says trader. Volume moderate with 42.5 million shares valued at VND1.24 trillion traded. Among actives, \uc2 Sacombank\uc2  (STB.VH) ends +0.6% at VND17,500, Ocean Group (OGC.VH) ends +1.6% at VND45,800, REE (REE.VH) ends down 3.6% at VND18,900. Traders tip benchmark index in 495-498 range Tuesday. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0430 GMT [Dow Jones] India interbank call rate above RBI's key lending rate of 5.50%, set to rise more as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to hike key rates by at least 25 bps at policy review Tuesday, says state-run bank dealer. Overnight call rate at 5.75% vs 5.30% last close. Collateralized lending and borrowing obligation, or CBLO, rate unchanged vs 5.50% at open. "The RBI will hike key policy rates and there are fears that it may go all the way and hike by 50 basis points because of high inflation concerns. That will push up the call rate immediately by an equal amount," trader says. Total traded volumes under call window INR96.41 billion so far. Notes, call rates may be cushioned tad after expected rate hike as INR300 billion of 11.30% 2010 bonds mature July 28. Trader tips call rate in 5.50%-5.80% range in session, CBLO rate 5.40%-5.55% range. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0427 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 0.8% at 9507.86, surrendering some morning gains on sluggishness in Shanghai stocks. Hopes rising for better-than-expected 1Q earnings results by major Japanese firms due later this week, but investors also eyeing FY guidance. "Many companies are likely to maintain conservative full-year outlooks" amid uncertainties about the U.S. economy and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks, says Tsuyoshi Segawa, equity strategist at Mizuho Securities. Adds Nikkei likely to test 9700 level this week. Exporters remain higher on weaker yen; Canon (7751.TO) up 2.9% at Y3,545, TDK (6762.TO) up 2.0% at Y5,140. Eisai (4523.TO) up 4.4% at Y2,900 after winning FDA approval for Alzheimer's treatment drug. \uc2 Suzuki Motor\uc2  (7269.TO) heaviest-weighted drag on Nikkei, down 2.6% to Y1,774 after Indian subsidiary posted unexpected 20% on-year fall in 1Q net profit. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0429 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD maintaining firm tone in wake of widely perceived favorable European bank stress tests, says \uc2 Westpac\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Imre Speizer; "The way I'm seeing it the Kiwi will stay high for the first part of the week but there is a good chance it has a reversal later in the week." Pair last at 0.7279 vs 0.7265 early this morning. Adds, NZD/USD likely to hold tone overnight as U.S. equity futures have nudged higher, Europe markets expected stronger on the news; "Despite all the criticism around methodology, severity (of the stress tests) the upshot is the market liked it, the market rallied on it and the market is staying high." Tips support at 0.7200 with resistance at 0.7300-0.7325. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0426 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD tad lower at 32.126 midday in thin volume vs 32.152 late Friday on improved risk appetite following gains on Wall Street Friday, better-than-expected outcome from stress tests on some of Europe's biggest banks, says trader with foreign bank. Tips pair in 32.100-32.150 band. "The market is pretty quiet today. NT$32.100 will be the strong support level as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  whenever the pair falls below this rate to protect the local exporters." Says Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected of buying Friday; USD/TWD ended at 32.150 Friday, off 32.09 low. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0425 GMT [Dow Jones] Recent comments from Japanese politicians regarding negative impact of recently strong JPY on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy "suggest the chances of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are rising," says Masafumi Yamamoto, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  in Japan. While Japanese government hasn't stepped into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to tame JPY strength since 2004, says chances would increase, especially if USD/JPY falls toward 85.00, dragging down Japan share markets. Cites comments last week from trade minister Naoshima, economic, fiscal policy minister Arai that stronger JPY could hurt Japanese economy; remark from parliamentary secretary Tsumura that current JPY level is little too high. Says while BOJ seems more hesitant to try to fight JPY strength with additional monetary easing, BOJ/government divide would likely not deter government from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  if JPY strengthens further. In \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , not \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has authority for deciding on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . USD/JPY last 87.63.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0422 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ shares heading for positive close, but underperforming offshore markets, last +0.4% at 3006.65. Volume light as most investors tread water, await corporate earnings reports, says \uc2 Forsyth Barr\uc2  broker David Price; "People have set themselves and are not prepared to move, hence the lack of volume. People are just wanting confirmation that the stocks they have selected are delivering what they thought they would." Notes reporting season kicks off tomorrow with \uc2 Abano Healthcare\uc2  (ABA.NZ), +1.2% at NZ$5.20 although most companies don't report for another few weeks. Says people will mainly be focused on company outlook statements and "you get the feeling they will on balance be rather cloudy." Otherwise, some tepid interest in large caps such as Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ0 +0.9% at NZ$7.70, but Telecom (TEL.NZ) flat at NZ$1.96. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0420 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 0.4% at 4477, but well below this morning's 4-week high of 4503.1 after \uc2 QBE Insurance\uc2  (QBE.AU) profit warning and disappointing Harvey Norman (HVN.AU) sales. "I just think it's the disappointing announcements from Harvey Norman and QBE," says a senior institutional trader at a major broker. "I know \uc2 Wesfarmers\uc2  (WES.AU) was positive, but net net, the announcements today are negative." Also says the market is becoming wary of potential "buy rumor, sell fact" reaction by European markets to Friday's European bank stress tests, which were generally considered to be too "low bar" to be credible. On the charts, it will be important to see if S&P/ASX 200 can close above former resistance at 4465.6. QBE (QBE.AU) down 5.8% at A$16.92, while Harvey Norman down 3% at A$3.54. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0412 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds lower but trading in narrow range as market participants stay cautious ahead of RBI's monetary policy review Tuesday, says trader with state-run bank. Benchmark 7.80% 2020 bond at 100.73 vs 100.77 last close. "There are talks that the interest rate corridor may even be narrowed with a 50 basis point hike in reverse repo and a 25 basis point hike in the repo rate. But I think that RBI will stick to a more conventional 25 basis point hike in both rates," trader says. Tips benchmark 2020 bond in 100.65-100.90 range in session. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0356 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR tad lower, trade in narrow range as volatile local stocks slip in and out of positive territory, says trader with state-run bank. Adds, pair to mirror stock movements ahead of RBI's monetary policy meet Tuesday. Pair at 46.91 vs 46.94 late Friday. "Ideally, the rupee should be supported on global cues but Asian shares are only slightly higher. Besides, the pair will closely track local stocks and the 18,000 level is a very nervous Sensex level. So, there will likely not be any brisk movement in stocks today," trader says. Tips pair in 46.85-47.05 range in session. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com) [ 07-26-10 0042ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100726e67q00036\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
