\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/TWD Lower Midday; 32.100-32.150 Band Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,752 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juli 2010\par 06:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0426 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD tad lower at 32.126 midday in thin volume vs 32.152 late Friday on improved risk appetite following gains on Wall Street Friday, better-than-expected outcome from stress tests on some of Europe's biggest banks, says trader with foreign bank. Tips pair in 32.100-32.150 band. "The market is pretty quiet today. NT$32.100 will be the strong support level as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  whenever the pair falls below this rate to protect the local exporters." Says Taiwan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  suspected of buying Friday; USD/TWD ended at 32.150 Friday, off 32.09 low. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0425 GMT [Dow Jones] Recent comments from Japanese politicians regarding negative impact of recently strong JPY on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy "suggest the chances of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are rising," says Masafumi Yamamoto, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  in Japan. While Japanese government hasn't stepped into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to tame JPY strength since 2004, says chances would increase, especially if USD/JPY falls toward 85.00, dragging down Japan share markets. Cites comments last week from trade minister Naoshima, economic, fiscal policy minister Arai that stronger JPY could hurt Japanese economy; remark from parliamentary secretary Tsumura that current JPY level is little too high. Says while BOJ seems more hesitant to try to fight JPY strength with additional monetary easing, BOJ/government divide would likely not deter government from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  if JPY strengthens further. In \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , not \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has authority for deciding on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . USD/JPY last 87.63.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0422 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ shares heading for positive close, but underperforming offshore markets, last +0.4% at 3006.65. Volume light as most investors tread water, await corporate earnings reports, says \uc2 Forsyth Barr\uc2  broker David Price; "People have set themselves and are not prepared to move, hence the lack of volume. People are just wanting confirmation that the stocks they have selected are delivering what they thought they would." Notes reporting season kicks off tomorrow with \uc2 Abano Healthcare\uc2  (ABA.NZ), +1.2% at NZ$5.20 although most companies don't report for another few weeks. Says people will mainly be focused on company outlook statements and "you get the feeling they will on balance be rather cloudy." Otherwise, some tepid interest in large caps such as Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ0 +0.9% at NZ$7.70, but Telecom (TEL.NZ) flat at NZ$1.96. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0420 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 0.4% at 4477, but well below this morning's 4-week high of 4503.1 after \uc2 QBE Insurance\uc2  (QBE.AU) profit warning and disappointing Harvey Norman (HVN.AU) sales. "I just think it's the disappointing announcements from Harvey Norman and QBE," says a senior institutional trader at a major broker. "I know \uc2 Wesfarmers\uc2  (WES.AU) was positive, but net net, the announcements today are negative." Also says the market is becoming wary of potential "buy rumor, sell fact" reaction by European markets to Friday's European bank stress tests, which were generally considered to be too "low bar" to be credible. On the charts, it will be important to see if S&P/ASX 200 can close above former resistance at 4465.6. QBE (QBE.AU) down 5.8% at A$16.92, while Harvey Norman down 3% at A$3.54. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0412 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds lower but trading in narrow range as market participants stay cautious ahead of RBI's monetary policy review Tuesday, says trader with state-run bank. Benchmark 7.80% 2020 bond at 100.73 vs 100.77 last close. "There are talks that the interest rate corridor may even be narrowed with a 50 basis point hike in reverse repo and a 25 basis point hike in the repo rate. But I think that RBI will stick to a more conventional 25 basis point hike in both rates," trader says. Tips benchmark 2020 bond in 100.65-100.90 range in session. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0356 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR tad lower, trade in narrow range as volatile local stocks slip in and out of positive territory, says trader with state-run bank. Adds, pair to mirror stock movements ahead of RBI's monetary policy meet Tuesday. Pair at 46.91 vs 46.94 late Friday. "Ideally, the rupee should be supported on global cues but Asian shares are only slightly higher. Besides, the pair will closely track local stocks and the 18,000 level is a very nervous Sensex level. So, there will likely not be any brisk movement in stocks today," trader says. Tips pair in 46.85-47.05 range in session. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0351 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex opens higher, helped by positive regional markets, overnight gains in U.S. markets after stress tests of European banks showed most banks could withstand economic downturn or government-bond crisis. Still, index sheds all gains, now flat at 18,132.27 vs day's high of 18,194.09, as investors likely cautious at higher levels, says Deven Choksey, managing director of K.R. Choksey Shares and Securities. "Buying will not come immediately. But since the global scenario looks much better post the stress tests, we expect investors to look for buying opportunities at lower levels." Expects trading volumes, which have been low lately, to gradually pick up. Maruti Suzuki (532500.BY) down 9.2% at INR1,232.60 after disappointing 1Q results, \uc2 Hero Honda Motors\uc2  (500182.BY) off 5.7% at INR1,847.15. \uc2 Bharti Airtel\uc2  (532454.BY) +1.1% at INR317, ICICI Bank (532174.BY) +0.8% at INR919.80. S&P CNX Nifty off 0.1% at 5444.75. (amitha.rajan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0348 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares turn lower midday, led by property developers, banks on profit taking. Shanghai Composite Index down 0.5% at 2559.59, expected to consolidate around 60-day moving average (now at 2575) in afternoon. "Investors haven't regained their confidence (in the stock market) as there are no signs that the government will ease its control over the property market," says Wu Dazhong, analyst at Shenyin Wanguo. "All eyes are on China's macro-economic policies for the rest of the year. If monetary authorities' stance turns accommodative, that would benefit the stock market." Banks, property developers lead fall after recent sharp gains: ICBC (601398.SH) off 0.9% at CNY4.28, AgBank (601288.SH) off 1.1% at CNY2.78; Vanke (000002.SZ) off 1.1% at CNY7.91, Poly Real Estate (600048.SH) off 2.0% at CNY12.13. Shenzhen Index down 0.2% at 1036.86. (rose.yu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0341 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0236 GMT mistakenly reported the relative strength of EUR/JPY. The correct version follows:) Nikkei up 1.2% at 9546.01 midday on slew of sanguine pre-earnings reports for Japanese firms like Softbank (9984.TO); firmer EUR/JPY (now 113.35) after Friday's European bank stress test results. "Relief over the (bank test) results may last for this week, but it is possible doubts will later increase on whether the tests were sufficient," says Yutaka Yoshii, general manager at \uc2 Mito Securities\uc2 . Nikkei range for afternoon: 9520-9636. 31/33 Topix subindexes higher; Inpex (1605.TO) up 5.0% rise to Y430,500 ahead of pricing of firm's mammoth equity capital increase offer. Exporters up; Canon (7751.TO) up 3.2% at Y3,555, helped by stronger EUR/JPY. Softbank (9984.TO) up 2.3% at Y2,574 after Nikkei report company's 1Q operating profit likely to rise about 80% on-year. Eisai (4523.TO) up 4.4% at Y2,901 after winning FDA approval for Alzheimer's treatment. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0330 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares pare gains, last flat at 500.10 vs 502.7 earlier, as local investors cautious on growing concerns banks will continue to keep high interest rates next month. "The government said inflation isn't the main concern for this month, but there is no official indication that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will soon cut the benchmark interest rate," says analyst. Volume thin with 31 million shares valued at VND900 billion traded. Among leading advancers, \uc2 Petrosetco\uc2  (PET.VH) +1.3% at VND24,000, Dai Thien Loc (DTL.VH) +4.5% at VND23,400, Petroleum Industrial & Civil Construction (PXI.VH) +3.7% at VND30,700. Tips immediate support at 500. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0312 GMT [Dow Jones] Bank of America-Merrill Lynch economist Lu Ting says floods in southern China may cause July CPI to rise 3.5% on-year, up from +2.9% in June, as vegetable prices have jumped 8% from end-June to mid-July. But Lu says impact likely to be temporary; keeps full year inflation forecast at 2.9%. Lu also argues flooding shows need for more FAI spending on water reservoirs, river banks. "FAI in water projects could be used as a major tool to bolster China's growth in the upcoming period." (aaron.back@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0312 GMT [Dow Jones] LME 3-month copper likely to wait for London open to gain any momentum with arbitrage window shut, stalling any momentum in Asia. Contract at $7,015/ton, down $14 with LME contract at slight premium to Shanghai encouraging some selling from China based traders. Overall, last week's 9% gains point to further upside with some big picture indicators like Shanghai equities, dry bulk freight rates starting to turn. "The Chinese market has undergone a resurrection, having posted its best week in seven months, and is breaking out, which bodes well near-term for the commodity complex - and if you strain your eye just enough, you will see the Baltic Dry Index is forming a bottom," said David Rosenberg, chief economist at Gluskin Sheff. BDI up 1.4% to 1,826 Friday with a bounce in Chinese steel prices helping to put a floor under the market after 60% drop in freight rates over June, July. (james.campbell@dowjones.com) [ 07-26-10 0026ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100726e67q00030\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
