\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Indonesia Shares Likely Lower; 3000 Support\b0\par\par\uc2 1,546 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 2010\par 04:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0205 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares likely to open lower, tracking falls in several Asian markets such as Taiwan, Japan and South Korea after overnight fall on Wall Street, traders say; tip support at 3000 after ending up 0.6% at all-time closing high of 3013.401 yesterday. "I think there should be a mild and healthy correction after recent gains with foreign funds likely continuing to take profit," says trader at Lautandhana Securities. Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK), \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Mandiri\f28 \uc2 \f2  (BMRI.JK) may lead falls following recent gains. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0203 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY rises on overnight USD strength vs EUR after U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke notes an "unusually uncertain" economic outlook, dollar/yuan central parity rate set at 6.7859, highest since June 30, vs 6.7802; offshore 1-year USD/CNY NDFs advance to 6.7038/6.7098 (pricing in merely 1.1% CNY appreciation), highest since June 18 before PBOC announced effective depegging of CNY vs USD, vs 6.6893/6.6943 late yesterday. Onshore USD/CNY spot at 6.7812 vs 6.7769 last close. "The recent rise in dollar-yuan NDFs seem to be driven by corporate hedging needs and repatriation of dollars by foreign firms at mid-year, with no strong market expectations of CNY appreciation," says Shanghai-based trader at European bank. He expects 1-year USD/CNY NDFs to consolidate in 6.6800-6.7300 range near term. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0202 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI down 0.3% at 1337.59 as overnight declines on Wall Street spur profit-taking; but KLCI's losses not expected to last as recent increase in corporate M&A activities, expected roll-out of large government infrastructure projects likely to keep market on uptrend, dealers say. "The market still appears poised to challenge the 1350 resistance level," dealer says. KLCI has yet to surpass 1350 in 2010, hitting high of 1349 in May. Among decliners, MRCB (1651.KU) down 0.6% at MYR1.72, Mudajaya (5085.KU) down 1.4% at MYR5.63, Tenaga (5347.KU) down 0.7% at MYR8.59. Among gainers, Tatt Giap (5178.KU) +4.3% at 60.5 sen on its debut. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0200 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares likely to fall due to weakness on Wall Street and lack of fresh news about what government is doing to boost liquidity. "The state agencies have talked about ways to increase lending but as long as banks continue to keep high borrowing rates which are above 15% these days, the economy will see weak inflows for a while, and the stock market will likely move sideways in the near term," says Hanoi-based trader. Tips key index in 500-503 range after falling 0.2% to 505.4 yesterday. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0153 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR higher at 9,075 vs 9,060 late yesterday, tracking USD gains against major Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after U.S. Fed Chairman Bernanke's downbeat comments on U.S. economy overnight, traders say. "Recent foreign selling in local equity market has also helped strengthen the greenback," says trader at local bank. Expects \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to step in if USD nears 9,100; tips pair in 9,050-9,090 band rest of day. Foreigners net sellers of IDR297 billion worth of local shares yesterday. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0150 GMT [Dow Jones] A Beijing-based biotechnology company is providing 23 tons of oil-eating bacteria to help clean up the 183-square-kilometer slick around China northern Dalian port, after two oil pipelines exploded there last Friday. Beijing Micro-Cleaner Biotechnology Co., called Weiyeyuan in pinyin Chinese, is a young company; introduction on its website says it was founded just last year and that the company makes "environmentally friendly microbial products." It's not clear yet how effective the little bugs will be in this case, but likely to help at least a little. Wouldn't be first time oil-eating microbes have helped clean spills; they were used with some success in 1989 Exxon Valdez spill in Alaska. See WSJ BLOG: "As Oil Spills, China Sends In The Bugs." (chuin-wei.yap@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0147 GMT [Dow Jones] July HSI futures down 0.4% at 20,456, as cash market tipped to open lower on Wall Street cue. Still, contract ended yesterday at 48-point premium to cash, pointing to positive near-term bias, perhaps as HK investors' confidence bolstered by A-shares' recent strength. July open interest increases 1,944 lots to 74,419. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0146 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD advances slightly to 32.170 vs 32.150 late yesterday, tracking firmer USD/Asians, 0.4% drop in local stocks, says local bank trader. "(Fed) Bernanke's comments yesterday (calling the economic outlook) "unusually uncertain" caused selling in high-yield \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a strengthening of the U.S. dollar." But adds \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue confining local pair to a tight range, tips 32.100-32.200 band today. (alex.pevzner@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0142 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore stocks holding up despite weakness on Wall Street, with players seizing opportunity to nibble after yesterday's pullback. STI +0.3% at 2934.39, with immediate resistance at current July high of 2958. "I'm surprised by the market's resilience. It's hardly taking cues from Wall Street these days," says dealer at foreign brokerage. Still, analysts remain cautious, with \uc2 Kim Eng Securities\uc2  saying STI appears to have peaked; "expect difficult trading today." NetResearch Asia tips rangebound trading "as investors continue to weigh and mull over improving corporate earnings numbers and receding fears of a double-dip recession against concerns over high unemployment in the U.S. and the lack of conviction on the sustainability of the U.S. and global economic recovery." Among STI components, gainers led by Jardine group, \uc2 Fraser & Neave\uc2  (F99.SG) +1.4% at S$5.65, Olam (O32.SG) +1.2% at S$2.65. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0140 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds, bond futures higher in early trade boosted by comments from Fed Chief Bernanke overnight that U.S. economic outlook "unusually uncertain," adding to concerns over possible slowdown in U.S. economic recovery; but participants don't expect much sharper gains in local bond prices, as yields now hovering around lower-end of recent trading ranges. "Futures have been outperforming cash bonds recently, mostly helped by foreign buying," says local securities firm trader. But adds, if foreigners don't buy aggressively, other participants unlikely to cover shorts in futures, which could mean limited gains for bond prices today. Puts support for 3-year yield at 3.82%; last down 2 bps at 3.85%, 5-year yield also down 2 bps at 4.44%, September futures +9 ticks at 110.81. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0136 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan Deputy Finance Minister Ikeda signals discomfort with recent JPY gains, saying government wants to avoid excessive JPY strength; but USD/JPY's lack of reaction highlights "people, at least in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , believe the government would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at this point due to its desire to not upset the U.S.," says Mamoru Arai, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 . Weaker USD benefits U.S. by making country's exports more competitive. "We look at how much pressure the U.S. government has put on China on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue, for example, and think to ourselves, 'How could Japan, which relies a lot on the U.S. in the security realm, conduct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it would clearly go against what the U.S. wants?'" But adds, because such perceptions may not be so strong outside Japan, "market participants in Europe and the U.S. could be more prone to think that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be possible." Also says \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  government's calculus on possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could change if USD/JPY dropped very sharply, by 2 or more JPY. Pair last 86.60, down from earlier high at 87.09. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0131 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold early in Sydney bid $1,185.70/oz, down 60 cents from late Wednesday in NY. Capital Economics says with inflation risks low, gold already having priced in a lot of bad news, major new shock is needed to propel gold much higher, such as threat of EMU break-up, renewed doubts about creditworthiness of a major country such as U.S. or Japan, risk of a trade war between China and the West. "But none of these risks are likely to come to a head over the remainder of this year. Accordingly, we continue to expect gold prices to drop back towards $1,000/oz by end-2010," consultancy says in note. Gold's fall in past week to 2-month low below $1,200 vindicated Capital's skepticism about forecasts of a spike to $1,400, which were common in May, it says. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com) [ 07-21-10 2205ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100722e67m0001g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
