\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: STI +0.3%; Index Seems To Have Peaked - Kim Eng\b0\par\par\uc2 1,631 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 2010\par 03:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0142 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore stocks holding up despite weakness on Wall Street, with players seizing opportunity to nibble after yesterday's pullback. STI +0.3% at 2934.39, with immediate resistance at current July high of 2958. "I'm surprised by the market's resilience. It's hardly taking cues from Wall Street these days," says dealer at foreign brokerage. Still, analysts remain cautious, with \uc2 Kim Eng Securities\uc2  saying STI appears to have peaked; "expect difficult trading today." NetResearch Asia tips rangebound trading "as investors continue to weigh and mull over improving corporate earnings numbers and receding fears of a double-dip recession against concerns over high unemployment in the U.S. and the lack of conviction on the sustainability of the U.S. and global economic recovery." Among STI components, gainers led by Jardine group, \uc2 Fraser & Neave\uc2  (F99.SG) +1.4% at S$5.65, Olam (O32.SG) +1.2% at S$2.65. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0140 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds, bond futures higher in early trade boosted by comments from Fed Chief Bernanke overnight that U.S. economic outlook "unusually uncertain," adding to concerns over possible slowdown in U.S. economic recovery; but participants don't expect much sharper gains in local bond prices, as yields now hovering around lower-end of recent trading ranges. "Futures have been outperforming cash bonds recently, mostly helped by foreign buying," says local securities firm trader. But adds, if foreigners don't buy aggressively, other participants unlikely to cover shorts in futures, which could mean limited gains for bond prices today. Puts support for 3-year yield at 3.82%; last down 2 bps at 3.85%, 5-year yield also down 2 bps at 4.44%, September futures +9 ticks at 110.81. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0136 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan Deputy Finance Minister Ikeda signals discomfort with recent JPY gains, saying government wants to avoid excessive JPY strength; but USD/JPY's lack of reaction highlights "people, at least in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , believe the government would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at this point due to its desire to not upset the U.S.," says Mamoru Arai, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 . Weaker USD benefits U.S. by making country's exports more competitive. "We look at how much pressure the U.S. government has put on China on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue, for example, and think to ourselves, 'How could Japan, which relies a lot on the U.S. in the security realm, conduct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it would clearly go against what the U.S. wants?'" But adds, because such perceptions may not be so strong outside Japan, "market participants in Europe and the U.S. could be more prone to think that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be possible." Also says \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  government's calculus on possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could change if USD/JPY dropped very sharply, by 2 or more JPY. Pair last 86.60, down from earlier high at 87.09. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0131 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold early in Sydney bid $1,185.70/oz, down 60 cents from late Wednesday in NY. Capital Economics says with inflation risks low, gold already having priced in a lot of bad news, major new shock is needed to propel gold much higher, such as threat of EMU break-up, renewed doubts about creditworthiness of a major country such as U.S. or Japan, risk of a trade war between China and the West. "But none of these risks are likely to come to a head over the remainder of this year. Accordingly, we continue to expect gold prices to drop back towards $1,000/oz by end-2010," consultancy says in note. Gold's fall in past week to 2-month low below $1,200 vindicated Capital's skepticism about forecasts of a spike to $1,400, which were common in May, it says. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0131 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares tipped lower, taking cue from overnight losses in Wall Street after Fed Chairman Bernanke's "unusually uncertain" outlook comment; benchmark PSEi to head towards 3370 support after closing 0.4% higher at 3418.17 yesterday, resistance at 3450; likely to decline are Philippine National Bank (PNB.PH), \uc2 DMCI Holdings\uc2  (DMC.PH) and Universal Robina (URC.PH), all gainers in recent days. "News like Bernanke's comment is negative to the market. Expect the market to be in a corrective mode," says Paul Balaoing, analyst at PCCI Securities Brokers. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0128 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD tad lower in thin trade; but pair biased up on risk aversion after U.S. Fed Chairman Bernanke's remark on uncertain economic outlook, says trader at Chinese bank. Tips pair in 7.7740-7.7790 band today. "The expected fall in local stocks, tracking the weaker U.S. stocks overnight, might prompt investors to sell the local unit," trader says. Pair last at 7.7761 vs 7.7769 yesterday.(chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0128 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB higher at 32.32-32.34 vs 32.29-32.30 late yesterday in Asia in line with mostly weaker regionals after testimony overnight by Fed Chairman Bernanke who noted an "unusually uncertain" economic outlook. "The market is also waiting for Europe's stress tests on Friday, so it's a bit cautious about that and some people may be looking to square their positions ahead 