\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ 2nd UPDATE: BOJ Yamaguchi Voices Caution Against Yen Rises\b0\par\par\uc2 1,040 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juli 2010\par 10:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Adds comments from BOJ Deputy Gov. Yamaguchi at a press conference.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   By Megumi Fujikawa \par   Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 TOYAMA, Japan (Dow Jones)--A Bank of Japan deputy governor voiced caution Wednesday against the yen's recent rises, saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is keeping a close eye on how movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market affect the overall economy.\par \par \uc2 "It cannot be denied that the yen's strength has been having an impact on exporters," Hirohide Yamaguchi told reporters after meeting business leaders in Toyama, central Japan. "But the extent of the impact depends on ranges and how long the yen's appreciation continues. (The BOJ) will monitor real developments in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and examine their effects on business sentiment."\par \par \uc2 Any comments on the dollar's recent drift toward the Y85 mark by a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy board member will likely be scrutinized by financial market participants amid speculation that the BOJ may take action if the yen's ascent continues and further damages the nation's export-driven economy.\par \par \uc2 People familiar with BOJ thinking told Dow Jones Newswires recently that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may consider taking additional easing steps to prevent the economy from worsening if the dollar stays around Y85 for a month or two.\par \par \uc2 While an outright \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets is in the domain of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Ministry, the BOJ has other weapons in its arsenal to deter the yen from rising further. BOJ watchers say that such options include the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  providing the financial sector with extra funds by boosting the amount of Japanese government bonds it buys or increasing the amount of low-interest loans to the money market.\par \par \uc2 Downplaying speculation that the BOJ plans to take action once the yen crosses a certain line, Yamaguchi on Wednesday emphasized that the BOJ doesn't steer monetary policy with specific \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates in mind even though it is keeping a close eye on market moves.\par \par \uc2 With Europe's sovereign debt troubles still unsettling financial markets and concern growing that the global recovery is stalling, the greenback briefly dropped to Y86.27 on Friday---its lowest level since Dec. 1. As of 0630 GMT, the dollar stood at Y87.15. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     See Larger Upside, Downside Risks \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In a speech to business leaders earlier in the day, Yamaguchi said that both upside and downside risks to the nation's economy have been growing over the past three months, but he kept the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's standard optimistic view of the domestic economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The mixed message suggests the BOJ will maintain a wait-and-see stance, keeping monetary conditions extremely easy unless risk factors somehow crash the financial system or undermine Japan's nascent economic recovery.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yamaguchi said upside risks--such as faster growth in emerging and commodity-driven economies--and downside risks--such as uncertainty in international financial markets--"have become somewhat large in both directions" compared with April, when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  issued its semiannual outlook report on the economy and prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In particular, attention should be paid (among downside risks) to the effects of developments in fiscal and financial conditions in some European economies on international finance and the global economy," Yamaguchi said in the speech.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "International financial markets have recently become increasingly volatile, triggered by the fiscal problem in Greece and some European countries," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Other BOJ officials have also expressed concern about impact of the European sovereign debt crisis, according to the minutes of the BOJ's June 14-15 meeting, released Wednesday morning.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many BOJ policy board members expressed the view that "although the effects of the heightened uncertainty in European economies on Japan's economy were considered to be limited at present, close attention should be paid to the risk that such uncertainty might depress the world economy through various channels, such as financial markets and international trade," the minutes showed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But Yamaguchi still sounded bullish about the domestic economy, saying "positive moves of increase in exports spreading to domestic private demand have gradually been seen" on the back of the improvement in overseas economic conditions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indeed, the BOJ last week upgraded its growth projections for this fiscal year to 2.6% from 1.8% predicted in April, with the economy expected to continue recovering on the back of healthy exports and a pickup in domestic demand.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Improvements in the supply and demand balance since the spring of last year have also started to appear on prices, slowing the pace of on-year declines in the consumer price index, he added. The latest CPI, which excludes the effects of subsidies for high school tuition, recovered to a 0.7% decline, compared with a record-low 2.4% fall last summer.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, Yamaguchi said achieving a longer-term growth path would be key to combating the nation's lingering deflation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "A fundamental reason for deflation, namely, a continuous price decline, is a lack of basic strength, or, in other words, growth potential of the economy. Therefore, to fundamentally overcome deflation, it is essential to increase the growth potential of Japan's economy from a medium- to long-term perspective."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yamaguchi suggested it may be difficult to boost productivity--and thus the nation's growth potential--by accommodative monetary conditions alone.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In its effort to reinforce Japan's economic foundations, the BOJ in June decided to set up a facility to make Y3 trillion in loans available to private banks for one year at an interest rate of 0.1%, the same rate as its policy target. The program is aimed at encouraging lending to companies in high growth areas, such as environment-related businesses and child-care services, to foster productivity at home.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The deputy chief added that the BOJ would be "willing to actively cooperate," if market participants consider strategies, such as securitizing loans and investments, that would contribute to strengthening the foundations of economic growth. He said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could explore ways to accept such securitized products as eligible collateral for the BOJ's money market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Megumi Fujikawa, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2786; megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 July 21, 2010 04:27 ET (08:27 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100721e67l001ez\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
