\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-INTERVIEW-BOJ sees slow progress on Europe's debt woes\b0\par\par\uc2 742 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juli 2010\par 06:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * TIBOR does not reflect real market rates - BOJ official\par \par \uc2 * Sharp \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves affect economic activity\par \par \uc2 * Big fiscal tightening would hurt European growth\par \par \uc2 * Comments on TIBOR push up euroyen futures (Adds quotes, details)\par \par \uc2 By Leika Kihara and Yoshiyasu Shida\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, July 5 (Reuters) - Global financial markets remain jittery with little progress being made in dealing with the root cause of Europe's debt woes, a senior Bank of Japan (BOJ) official said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Haruyuki Toyama, head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's financial markets department, also said the Tokyo interbank lending rate (TIBOR) -- the rate at which commercial banks lend and borrow to each other -- does not reflect real interest rates in the market place.\par \par \uc2 "There is a discrepancy between the TIBOR and real market rates," Toyama told Reuters in an interview.\par \par \uc2 "When there is a discrepancy between TIBOR and real market rates, it could distort rates in other markets. From the standpoint of ensuring proper market function, I hope the TIBOR is determined appropriately."\par \par \uc2 The comments helped push up euroyen interest rate futures on expectations the BOJ will encourage a fall in TIBOR, a reference rate calculated from rates provided by a pool of banks.\par \par \uc2 TIBOR has been fixed at much higher levels than actual rates in markets despite the BOJ's ultra-easy monetary policy as commercial banks base lending rates they charge corporate borrowers on TIBOR. To them, a lower TIBOR means less profit.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark three-month TIBOR was set at 0.37615 percent on Monday, higher than euroyen futures yield of around 0.25 percent.\par \par \uc2 EUROPE BANKING WOES REMAIN\par \par \uc2 Toyama said it will take some time before market confidence is regained in Europe's banking sector, adding that the BOJ will carefully watch how developments affect financial markets.\par \par \uc2 Persistent worries about Europe's banking problems have led investors to seek a safe haven in bonds and shun risky assets.\par \par \uc2 Share prices also suffered big losses last week as weak U.S. and Chinese economic data heightened concerns that the global economy could be entering a new downturn.\par \par \uc2 Some countries, notably the United States, worry that the fiscal tightening policies now being adopted in Europe could add to the pain.\par \par \uc2 Toyama echoed this view, warning that while Europe's fiscal consolidation will help ease market worries about its debt woes, tightening simultaneously could backfire.\par \par \uc2 "Big fiscal tightening, when taken all at once, will have a negative impact on the economy in the short term, although some of it will be offset by confidence (in fiscal policy)," he said.\par \par \uc2 "I understand this has been discussed by the G20. But I hope due consideration is paid to the impact on the economy."\par \par \uc2 Asked whether the yen's recent sharp gains will hurt Japan's economy, Toyama said: "I'd like to refrain from commenting on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels. But in general, sharp \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves affect household and corporate activity."\par \par \uc2 The dollar edged up against the yen on Monday but remains near a seven-month low of 86.96 yen hit last week as risk-shy investors sought a safe haven in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The yen's rise has brought it to levels that could cause pain to Japanese exporters, with the BOJ's tankan corporate sentiment survey showing large manufacturers forecasting an average dollar/yen rate of 90.18 yen for the year to next March.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  rather than the BOJ has jurisdiction over \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducts \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the ministry.\par \par \uc2 BOND BUYING CAP STAYS\par \par \uc2 The BOJ has kept interest rates near zero since late 2008, and eased monetary policy last December and again in March by setting up and later expanding a facility offering cheap funds to banks.\par \par \uc2 It also buys 21.6 trillion yen ($246.2 billion) of long-term government bonds outright each year as part of measures to supply funds to the market.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ sets a self-imposed rule of capping its government bond holds to the amount of bank notes in circulation, to avoid giving markets the impression it may monetise public debt.\par \par \uc2 Toyama said the BOJ's government bond holdings could reach the self-imposed cap within several years. But he said there was no need to change the rule. ($1=87.74 Yen) (Editing by Michael Watson)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/BOJ (INTERVIEW) (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100708e6750007y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
