\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Focus: The Bank Of Japan Should Stand Back From Market\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicholas Hastings   \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN   \par \uc2 856 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juli 2010\par 09:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--The Bank of Japan would be well advised to sit on its hands.\par \par \uc2 As upward pressure on the yen intensifies, the temptation for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  is certainly growing.\par \par \uc2 The yen has now risen to critical levels for Japanese exporters, just as economic sentiment in Japan has started to improve.\par \par \uc2 But, as recent attempts by the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  to halt the advance of the Swiss franc have shown, market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can often prove futile.\par \par \uc2 The dilemma for Tokyo is certainly a hard one.\par \par \uc2 Chances are that if nothing is done, the dollar will fall as far as Y85.00 now that it has broken under its key 2010 low of Y87.95.\par \par \uc2 See how rapidly the dollar has fallen against the yen:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4213"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4213}}}\par \par \uc2 This a far cry from the Y90.18 level that Japanese companies budgeted for in their plans for the fiscal year ending next March, according to the new Tankan survey from the Bank of Japan Thursday. This means that the yen has now reached levels where it poses a threat to export profits, just as the Japanese economy was starting to look up.\par \par \uc2 Unlike its predecessor for the first quarter, this second quarter Tankan survey shows an optimism that pushed the main diffusion index all the way up from -14 to +1.\par \par \uc2 The problem for the authorities is that the yen has not only risen to critical levels but it is doing so with even more buying pressure than before.\par \par \uc2 Market concern over the global recovery has risen to new heights this week, with the European debt crisis showing little sign of going away and with new data suggesting that China is slowing down faster than anticipated.\par \par \uc2 In the past few months, as the dollar has drifted back down from Y95, the move into safe havens have benefited both the yen and the dollar.\par \par \uc2 But now, as doubts over the U.S. economy grow and the yield on two-year Treasury bonds fall to a new record low, investors are showing less interest in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As a result, flows that might well have benefited the dollar previously are now being diverted towards the yen, increasing the risk that the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will break through its high for the year against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo officials are certainly aware of this with the country's new senior vice finance minister Motohisa Ikeda admitting Thursday that a weak yen is "a plus" for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export-led economy.\par \par \uc2 He hinted at \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with a warning that the government's goal is to prevent "excessive" yen strength.\par \par \uc2 However, with upward pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  increasing, Ikeda and his cohorts at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  may find it best to say or do little more.\par \par \uc2 More explicit threats and actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself could well provide speculators with just the excuse they are looking for to buy the yen even more.\par \par \uc2 And, as the SNB found out to its recent cost, there is little a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can do to stop this rally once it starts.\par \par \uc2 Early Friday in Europe, a recent move out of dollar assets, much to the benefit of the euro and the yen, had stalled as investors now wait to see if the U.S. non-farm payrolls live up to its bad billing. The consensus forecast is for payrolls, which rose by 431,000 in May, to have fallen by 110,000 in June.\par \par \uc2 Although the reversal is associated with a decline in jobs for census takers, there is rising concern, given other negative data, that the U.S. economy is facing a double dip recession.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, news that the new Australian prime minister had negotiated concessions on a new mining tax, including a reduction to 30% from 40% on profits, helped to improve global sentiment. The bounce was evident in a 0.1% rise in the Nikkei index in Japan.\par \par \uc2 By 0700 GMT, the dollar was up at Y88.10 from Y87.56 late on Thursday in New York, according to EBS.\par \par \uc2 The euro was down at $1.2485 from $1.2515 but was up at Y110.00 from Y109.58.\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg TNI FRX POV\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Reuters   USD/DJ \par    Thomson   P/1066 or P/1074 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Nick Hastings has covered the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets and industry for over 20 years. Apart from his written commentary and analysis, he also appears on Fox Business News and CNBC television in Europe, Asia and the U.S. He can be contacted on +44-20-7842-9493 or by email: nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkBackEurope@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 02-07-10 0715GMT ]\f28 \p