\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Key political risks to watch in Japan\b0\par\par\uc2 By Linda Sieg   \par \uc2 1,149 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juli 2010\par 09:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, July 1 (Reuters) - Japan's ruling party faces a July 11 upper house election that it needs to win to forge ahead with policies including steps to rein in huge public debt, such as a possible sales tax rise in two or three years.\par \par \uc2 Japan's 5-year sovereign credit default swap spread rose to as high as 100 basis points in late May, the highest in more than a year, but has since slipped 10 basis points to 89.75 basis points. That is still more than 30 basis points higher than a trough seen in early March.\par \par \uc2 Following is a summary of key political risks to watch:\par \par \uc2 * FISCAL DILEMMA\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Naoto Kan, who took over from his unpopular predecessor Yukio Hatoyama in June, has made fixing state finances a centrepiece of the election campaign and floated the notion of doubling the 5 percent sales tax in a political gamble.\par \par \uc2 The government unveiled a strategy in June to tackle public debt, but the plan lacked specific ideas on how to meet its long-term aim of achieving budget balance targets and reducing its debt-to-GDP ratio -- now estimated at nearly twice the size of GDP, the worst in the developed world.\par \par \uc2 Economists say a sales tax hike is crucial, perhaps to as high as 20 percent. Surveys show voters worried about public debt are less sensitive to the move than in the past.\par \par \uc2 But the need to woo fresh coalition partners if the Democrats fall short of a majority in the upper house poll would complicate and probably delay debate on the sales tax.\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- Comments by Kan, Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda and senior party officials will show whether the Democratic party-led government plans to stick with its fiscal reform drive or if it will temper its message in the face of declining voter support.\par \par \uc2 -- Comments or downgrades by ratings agencies. Credit ratings agencies have cautiously welcomed the government's fiscal plans but have warned a lack of fiscal discipline or an external shock could place Japan's sovereign rating under pressure.\par \par \uc2 NEW LEADER, ELECTION OUTLOOK\par \par \uc2 The selection of former grassroots activist Kan as prime minister has improved the DPJ's chances in the upper house poll but no media surveys currently suggest it can win a majority.\par \par \uc2 The DPJ swept to power in a historic election last year and will run the government whatever the outcome of the July upper house poll, but the ruling bloc needs to win a majority in the upper chamber to ensure legislation is enacted smoothly.\par \par \uc2 If the DPJ and its tiny coalition partner, the pro-spending People's New Party, fall short of a majority, the Democrats will need to find other partners such as the Buddhist-backed New Komeito Party or the tiny reformist Your Party.\par \par \uc2 That would lead to messy manoeuvring and potential deadlock but might push the government toward more market-friendly policies to get the pro-deregulation Your Party on board.\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- Opinion polls in the run-up to the upper house election will provide clues on whether the ruling coalition can maintain its majority in the chamber.\par \par \uc2 -- If the DPJ falls short of Kan's "54 plus alpha" target for upper house seats he will be vulnerable to challenges from within the party in a September leadership election.\par \par \uc2 -- The actions of party powerbroker Ichiro Ozawa, who resigned as the DPJ's No.2 executive when Hatoyama quit, should also be watched as his manoeuvring could weaken Kan. Ozawa opposes bold steps to tackle Japan's tattered finances.\par \par \uc2 * POLICY TENSION WITH \b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 The government has put pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to take bolder steps to overcome deflation, and the bank has responded with additional easing steps, often even as it upgraded its economic assessment. Government pressure has subsided since the bank eased monetary policy in several stages, and Kan has said the government and the Bank of Japan are on the same page.\par \par \uc2 Although some ruling party lawmakers have demanded the BOJ adopt formal inflation-targeting to strengthen its commitment to end deflation, the party stopped short of adopting strong language on the BOJ in its campaign platform for elections.\par \par \uc2 It merely said it seeks an early end to deflation through focused efforts and cooperation between the government and BOJ.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has argued against the idea of adopting inflation targeting, saying policy under such a scheme could be swayed by short-term price fluctuations and overlook other important factors such as asset prices.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ nevertheless offered additional help for the recovery by launching a new loan scheme aimed at redirecting money to industries with growth potential.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts say the scheme is an apparent attempt to relieve pressure from the government for more action, but many doubt its effectiveness in supporting the fragile economy.\par \par \uc2 Though the BOJ can set its policy independently, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is required to work closely with the government to align policy and has in the past caved in to government pressure.\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- BOJ policy board meeting on July 14-15, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will review its long-term economic and price projections issued in April.\par \par \uc2 * YEN \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 Kan is widely regarded by \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders as favouring a weaker yen, and his recent comments have done nothing to change that impression. In his first news conference as premier, Kan said: "I am aware of the general argument that a weak yen is beneficial for Japan's exporters."\par \par \uc2 Junior lawmakers in the DPJ, including Motohisa Ikeda, who is now deputy finance minister, in the past called for a policy of pushing the yen down towards 120 per dollar -- a level it has not traded at since 2007. Comments like these have kept \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders wary that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen.\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 -- Comments by government officials about sudden \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves and possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Picking a level that would trigger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is tricky. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the yen surged beyond its 14-year high of 84.82 per dollar set in November, some analysts say. But \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be hard given that Group of Seven countries were willing to stand by as the euro plunged due to Greece's debt crisis. The G7 is encouraging flexibility in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, particularly in China, and that would also make it hard for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to justify \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 -- A more likely way of countering a surge in yen strength could be for the government to pressure the Bank of Japan into taking more easing steps, as it did in December after the yen hit a 14-year high.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Linda Sieg, Yoko Nishikawa and Leika Kihara)\par \par \uc2 RISKS-JAPAN/ (FACTBOX)|LANGEN|Table|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|ABX|BNX|FUN|SXNA   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100701e671000dr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
