\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ Japan Ex-MOF Sakakibara: Current JPY Level Not Such A "Blow" To Japan Economy\b0\par\par\uc2 360 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juni 2010\par 01:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 14 Jun 2010 19:20 EDT *DJ  Sakakibara: Expect Kan To Propose Consumption Tax Hike After 2013 Election\par \par \uc2 14 Jun 2010 19:22 EDT *DJ  Sakakibara: No Fiscal Problem For Japan Next 3-4 Years\par \par \uc2 14 Jun 2010 19:22 EDT *DJ  Sakakibara: "Exaggeration" To Compare Japan Debt Problem To Greece\par \par \uc2 14 Jun 2010 19:23 EDT *DJ  Sakakibara: Not Worried About Japan Deflation\par \par \uc2 14 Jun 2010 19:24 EDT *DJ  Sakakibara: No Major Volatility Problem With JPY, Japan Economy\par \par \uc2 14 Jun 2010 19:25 EDT *DJ  Sakakibara Was Speaking In CBNC Interview\par \par \uc2 14 Jun 2010 19:49 EDT DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Ex-MOF Sakakibara Doesn't See Need For \b \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's former point man on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Eisuke Sakakibara, said Tuesday he doesn't see a need for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as the yen isn't too volatile and stepping in now wouldn't be effective.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against the wind doesn't work," said Sakakibara, who led aggressive yen-selling and yen-buying campaigns as a top Finance Ministry official in the late 1990s. "It only works when it can turn the tide."\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  wouldn't be "appropriate" without concerted action that also involved the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Sakakibara, now a professor at Waseda University in Tokyo, told CNBC.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The former vice minister for international affairs predicted new Prime Minister Naoto Kan will keep his predecessor's promise not to raise Japan's consumption tax before 2013 but will propose raising it after elections that year to 10% or 15% from the current 5%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He said Japan, despite having "huge" government debt amounting to 180% of gross domestic product, won't face any fiscal problems for three or four years as accumulated household savings are worth 220% of GDP and some 95% of Japanese government bonds are held by Japanese.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It's an "exaggeration" to compare Japan's debt problems to those of Greece, although Tokyo will need to begin tackling its debt in about four years, Sakakibara said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 June 14, 2010 19:49 ET (23:49 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100614e66e0054v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
