\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-FACTBOX-Key political risks to watch in Japan\b0\par\par\uc2 1,447 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juni 2010\par 07:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Fixes typo in graphics box)\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, June 7 (Reuters) - Japan's Naoto Kan takes over as prime minister this week, faced with the challenge of fixing policy disarray and cutting a huge public debt that is constraining options for keeping economic recovery on track.\par \par \uc2 Kan, previously finance minister, takes over from Yukio Hatoyama, who quit last week after just eight months in office.\par \par \uc2 With euro zone tremors roiling markets worldwide, the focus is on Japan's debt burden and the government risks credit rating downgrades if it fails to get a grip on public finances.\par \par \uc2 Japan's 5-year sovereign credit default swap <JPGV5YUSAC=MP> spreads have risen 23.5 basis points over the past month to 94.2 basis points, the highest since March 2009. U.S. and UK spreads have widened only 5.3 basis points and 4.2 basis points. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\par Graphic on debt-GDP ratio:    \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/hag26k"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/hag26k}}}\uc2 \f2 \par  Graphic on CDS spreads:       \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/gag26k"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/gag26k}}}\uc2 \f2 \par  More stories on Japanese politics    [ID:nPOLJP]           \par  More stories on Japan's economy      [ID:nECONJP]          \par  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Following is a summary of key political risks to watch:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * FISCAL DILEMMA\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Successive Japanese governments have for years failed to deal with the country's growing debt. Kan's rise to prime minister has raised some hopes that Japan will do more to lower the debt burden, because he became a vocal proponent of fiscal discipline after becoming finance minister.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government plans to unveil a strategy this month to fix public finances, with a medium-term fiscal plan that includes binding caps on spending for the state budget over the next three years, plus a set of longer-term targets to bring the primary balance into surplus.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Kan has spoken in favour of raising the sales tax from its current 5 percent and has acknowledged the need for a credible fiscal plan. A sales tax hike is seen as crucial to cutting the fiscal deficit. Surveys show voters worried about the public debt are far less allergic to the move than in the past.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yet with mid-year elections to the upper house looming and the government's popularity still far below last year's levels despite a bounce in support following Hatoyama's resignation, many lawmakers in Kan's Democratic Party of Japan (DPJ) are wary of announcing plans for fiscal austerity or tax rises.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 What to watch:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- The new premier is likely to promote Deputy Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda, a fiscal conservative, to the post of finance minister, and appoint fiscal reformer Koichiro Genba as the Democratic Party's policy chief. Kan has also decided to move Yoshito Sengoku, another advocate of fiscal discipline, to the important post of chief cabinet secretary.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Comments from Kan, the new finance minister, the chief cabinet secretary and senior party officials will show whether the new cabinet plans to stick with its fiscal reform drive or whether it will temper its message to appease upper house lawmakers whose seats are up for election.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Any sign of delay in the medium-term fiscal plan and fiscal discipline targets expected this month could be a sign they measures will be diluted to seek consensus in the party.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Comments or downgrades from ratings agencies. Unless the government comes up with a credible plan, sovereign downgrades are a real possibility. "The risks are more for a downgrade than for the ratings agencies staying on hold," Masamichi Adachi, a senior economist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Tokyo, said last week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW LEADER, ELECTION OUTLOOK\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The selection of Kan as prime minister has improved the DPJ's chances in an upper house poll expected next month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Policy disarray under Hatoyama, who quit abruptly last week, had sent government and party support rates plummeting, but weekend media surveys showed a rise in the percentage of those planning to vote for the ruling party.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The DPJ swept to power in a historic election last year and will run the government whatever the outcome of the July upper house poll, but the ruling bloc needs to win a majority in that chamber to ensure that legislation is enacted smoothly.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the DPJ may still fall short of a majority even with the help of their tiny coalition partner, the People's New Party.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In that case, the DPJ is likely to seek new partners, such as the Buddhist-backed New Komeito Party or the tiny reformist Your Party, but this could lead to more messy coalition politics and distract the DPJ from urgent matters such as cutting public debt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some DPJ lawmakers feel spending cuts and tax hikes are rarely vote winners, so the more insecure the government feels, the more it may shy away from prescribing tough fiscal medicine.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 What to watch:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Opinion polls in the run up to the upper house election will provide clues on whether the ruling coalition can maintain their majority in the chamber and avoid policy deadlock.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Kan's support rates and the election outcome could also determine whether he can stay in the job after the election, although analysts expect him to hold on and run again in a party leadership vote in September unless the DPJ loses big.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- The actions of party powerbroker Ichiro Ozawa, who resigned as the DPJ's No.2 executive when Hatoyama quit, should also be watched as his manoeuvring could weaken Kan. Ozawa opposes bold steps to tackle Japan's tattered finances.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * POLICY TENSION WITH \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The government has put pressure on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to take bolder steps to overcome deflation, and the bank responded with additional easing steps, often even as it upgraded its economic assessment. Government pressure has subsided after the bank eased monetary policy in several stages, and Kan has said the government and the Bank of Japan are on the same page.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But some ruling party lawmakers are also demanding the BOJ adopt formal inflation-targeting to strengthen its commitment to end deflation. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has said policy under such a scheme could be swayed by short-term price fluctuations and overlook other important factors such as asset prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In May, the BOJ lifted its outlook for the economy, citing improving domestic demand. It offered additional help for the recovery by announcing a loan scheme targeting growth industries and indicating it may be prepared to accept a broader range of collateral for BOJ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . [ID:nSGE64K0AS]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some analysts say the loan scheme was a pre-emptive strike to relieve pressure from the government for more action to support the economy, and even for it to buy more government bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Though the BOJ can set its policy independently, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also is required to work closely with the government to align policy and has in the past caved in to government pressure.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 What to watch:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- BOJ policy board meeting on June 14-15.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- The Democratic Party is drafting a manifesto for the upper house election and a group of junior lawmakers wants strong language on the BOJ. Few expect their demands to be entirely adopted, but attention will be paid to final wording.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * YEN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ?\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Incoming Prime Minister Kan is widely regarded by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders as favouring a weaker yen, and his recent comments have done nothing to change that impression. Last month, as euro zone turbulence pushed the yen higher, he said: "A close watch is needed so that yen rises do not become excessive."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Junior lawmakers in the DPJ have also called for a policy of pushing the yen down towards 120 per dollar -- a level it hasn't traded at since 2007. Comments like these have kept \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders wary that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 What to watch:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Comments by government officials about sudden \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moves and possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Picking a level that would trigger \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is tricky. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if the yen surged beyond its 14-year high of 84.82 per dollar set in November, some analysts say. But \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could be difficult given Group of Seven countries were willing to stand by as the euro plunged due to Greece's debt crisis. The G7 is encouraging flexibility in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates, particularly in China, and that also makes it difficult for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to justify \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- A more likely way of countering a surge in yen strength could be for the government to pressure the Bank of Japan into taking more easing steps, as it did in December after the yen hit a 14-year high.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Compiled by Andrew Marshall)\f28 \par \par \uc2 RISKS-JAPAN/ (FACTBOX)|LANGEN|G|RBN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|J|RNP|DNP|PGE   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100607e6670005m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
