\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Japan Shouldn't Bank On G20 Help On EUR -Analyst\b0\par\par\uc2 1,644 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 2010\par 04:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0222 GMT [Dow Jones] Despite Japan Finance Minister Kan saying G20 early next month likely to discuss, among key topics, impact on EUR from euro zone's lingering troubles, Japan's exporters suffering from EUR/JPY's recent slide shouldn't bank on forum yielding much help, says senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at major foreign bank in Japan. "The G20 has previously committed to decrease global imbalances and to avoid \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , so it would be difficult for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to push a position that the G20 needs to stop euro weakness and yen strength." Says another impediment to any G20 support for EUR, which would buoy EUR/JPY, is that diversity of views in forum makes it more difficult to come to consensus on thorny issues such as possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Says smaller G7 would still be better forum for such moves. EUR/JPY last 112.34; cross hit 108.83 on Tuesday, lowest since November 2001. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0217 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei ends morning session +1.7% at 9805.46 due to overnight gains in U.S. and European stock markets; "The Nikkei will likely hover around 9800 today, but could rise to 9860 (depending on other stock markets' move in Asia)," says Investrust CEO Hiroyuki Fukunaga. Adds, concerns about European debt and escalated geopolitical risk on Korean peninsula persist and will likely continue weighing on index. All of 33 Topix subsectors in positive territory, led by mining & oil sector's gain on higher crude price. \uc2 Inpex Corp\uc2 . (1605.TO) +4.9% at Y574,000 and \uc2 Showa Shell\uc2  (5002.TO) +3.4% at Y643. Insurance plays also higher after strong earnings results of major life insurers came out yesterday; \uc2 Tokio Marine Holdings\uc2  (8766.TO) +3.2% at Y2,547. Stronger USD, EUR vs JPY also helping exporters extend gains with \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  (7203.TO) +1.8% at Y3,335, \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) +2.9% at Y2,869. (atsuko.fukase@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0211 GMT [Dow Jones] LME base metals mixed in morning Asian trade; most tad down, but holding close to yesterday's strong close. Early rises in copper reversed around Shanghai open as arbitrage came into play, says Wang Hao at Standard Bank in Hong Kong. SHFE/LME copper ratio driven from 7.9 overnight back above 8 by investors selling LME and buying SHFE, reverse of more common long-LME trade. SHFE September copper +1.9% at 0120 GMT at CNY55,810, LME 3-month down $7 at $6,977. "It's a little bit quiet now. I think people are watching the euro," which down slightly at $1.2315 after overnight rise, he says. Lead, zinc both up, nickel biggest decliner, down $150 at $21,650 with bare 7 lots traded. (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0204 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares open up tracking gains from Wall Street's rebound overnight, also after China government's denial of selling European bond holdings help boost confidence in global economic recovery. Shanghai Composite Index +0.7% at 2674.43, faces resistance at psychologically-important 2700 level. "We still need to see if volumes can hold up to sustain gains, it's still unclear if the market has broken out of its retreat-and-rally pattern," says Chen Huiqin from Huatai Securities. Oil, coal stocks leading gains due to rise in oil prices with \uc2 China Petroleum & Chemical Corp\uc2 . (600028.SH) +0.8% at CNY8.95, \uc2 China Shenhua Energy Co\uc2 . (601088.SH) +1.1% at CNY24.68. Shenzhen Composite Index +0.8% at 1068.68. (esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0203 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds decline moderately on negative cues from U.S. Treasurys and as Taiex +1.5%, but falls likely limited as no signs of any tightening by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says local bank trader. "Normalization of interest rates will take a long time"; adds \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monthly auctions of 1-year NCD/CDs also don't have large impact because market awash with liquidity. Tips bond yields to consolidate until \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's next quarterly policy meeting in late June unless stocks plunge to new lows. Yield on reopened 10-year bond up at 1.3817% vs 1.3699% in when-issued trade, tipped in 1.375%-1.385% band today, likely to stay in 1.350%-1.400% band next week; 5-year yield up at 0.9920% vs 0.9807%, tipped in 0.950%-0.985% band. (alex.pevzner@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0202 GMT [Dow Jones] Sucden Financial says Chinese physical demand for metals has been reasonably active in past few days at lower prices. Tips more gains today; "SHFE could see short covering especially ahead of the LME and NY markets bank holiday on Monday. Similarly, it would be reasonable to anticipate month-end and pre long weekend strength on the LME," says broker. Metals so far mixed with LME 3-month copper at $6,967/ton, down $17 with 643 lots done. SHFE September copper at CNY55,810/ton, up CNY1,020, catching up with overnight LME gains. Arbitrage not in play with prices close to par. LME 3-month zinc at $1,949/ton, up $9 with 369 lots done. Triland says zinc still vulnerable with market in surplus; "The $1,900 level is finding it hard to convince as a support as physical buying (Thursday) was still shy despite signs of recovery." (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0158 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY lower on EUR rebound overnight in light trading so far; pair at 6.8296 vs 6.8315 overnight, tipped at 6.8280-6.8320 for session. 1-year offshore USD/CNY NDFs down at 6.7250/6.7320 vs 6.7480/6.7580 late yesterday. "Global risk appetites improved somewhat, driving major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise against the dollar," says Shanghai-based trader at foreign bank. Adds USD selling in offshore NDFs stemmed from overseas hedge funds; tips 1-year NDFs support at 6.7000 near term. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0152 GMT [Dow Jones] May HSI futures +1.6% at 19,715, as cash market expected to rebound for 3rd straight day. Ahead of contract's expiry today, most rollover likely completed with May open interest decreasing 20,468 lots to 32,570. June HSI futures end yesterday at 45-point discount to cash, still pointing to negative near-term bias despite market rebound. June open interest at 69,790 lots. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0149 GMT [Dow Jones] Direction of China's already-sky high property prices remains open question and government keeps it a guessing game. Despite tough-looking property-tightening measures unveiled since mid-April, top leadership vague about where it wants prices to be. Shrugging off the measures, China's April property prices +12.8% on-year, but on dramatically shrinking transaction volumes, showing home buyers, property developers in standoff, not only because neither side wants to back down but also because both equally clueless about future. If Beijing genuinely wants to drive down home prices, boosting land supply an obvious solution, but while more land released in recent weeks, amount negligible. Government's response shows ultimate policy dilemma, conflict of interest: Beijing torn between competing views of real estate as growth engine and source of inflation. And as single biggest beneficiary of land sales, government has virtually no incentive to depress prices. So keep guessing. See: =WSJ BLOG/China Real Time Report: Property Price Guessing Game (hong.shen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0145 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD falls to 31.990 vs 32.187 late yesterday tracking broadly lower USD vs Asians, gains in local shares (Taiex +1.5%), says foreign bank trader. Foreign investors selling as offshore NDF discount widening also weigh on pair. Tips pair in 31.950-32.050 band rest of morning. "I think markets in Asia are likely to be more stable today, as the Dow Jones Industrial Average's sharp rebound overnight has helped investors regain some confidence," she says. Tips more exporters selling above 32.000, with some importers buying greenback below 32.000 helping to support pair. (charmian.kok@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0145 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW recoups losses, last at 1,203.4 vs 1,199.6 earlier, 1,224.0 late yesterday in Seoul as BOK suspected of buying near 1,200, according to 3 Seoul based traders. But think pair's downward momentum remains intact due to strong local shares (Kospi last +0.9%); "the test of KRW1,200 support failed (earlier) but the pair may give it another shot," says local bank trader. Foreign bank trader tips pair in tight 1,200-1,210 range for now. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0138 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead Kospi200 June futures last +0.3% at 210.55, off early 212.10 high on heavy futures selling by local retail investors. "They are selling on the gap-up open today", deteriorating market basis sharply to minus 0.73 vs theoretical basis of 0.20; this increases program selling, weighing on spot market. "Although sentiment has stabilized somewhat, market volatility hasn't fallen much yet as fundamental problems in Europe remain unsolved. That's why people are urged to lock in profits when there's a rebound," says Kang Song-chol at \uc2 Eugene Investment & Securities\uc2 ; but adds "I don't worry too much about program selling. Foreigners resumed stock buying today and domestic institutions are actually buying stocks again today if you exclude program selling (from their accounts)." Lead Kospi200 resistance pegged around 212.50. (soo-kyung.seo@dowjones.com) [ 05-27-10 2222ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100528e65s0001k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
