\par\par \b \uc2 CHRONOLOGY-History of  \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 2,038 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Mai 2010\par 11:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 LONDON, May 24 (Reuters) - The following is a chronology of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by major central banks on the dollar, yen, mark and euro.\par \par \uc2 For an analysis of prospects of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  following the euro's recent rapid fall, see [ID:nLDE64K12A]\par \par \uc2 May 2010 - The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to weaken the franc as the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hits all-time high beyond 1.40 per euro. Traders say it has bought 17 billion euros on May 19. They cite regular SNB activity since April.\par \par \uc2 April 2010 - The SNB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to weaken the franc against the euro. Preliminary government data shows Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose 28.7 billion francs ($24.83 billion) to 153.6 billion in April, when the SNB added to its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  war chest to hold the euro around 1.43 francs.\par \par \uc2 Dec 2009 - Mar 2010 - Traders cited SNB activity on Dec. 21, Jan. 29, Feb. 12, Feb. 23 and March 2 to defend a level of 1.46 per euro.\par \par \uc2 March 12, 2009 - The SNB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the franc for the first time since August 1995 after the franc hits all-time low of 1.4576 per euro. Such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  drove the franc to 1.5450 that month. Quarterly figures indicate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent some 4 billion euros worth of francs.\par \par \uc2 June 2007 - New Zealand \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time since the NZ dollar was floated in March 1985 after demand for the higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  drives it to a 22-year high near $0.7640. a level which the bank said was exceptional and unjustified in terms of the economic fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 March 2004 - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sells 4.7026 trillion yen in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March. In Q1, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold 14.8315 trillion yen to stem the yen's rise, stepping into the market for 47 days between Jan 1 and March 31. March marked the end of Japan's 15-month campaign to curb the yen's rise. It spent 35 trillion yen, or more than $300 billion.\par \par \uc2 Feb 2004 - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues with heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , spending 3.3420 trillion yen to curb yen rise. The dollar hit a 3-year low of 105.14 yen before staging a 4.5 percent recovery.\par \par \uc2 Jan 30, 2004 - Japan sells a record 7.1545 trillion yen between Dec 27 and Jan 28. The dollar fell to a 3-year low around 105.45 yen on Jan 27. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  also sold 5.014 trillion yen of foreign bonds to the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds through a repo deal under which it would buy the paper back at a later date.\par \par \uc2 Dec 2003 - Japan sells 2.2519 trillion yen between November 29 and December 26, bringing \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to record 20 trillion in 2003. The MOF spelled out in its draft 2004/05 budget that it would raise its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ceiling to 140 trillion yen from 79 trillion. Until the budget was passed the ministry asked the BOJ to provide short-term funds by buying foreign bonds from its \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  account under a repurchase agreement.\par \par \uc2 Nov 2003 - Japan sells 1.5996 trillion yen between Oct 30 and Nov 26, bringing \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to around 17.8 trillion yen in the year. In this period the dollar fell to a three-year low of 107.52 yen.\par \par \uc2 Oct 2003 - Japan sells 2.723 trillion yen between Sept 27 and Oct 29, bringing \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a new annual high of 16.2 trillion yen. On Sept 30, the MOF confirmed it sold yen for dollars, acting through the New York Federal Reserve. This broke the MOF's recent silence on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Sept 2003 - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sells 4.4573 trillion yen, a monthly record, in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in September after refraining from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in August. Analysts believe virtually all the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was carried out before Sept 20, when the Group of Seven industrial countries issued a statement calling for flexibility in exchange rates.\par \par \uc2 July 2003 - Japan sells 2.0272 trillion yen in July to prevent the yen from appreciating sharply. The July figure lifts the total yen selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  volume to a new annual high of around 9 trillion yen. The previous high was 7.6411 trillion yen hit in 1991.\par \par \uc2 June 2003 - Japan sells 628.9 billion yen in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in the May 29-June 26 period to stem the yen's rise. The figure was substantially less than the previous month.\par \par \uc2 June 2003 - Japan's official reserves rise to record levels at the end of May, largely due to the biggest-ever monthly volume of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- nearly four trillion yen ($34 billion), the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 May 19, 2003 - Dealers in Tokyo say Japanese monetary authorities bought dollars for yen at around 115.10. In European midsession, dollar climbs more than a full yen from 115.25 in a short space of time. Japan declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 May 15, 2003 - Dealers in London and New York say Japanese monetary authorities sold yen for dollars when the yen rose to a 10-month high of 115.33 in early New York trade. Japanese monetary authorities declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 May 13, 2003 - Japan is seen selling yen after the dollar falls as low as 116.36 yen in late Tokyo trade. There was no confirmation from the Japanese authorities.\par \par \uc2 Jan-March, 2003 - Data shows Japanese authorities spent 2.5 trillion yen on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from January to March.\par \par \uc2 Feb 28, 2003 - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Ministry confirms it has conducted solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a second straight month, buying dollars and euros worth about 513 billion yen. It said it had asked the BOJ to step into the market several times in late February and that euro-buying had been "far less" than dollar-buying versus the yen.\par \par \uc2 Mid-Jan, 2003 - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducts solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  under newly-appointed top financial diplomat Zembei Mizoguchi, spending a little less than 700 billion yen days after the yen rose to a four-month high around 117.40 against the dollar. The MOF confirmed the action later in January.\par \par \uc2 June 28, 2002 - BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen, and U.S. Federal Reserve and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also sell yen on BOJ's behalf early in New York trading. ECB confirms it buys euros for yen. Traders see sharp euro move from around 118.10 to highs above 119. Dealers also see Fed buying dollars for yen from around 119, lifting the dollar to highs around 120.35. BOJ data released in July showed Japan spent about 520.5 billion yen to weaken the yen on this day alone.\par \par \uc2 June 26, 2002 - Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen, lifting the dollar to 121.35 from around 120.20-30. Second bout takes the dollar to around 121.10 from 120.50/60. Third bout in early European session takes dollar to 120.70 from 120.03. BOJ is estimated to have bought $18.56 billion in its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in June, including May 31.\par \par \uc2 June 24, 2002 - BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen in Tokyo trading, lifting the dollar to around 122.80 from 121.10/15 as the greenback fell to a seven-month low below 121 in the previous session.\par \par \uc2 June 4, 2002 - BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen, lifting the dollar a full yen from 123.35 yen.\par \par \uc2 May 31, 2002 - BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen for dollars as the dollar falls to around 123 yen, after it dropped to a six-month low below 123 in the previous session. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  takes it back to 124.45/50 before another slide to 124 and another round of BOJ yen-selling. Third bout takes it from around 123.75 to above 124.\par \par \uc2 May 23, 2002 - BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  for second straight day, seen selling yen for dollars at 123.90/95.\par \par \uc2 May 22, 2002 - BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen for dollars, after the dollar fell to a 5-1/2 month low of 123.50 on doubts about the speed of the U.S. economic recovery. BOJ seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 123.80/90 yen per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Sept 24/26/27/28, 2001 - BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen for dollars, worrying about an export-crippling rise in the value of the yen and following attacks on U.S. cities on September 11. Sept 24/26/27 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not limited to the dollar but includes buying euros for yen, with European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) and euro zone central banks operating on behalf of BOJ. On Sept 27 \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  says the New York Federal Reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its behalf for the first time in the September round.\par \par \uc2 Sept 17, 19, 21, 2001 - BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen for dollars.\par \par \uc2 Nov 10, 2000 - ECB in conjunction with national euro zone central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy euros.\par \par \uc2 Nov 6, 2000 - ECB and other euro zone national central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy euros.\par \par \uc2 Nov 3, 2000 - ECB and other euro zone national central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at least twice to buy euros, following the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 5 percent recovery from record lows set the previous week around $0.8225.\par \par \uc2 Sept 22, 2000 - Central banks in Europe, Japan and the United States, acting together for first time since 1995, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to drive euro higher after \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hits all-time low below 85 cents two days earlier, a loss of nearly 30 percent of its value since its January 1999 launch. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is first by ECB since its birth.\par \par \uc2 Jan 1999-April 2000 - Japan concerned too-strong yen, trading around 108 to the dollar in January, 1999, will choke off fragile economic recovery and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to tame its strength. BOJ sells yen at least 18 times in this period, including once via the Federal Reserve and once via the ECB. Despite \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , yen continues to strengthen against dollar, reaching 102 by April 2000.\par \par \uc2 April-Jun 1998 - With yen weakening, BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . As yen crumples below 144 to the dollar, U.S. authorities on June 17, 1998 join the BOJ, spending $833 million buying yen.\par \par \uc2 Apr 1994-Aug 1995 - Dollar sinks to record lows against the German mark, reaching 1.41 in July 1995, and it hits post-World-War Two lows against the yen below 83 to the dollar in April 1995. From April 1994, United States \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly, often in concert with Japanese and individual European central banks, to prop up U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The last coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with European banks in this period takes place on Aug 15, 1995.\par \par \uc2 Apr-Aug 1993 - U.S. buys dollars and sells yen.\par \par \uc2 1991-1992 - U.S. and European central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly as U.S. economy tumbles into recession during Gulf War, which weakens the dollar. United States \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  on both sides of market, spending more than $2.5 billion buying \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and selling $750 million.\par \par \uc2 1988-1990 - Dollar trends higher. United States \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Group of Seven statements on importance of maintaining exchange rate stability.\par \par \uc2 Feb 1987 - Louvre Accord. Weak dollar, growing U.S. trade deficit and prospect of weakening U.S. economy raises concerns in Europe and Japan about further dollar weakening. Group of Five (G5) plus Italy meet at the Louvre in Paris and issue statement agreeing to "foster stability of exchange rates around current levels." United States, frequently in coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  a number of times to buy dollars.\par \par \uc2 Sept 1985 - Plaza Accord. G5 - U.S., Germany, Japan, Britain, France - meet at Plaza Hotel in New York to discuss concerns about very strong dollar and U.S. worries about declining competitiveness. Following weeks see substantial \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales of dollars for other G5 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  by U.S. and other monetary authorities.\par \par \uc2 1980-1981 - U.S. authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tame strengthened dollar.\par \par \uc2 1978-1979 - Dollar comes under heavy pressure amid high oil prices, high U.S. inflation and deteriorating balance of payments. In November 1978 a major new dollar support programme is introduced to raise a large war chest of up to $30 billion. U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  forcefully and often in the market with other central banks.\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (CHRONOLOGY)|LANGEN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100527e65o000ko\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
