\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: EUR/CHF Rises As Hedge Funds Cover Shorts-Dealer\b0\par\par\uc2 1,636 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Mai 2010\par 08:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0625 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CHF rises due to non-Japan hedge funds closing their EUR-shorts as investors cautious about possible SNB \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , says senior trader at major Tokyo bank. While rumored SNB's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may have spurred EUR/CHF's rapid gains overnight, "hedge funds aren't in a position where they want to try the downside for now," prompting them to cover shorts. Adds, although Eurogroup Chairman Juncker Thursday didn't indicate immediate need for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , "he voiced concerns about the euro's recent rapid declines, thus we cannot rule out that possibility." But in long-term, "the euro's trend is still downward" amid uncertainty over euro zone's debt issues. Says EUR/CHF may keep rising to 1.4600 in global day vs last 1.4476; EUR/USD may gain to 1.2700 vs last 1.2580.(miho.nakauchi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0623 GMT [Dow Jones] The substantial weakening of both SEK and \uc2 NOK\uc2  has continued, and Thursday broke through the 8.10-level in EUR/\uc2 NOK\uc2  and is reaching for 10.00 in EUR/SEK on the back of the decline in equity markets and lower commodity prices, says Danske Markets. "This morning EUR/\uc2 NOK\uc2  is heading for 8.20, while EUR/SEK has 'stabilized'." EUR/SEK currently trades at 9.9174, while EUR/\uc2 NOK\uc2  trades at 8.1637. (karl.bruze@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0619 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bond auction later today likely to draw strong demand, says trader with state-run bank. Cut-off yield for 7.02% 2016 bond likely at 7.37% (currently 7.3414%) as per median estimate of Dow Jones Newswires poll of 5 dealers. Cut-off yield for 8.20% 2022 bond likely at 7.64% (currently 7.6131%), cut-off yield for 8.26% 2027 bond likely 8.00% (currently 7.9980%), according to poll. "There will be enough buying for the auction to sail through," says dealer. RBI to hold INR130 billion government debt sale later today; \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will re-issue INR50 billion of 2016 bond, INR50 billion of 2022 bond and INR30 billion of 2027 bond. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0619 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude unlikely to stay above $70/bbl for long until European fiscal concerns ease, says Ben Westmore of \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2 ; even though euro continues to reverse recent falls against dollar on profit-taking in Asia trade Friday, long-term questions over Greek sovereign debt remain unsolved. "It's hard to see oil stay well above $70 before European issues are resolved," says Westmore; adds vote of German parliament scheduled Friday on nearly $1 trillion EU-IMF aid package for Greece could provide downside risks, if lawmakers surprise market by rejecting package. Nymex July crude down 42 cents at $70.38/bbl. (max.lin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0615 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares reverse earlier losses, due in part to expectations that Beijing will likely hold off on further tightening. Shanghai Composite Index +0.6% at 2571.83, off earlier low of 2481.97, expected to consolidate around 5-day moving average at 2573 rest of session. "We don't expect the tightening policy trend to reverse, but its pace has softened," says Dong Tao, economist at \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 , "this may also affect the timing of the launch of an interest rate hike or exchange rate revaluation." Concerns of negative impact on economy from recently multiple tightening policies have grown, amid European debt crisis. Xinjiang-based companies in lead gainers, after China unveils stimulus package to boost economy in the far-west and restive region: Xinjiang Talimu Agriculture Development (600359.SH) +6.1% at CNY16.16, Xinjiang Tianye (600075.SH) +10% daily limit at CNY11.68. Shenzhen Index +2.0% at 1003.88.(rose.yu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0615 GMT [Dow Jones] Isolating market impact from South Korea officially blaming North Korea for sinking South's warship Mar. 26 difficult due to turbulence from Europe, says ANZ. "We are not political experts but, at the risk of being cynical, we have seen this movie before. A provocative act by North Korea generates a flurry of diplomatic activity and there is an attempt to ramp up international pressure on the reclusive regime, including by appealing to China." Notes political options are limited; suspects that, "barring the unthinkable scenario, market interest in this matter will dissipate." (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0610 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR pares gains on hopes of more foreign fund inflows into local debt market after senior finance ministry official says government may raise limit on foreign investment in government bonds, says trader with private bank; pair last at 46.86 vs 47.25 earlier, 46.82 late Thursday. "INR47.25 was an important technical level would have been difficult to breach, so when the news came in, everybody switched positions and sold," says trader. Tips pair to stabilise at current levels. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0604 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets mostly lower, with China region's lone green patch on bargain buying in property stocks; Nikkei ends down 2.5%, S&P/ASX down 0.2%, STI off 2.1%, Shanghai Composite +0.1%, NZX-50 2.0% lower, KLCI down 1.6%, Taiex down 2.5%, Sensex down 1.1%. South Korea, HK markets closed for public holiday, financial markets in Thailand also closed due to state of emergency. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, JPY weaker vs USD, EUR after Japanese Finance Minister Naoto Kan says JPY's excessive strength not desirable; market takes as hint \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in market if yen strengthens too much. USD/JPY at 90.17 vs 89.52 in late NY Thursday, EUR/JPY at 113.50 vs 111.75. EUR/USD recovers some of recent losses, last at 1.2582 vs 1.2480. U.S. senators pass ambitious overhaul of U.S. financial markets; bipartisan group of 59 lawmakers voted in favor of wide-ranging bill that would affect everything from exotic derivatives products to consumers' use of debit cards. BOJ keeps overnight call rate unchanged at 0.1%; policy board unveils broad outline of bank lending support plan that includes offering loans to private banks for 1 year at 0.1%. BOJ also raises its assessment of broad economy, says domestic economy "starting to recover moderately." Credit card billings in NZ +1.9% on-year in April in seasonally adjusted terms to NZ$2.355 billion but 1.2% lower on-month. NZ April visitor arrivals down 1.8% on-month. Spot gold at $1,177.70/oz, down $5.30 vs NY close. June Nymex crude oil futures down 43 cents at $70.37/bbl. (philip.vahn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0601 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CHF slips off the highs reached in Asia, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market remains nervous with participants expected to adopt a 'wait and see' attitude, according to BayernLB. EUR/CHF climbed to around 1.4570 earlier Friday after rising sharply from below 1.42 late on Thursday. "The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  was certainly in the market yesterday, but haven't seen anything today," says Roland Raczek. "It's a very nervous market, and we have to get used to the euro at these levels," he adds. EUR/CHF is now at 1.4494. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0558 GMT [Dow Jones] Given the strong sell-off in the zloty, the PLN is now trading at fundamentally undervalued levels relative to long-term fair value levels, Danske says. Sees the zloty having the potential for making a recovery going forward. "Hence, if the zloty is trading above 4.21 against the euro, we recommend selling EUR/PLN," Danske adds. EUR/PLN trades at 4.1375, from 4.1836 late Thursday, USD/PLN at 3.2788 from 3.3880. (malgorzata.halaba@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0554 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end down 2.5% at 3.5-month low of 7237.71 (though off intraday low of 7164.21), weighed by sharp declines on Wall Street, regional markets; index fell every session this week. "The selloff is related less to (Taiwan's) fundamentals but rather associated with Europe where the fear is it's going to get worse before it gets any better," says KGI Securities head of Greater China sales trading Marc-Andre Gilbert. Says people fear European leaders "are running out of bullets" to save economy; notes uncertainty whether Europe as one economic block will even exist in one year. Gilbert adds many clients waiting to sell on rebound, might cap upside next week; Taiex will take cues from Europe, U.S. developments tonight. 7000 likely to support Monday but if euro-jitters linger, benchmark may fall to 6800 next week. Hon Hai Precision (2317.TW) ends down 2.2% to NT$131.0, AU Opto (2409.TW) off 1.2% to NT$30.05, Acer (2353.TW) falls 4.1% to NT$74.50; Cathay Financial (2882.TW) down 4.0% to nearly 9-month low of NT$46.60. (alex.pevzner@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0539 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei 225 June futures down 2.9% at 9750; index may find support at 9700 for rest of session as selling has eased as USD/JPY, EUR/JPY higher vs morning levels; but caution warranted as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market remains volatile, say analysts. "Since the pace of declines (in both cash and futures markets) is big, we need to be prepared for more weakness," says Hiroyuki Fukunaga, Investrust CEO, adding that if USD/JPY trades and stays below 90, futures could test 9500. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com) [ 05-21-10 0225ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100521e65l00043\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
