\par\par \b \uc2 2nd UPDATE: Japan Finance Minister Hint At Action Pushes Yen Down\b0\par\par\uc2 723 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Mai 2010\par 07:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds comments from Japan's finance minister about his discussions with Prime Minister Hatoyama on financial markets.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi and Takashi Mochizuki \par    OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's finance minister Friday hinted at possible government action to prevent any further rises in the yen, spooking investors into pushing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower in Asian trading.\par \par \uc2 "Markets in principle should determine \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, but I think we must closely watch (markets) and ensure that there won't be any excessive yen rises," Naoto Kan said at a news conference.\par \par \uc2 The yen touched a two-week high of Y88.95 against the dollar and an eight-and-a-half-year peak of Y109.47 against the euro Thursday amid Europe's escalating debt crisis. Kan declined to discuss the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's specific levels.\par \par \uc2 The finance minister's comments underscore how worried the Japanese government has become that a strong yen may derail the country's gradual economic recovery by damaging its most important growth engine: exports. Prime Minister Yukio Hatoyama held an impromptu meeting with Kan later in the day and told Kan to carefully monitor financial markets, although he didn't call for any specific countermeasures against Japan's sliding stock markets and soaring yen.\par \par \uc2 Japan's economy "is moving in the direction of recovery, but at the same time the euro's weakness is accelerating," Kan said at the news conference. "In general, it is desirable for exchange rates to stay stable at levels appropriate in terms of international trade, and it is undesirable for them to sharply stray from those levels."\par \par \uc2 The comments helped send dollar to an intraday high of Y90.37 from Y89.50 and the euro to Y114.40 from Y112.00 during the Asian session, partly "as short-term investors adjusted their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  portfolios," said Jun Kato, a senior dealer at Shinkin Asset Management.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely make more aggressive 'verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' if the Nikkei Stock Average falls to 9000 as the yen becomes stronger not only against the euro but also the dollar," said Masafumi Yamamoto, senior strategist at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Nikkei Stock Average stood at 9753.19 around 0540 GMT, down 2.8% from its closing level Thursday.\par \par \uc2 But doubts remain over whether Tokyo would act unilaterally to force the yen lower, a step that would invite criticism from the country's trading partners that it was unfairly aiding its exporters to the detriment of foreign competitors.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since 2004. But the remarks from Kan, who sets the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, reinforced speculation that officials from the Group of Seven industrialized nations may jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bring calm to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, some traders said.\par \par \uc2 Kan said finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs from the U.S., Japan, Germany, France, the U.K., Italy and Canada have no plans to hold any teleconferences "for now."\par \par \uc2 The yen's recent gains are mainly due to anxiety over the economic mayhem in Europe, sparked by Greece's fiscal problems and fueled by Germany's radical regulatory action. That has led investors to flee the euro into other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  deemed relatively safer.\par \par \uc2 A strong yen is a major source of concern for the Democratic Party of Japan-led government struggling to lift its tumbling public approval rate ahead of Upper House elections in July. Gains in the yen hurt Japanese exports by making them expensive in global markets, while they reduce the yen value of revenue earned through international trade.\par \par \uc2 Hatoyama has a "strong interest" in the stock and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, and he "told me to closely monitor" them, Kan said after meeting with the prime minister. "But the talks weren't about taking new action now."\par \par \uc2 If the need for countermeasures arise, Kan said he would first discuss it with Hatoyama.\par \par \uc2 Japan's economy grew an annualized 4.9% in the January-March period, marking the fourth straight quarter of gains and also the fastest pace of economic growth since April-June of 2009. But the solid expansion was in large part due to strong exports, particularly to Asia.\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2781; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com (Takeshi Takeuchi contributed to this article.) [ 21-05-10 0543GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100521e65l0009y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
