\par\par \b \uc2 3rd UPDATE:Eurogroup Juncker:No Need To Defend Euro Immediately\b0\par\par\uc2 643 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Mai 2010\par 15:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    (Adds analyst comment) \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi and Andrew Monahan \par    OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Eurogroup Chairman and Prime Minister of Luxembourg Jean-Claude Juncker voiced concern Thursday about the euro's recent rapid decline, but acknowledged some benefits from the weaker \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and said he sees little need for European policy-makers to act right away to defend it.\f28 \par \par \uc2 "I'm really concerned about the rapid (pace) of the fall of the exchange rate," he told reporters at the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  following a one-on-one meeting with Finance Minister Naoto Kan. But Juncker added that "I don't think that this is a matter (requiring) immediate action."\par \par \uc2 The comments may add to the view among some market participants that euro-zone authorities will tolerate a weaker euro so long as its falls are not abrupt. A weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  helps the region's export-driven economies at a time when austerity measures to cut debt threaten to crimp fragile recoveries.\par \par \uc2 Juncker told Dow Jones Newswires on the sidelines of an event Thursday evening that the euro's recent falls are "without any doubt helpful for the export-orientated part of the European economy".\par \par \uc2 "But this is not an argument to bring the euro further down," he added. "I'm not in favor of a weaker euro, nor in favor of a stronger euro."\par \par \uc2 Juncker's remarks came despite market speculation that European authorities may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets--possibly with their counterparts in the other Group of Seven nations--to prop up the euro, which has fallen nearly 15% this year largely due to Greece's debt problems. Market fears of official action helped the euro bounce back from a four-year low of $1.2143 on Wednesday. The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stood at $1.2320 at 1130 GMT.\par \par \uc2 Juncker declined to comment on the possibility of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although he said he had discussed the euro with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Kan during their talks.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said Juncker's remarks suggested that euro-zone authorities would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless its falls accelerate rapidly. The need to trim deficits while sustaining economic recovery means European leaders "should be welcoming the weak euro, although of course they can't explicitly mention that preference", said Masafumi Yamamoto, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  in Japan.\par \par \uc2 Juncker, however, didn't hide his frustration over the euro's recent falls, saying it was "largely due to irrational approaches of the financial markets".\par \par \uc2 He blamed market participants for "insufficiently" taking into consideration Europe's recent response to Greece's debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 Referring to a package extending nearly $1 trillion to fiscally strapped euro-members, notably Greece, Juncker told Dow Jones that the aggressive move was necessary to defend the euro.\par \par \uc2 Opposition has grown recently in richer European countries, mainly Germany, over the bailout, funded by the taxpayers of states with healthier finances. But "we have to explain to citizens that we have to support Greece because we are sharing with Greece the same \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Juncker said.\par \par \uc2 The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also moved to avert further deterioration in Europe's debt troubles. Last week, the ECB began buying sovereign bonds of euro-zone members on the secondary market, prompting some critics to accuse the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of caving in to political pressure.\par \par \uc2 But Juncker defended the ECB. Its decision "has been taken in full independence," he told Dow Jones. "I do not think the ECB could be criticized in any respect."\par \par \uc2 Commenting on financial regulations earlier in the day, Juncker said he didn't ask Kan for Japanese cooperation in regulatory action such as the banning of short-selling of certain products.\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2781; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com [ 20-05-10 1325GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100520e65k0016q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
