\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Lack Of Clarity Weighs On Sterling - BNY Mellon\b0\par\par\uc2 1,621 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 2010\par 04:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0239 GMT [Dow Jones] Looking at the UK election results in so far there appears to be a 4.4% swing towards the Conservatives which would fall slightly short of an outright majority, says Simon Derrick at BNY Mellon. Talk is now turning to whether PM Brown will push for a coalition with the Liberals, but this is by no means a done deal and even if it did occur the coalition may not be sufficient to command a majority in parliament. It's this lack of clarity that is weighing on sterling, says Derrick. GBP/USD currently trades at 1.4830, one cent off the day's high, while EUR/GBP is at 0.8547, from the day's low of 0.8445. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0236 GMT [Dow Jones] "No sense of panic selloff at all" at today's HK market open, says Anthony Lam at Emperor Securities; HSI last down 0.8% at 19,963.24 after opening down 2.0% on overseas cue. Some bargain-hunters stepping with HSI falling for 11 out of 13 sessions; Lam tips year-to-date low around 19,400 (hit mid-February) as short-term support. Some blue chips off intraday lows and rising; heavyweights \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (0939.HK), ICBC (1398.HK) both up a tad now; Swire Pacific (0019.HK) last +2.5% at HK$83.05 after earlier fall, as silver lining of calling off IPO perhaps is to avoid selling assets on cheap. Market volume heavy at HK$12.96 billion, suggesting strong bargain-hunting intersect. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0233 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds, bond futures moderately higher amid Kospi's 2.2% fall, but trade choppy as players remain cautious about possible selling of local bonds by foreign investors in wake of KRW's earlier slip to 3-month lows, traders say; 3-year yield down 4 bps at 3.75%, 5-year yield down 2 bps at 4.41%, June futures +5 ticks at 111.06. Bond trader at local securities firm says, "the bond market is responding negatively to the sharp rise in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate, and though the rate doesn't seem to be building on its gains for now, we'll have to see what happens in the afternoon." USD/KRW last up sharply at 1,158.9 vs 1,141.3 late yesterday in Seoul. Bond trader adds, "I think it'd be better to play defence now," and choose not to take big positions in bonds either way amid conflicting cues. Tips 3-year yield in wide 3.72%-3.86% range for rest of day. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0230 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 UBS\uc2  exits China onshore 1-to-5 year IRS steepener, says non-China factors (mainly jitters over European debt) likely to continue to drive up risk aversion, flatten curve; house entered trade at spread of 133 bps on April 21, stepped out at 114 bps yesterday. "We believe domestic (China) fundamentals still support a steepener. However, we expect external factors to continue to weigh on trade." Adds may re-enter trade when risk appetite recovers, "possibly after the release of China's April economic data next week." (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0229 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Malaysia's industrial output likely grew faster in March than in February on robust external demand; March industrial output likely +13.5% on year vs +4.9% in February, according to median forecast of 10 economists surveyed by Dow Jones. Economists say acceleration in industrial output in March due to strong demand for electronics; higher industrial output likely to translate to 1Q GDP growth of 8%-9% on-year, says Affin Investment Bank economist Alan Tan; industrial output due out 0401 GMT May 11. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0228 GMT [Dow Jones] 