\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Korea Bonds Up; Trade May Remain Choppy; KRW Eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 1,539 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 2010\par 04:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0233 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds, bond futures moderately higher amid Kospi's 2.2% fall, but trade choppy as players remain cautious about possible selling of local bonds by foreign investors in wake of KRW's earlier slip to 3-month lows, traders say; 3-year yield down 4 bps at 3.75%, 5-year yield down 2 bps at 4.41%, June futures +5 ticks at 111.06. Bond trader at local securities firm says, "the bond market is responding negatively to the sharp rise in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate, and though the rate doesn't seem to be building on its gains for now, we'll have to see what happens in the afternoon." USD/KRW last up sharply at 1,158.9 vs 1,141.3 late yesterday in Seoul. Bond trader adds, "I think it'd be better to play defence now," and choose not to take big positions in bonds either way amid conflicting cues. Tips 3-year yield in wide 3.72%-3.86% range for rest of day. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0230 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 UBS\uc2  exits China onshore 1-to-5 year IRS steepener, says non-China factors (mainly jitters over European debt) likely to continue to drive up risk aversion, flatten curve; house entered trade at spread of 133 bps on April 21, stepped out at 114 bps yesterday. "We believe domestic (China) fundamentals still support a steepener. However, we expect external factors to continue to weigh on trade." Adds may re-enter trade when risk appetite recovers, "possibly after the release of China's April economic data next week." (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0229 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Malaysia's industrial output likely grew faster in March than in February on robust external demand; March industrial output likely +13.5% on year vs +4.9% in February, according to median forecast of 10 economists surveyed by Dow Jones. Economists say acceleration in industrial output in March due to strong demand for electronics; higher industrial output likely to translate to 1Q GDP growth of 8%-9% on-year, says Affin Investment Bank economist Alan Tan; industrial output due out 0401 GMT May 11. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0228 GMT [Dow Jones] Base metals could head even lower, especially copper, says senior metals trader at European bank in Singapore. "As far as copper goes, if it settles below $6,800, it could go to $6,000. The Chinese warehouses are full. The perception in the market is that the demand just isn't there." Arbitrage buying still in play this morning but spread has narrowed, currently just $83 vs $290 on Wednesday morning. Adds some tightness apparent in aluminum, nickel physical markets, but futures likely to follow copper lower. LME 3-month copper at $6,825/ton, down $122.75 since yesterday's kerb with 2,658 lots done so far. LME nickel at $21,355/ton, down $695 with 88 lots done. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0226 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan's Finance Minister Naoto Kan says G-7 finance ministers will hold conference call on Greece's debt issues later, but adds he doesn't think that G-7 officials will discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that G7 will jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy EUR. Kan also says stock, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels determined by market, suggesting \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unlikely for now. But traders say possibility cannot be completely ruled out t