\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Base Metals Likely Lower; China Demand Waning\b0\par\par\uc2 1,534 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 2010\par 04:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0228 GMT [Dow Jones] Base metals could head even lower, especially copper, says senior metals trader at European bank in Singapore. "As far as copper goes, if it settles below $6,800, it could go to $6,000. The Chinese warehouses are full. The perception in the market is that the demand just isn't there." Arbitrage buying still in play this morning but spread has narrowed, currently just $83 vs $290 on Wednesday morning. Adds some tightness apparent in aluminum, nickel physical markets, but futures likely to follow copper lower. LME 3-month copper at $6,825/ton, down $122.75 since yesterday's kerb with 2,658 lots done so far. LME nickel at $21,355/ton, down $695 with 88 lots done. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0226 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan's Finance Minister Naoto Kan says G-7 finance ministers will hold conference call on Greece's debt issues later, but adds he doesn't think that G-7 officials will discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that G7 will jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy EUR. Kan also says stock, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels determined by market, suggesting \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unlikely for now. But traders say possibility cannot be completely ruled out that Tokyo might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if risks intensify that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export-driven economy will fall into double-dip recession by sharp JPY's appreciation. Stronger JPY could also push Japan's prices down at home as import prices decline, postponing end of deflation. USD/JPY lasts at 91.90, sharply recovering from Thursday's low of 87.95. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei ends morning session down 3.7% at 10,295.63, off intraday low of 10,257.32; despite 2nd day of steep declines, traders not panicked on view that part of Wall Street's overnight plunge due to mistaken securities order. Support for USD/JPY (now 91.80) and EUR/JPY (now 116.14) in morning session also limiting Nikkei's downside, says Nikko Cordial senior strategist Tsuyoshi Kawata. Still, further Nikkei recovery may be limited in afternoon; there won't be much short-covering because traders awaiting U.S. payrolls data and German parliament's vote for rescue package for Greece, he says. "Overseas newsflow will continue to determine the Japanese market for now." All 33 Topix subindexes remain negative, with several down at least 4.0%; machinery, electronics makers, shippers, and traders particularly weak. \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) down 4.1% at Y3,030. Honda (7267.TO) down 4.1% at Y2,984, \uc2 Nippon Yusen\uc2  (9101.TO) down 5.2% at Y345, \uc2 Nissan Motor\uc2  (7201.TO) off 5.4% at Y739. Nintendo (7974.OK) down 10% at Y27,520. \uc2 Fast Retailing\uc2  (9983.TO) off 5.1% at Y13,160. (juro.osawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0215 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY up as USD rose further against EUR overnight in international markets. Pair at 6.8274 vs 6.8266 late yesterday, after central parity set at 6.8271 vs 6.8269 yesterday. Offshore market moving much faster, with 1-year NDF up at 6.7650/6.7750 from 6.6830/6.6890 late yesterday. "Investors are cutting losses in the offshore market as we sense something similar to financial crisis now," says Guangzhou-based foreign bank trader in reference to investors who had bet on CNY rise. Adds, offshore products may rise further, but onshore dollar have limited upside as central parity steady. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0212 GMT [Dow Jones] The enthusiasm of the UK markets following the exit poll is definitely waning, says Simon Penn, market analyst at \uc2 UBS\uc2 . "Gilts, having been as much as 43 ticks higher on an adjusted basis, but are now actually down 10 ticks (adjustment made for international market gains post European settlement). Cable doing exactly the same, now a net 20 pips lower from the close of the polls and almost a whole point below the exit poll highs," says Penn. The issue is while the Conservatives are making gains against Labour, as expected, they are not making big enough gains against the LibDems, he says. A hung parliament, with the Conservatives as the largest party is the firm favorite result. June gilts stands at 117.82, and cable at $1.4849. (peter.nurse@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0210 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold's 2.8% ascent yesterday to beyond $1,200 shows strength of bull market, with NY close of $1,208.80 near session high of $1,212.60 positive as gold held on to gains even after losses in stocks pared, says Phillip Futures; adds gold proving "a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  alternative as investors lose faith in fiat \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." However, some profit taking in gold today; "our near term support level for gold is at $1,200. Should this level be breached, we will see a slight pullback and consolidation in prices before the upward trend continues." Spot gold at $1,201.10/oz, down $7.70. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0208 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP higher on risk aversion ahead of Monday's general polls while escalating worries over euro-zone debt crisis prompt safe-haven (USD) buying. Pair hits 2-month high at 45.78 vs 45.38 late Thursday. Still, upside likely limited for now as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected of selling to support PHP, traders say. "The central was there at the opening, first at PHP45.50, then PHP45.60, offers were also made at PHP45.78," says local bank trader. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have sold around $50-$100 million, traders say. Resistance eyed at 45.90, next support 45.50. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0205 GMT [Dow Jones] RBA clearly signals it has more policy tightening to do in medium-term as economy runs above trend, says RBA \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Robert Rennie. "Rates clearly need to go above average if you have a situation where inflation is at the top end of the band and growth is above trend." But adds, short-term direction for policy will be driven by risks offshore. "Short term swarms the medium-term outlook." (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0204 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore not considering immediate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  into \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stem the greenback's rally; MAS "will likely wait and see how the dollar behaves the rest of the day. There's been no \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by major Asia central banks. This is a flight to safety by investors worldwide and unless the U.S. dollar shoots up violently, the banks, will likely wait until the situation stabilizes before taking any decisions," says person familiar with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's thinking. USD/SGD above 1.4100 in early Asia, a two-month high, before stablizing around 1.455. (costas.paris@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0201 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares likely sharply lower, weighed by weak IDR, falls across global markets after stocks in U.S. tumble overnight on escalating concerns over debt crisis in Europe, traders say; tip support at 2700 after index ended down 1.3% yesterday at 2810.616. "The main index could fall by more than 3% but may recover later in session on bargain-buying on hopes many companies will give higher dividend in June," says trader at first Asia Capital. Car maker Astra (ASII.JK), Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK), heavyweight Telkom (TLKM.JK) may lead falls on foreign selling. Foreign funds yesterday net sellers of IDR860 billion worth of shares. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0201 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds up slightly amid rising appetite for less risky assets after steep falls on Wall Street overnight. Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index at 124.80 vs 124.79 last close; upside likely limited given concerns over further credit-curbing measures after Shanghai Securities News report that banks extended around CNY700 billion worth of new loans last month, up from CNY510.7 billion in March, says Shanghai-based analyst with local brokerage house. "If the reported loan figure is true, new yuan loans totalled CNY3.3 trillion in the first 4 months, or 44% of Beijing's full-year target of CNY7.5 trillion. The credit expansion pace is still too fast." (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0201 GMT [Dow Jones]USD/KRW off highs as exporters sell while euro coming off overnight lows helps support sentiment for risk sensitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders say. But expect overall upward bias in pair to remain amid broad risk aversion, Kospi's 2.8% fall. Foreign bank trader tips pair in 1,160-1,170 range rest of session; "offshore players are continuously picking up the dollar at lower levels." But adds hard to expect pair to rise beyond 1,170 level. "I think the market is somewhat shrugging off the (initial) panic we had," on euro-zone debt woes, sharp Wall Street losses. Pair still trading sharply higher at 1,163.9 vs 1,141.3 late yesterday in Seoul, but off earlier 1,169.5 high. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com) [ 05-06-10 2228ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100507e6570002j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
