\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Strength Threat For USD/JPY - DBS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,638 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2010\par 03:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0221 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's government likely to keep door open to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , as market "is still frustrated" that JPY is not weakening amid wider uptrend in Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , DBS says. Notes that government increased this its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  war chest for 2010/11, for first time in six years, move that follows additional monetary easing last week when Bank of Japan doubled amount of its fixed rate market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to Y20 trillion. "Japan is caught between the JPY as a safe haven against the Greek crisis in euro-zone, as well as a proxy for a lower USD against Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Hence, we think that it is the fear of more JPY appreciation from these twin factors that prompts \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to open the door on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ." USD/JPY last at 90.43 vs 90.50 late in NY trade Friday. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0221 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares likely tad lower, weighed by falls in several Asian markets after Wall Street's decline Friday, traders say; tip benchmark index support at 2730 after ending +0.2% Friday at 2742.974. "I think the market will consolidate today despite expectations of benign on-month inflation in March," says trader at Overseas Securities. March CPI data due April 1. Coal miner Bukit Asam (PTBA.JK), palm oil producer Astra Agro (AALI.JK) may lead falls on profit taking, lower commodity prices. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0217 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds up amid excess liquidity, solid demand from banks, who keen to buy low-risk fixed-income products for asset allocation purposes while regulators curbing their lending pace, says Shenyin Wanguo Securities trader; "bonds are expected to maintain positive bias, as the PBOC is likely to leave its official interest rates unchanged in the near term." Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index at 124.25 vs 124.21 last close, tipped to test 124.5 later this week. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0215 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  sees value in 2-to-5-year curve steepening trade in China short term, with target at 100 bps from 91 bps currently. Says liquidity in banking system remains ample which will continue to weigh on short-tenor rates. Adds window guidance on loan growth will likely lead to more liquidity into banking system. But notes slowing growth in M2, PBOC possible rate hikes in medium term could pose flattening risk. House forecasts M2 to slow to 17% for 2010; Expects PBOC rate hikes to start in 2Q. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0214 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares may fall on lower commodity prices, profit taking after 7.8% rise over past 8 sessions. Benchmark index likely to fluctuate around last close as players eye how regional bourses respond to India's interest rate hike, says KT Zmico Securities first VP Charoen Iamphattanatham. "Technically, the SET Index is approaching the overbought zone. But if the fundflow continues, further gains may push the benchmark to 800 in the near term." Resistance pegged at 780, support at 760. Foreign investors have been net buyers for 19 consecutive sessions; they have net bought THB30.55 billion worth of shares in month to date, THB28.49 billion year to date. SET Index ends up 2.1% at 774.59 Friday. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0211 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government marginally lower as some in market concerned Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may hike required reserves ratio in its quarterly policy meeting Thursday (no benchmark interest rate hike expected), says foreign bank trader; but says "I don't expect an RRR hike - the impact is too broad, and the economy overall isn't strong enough." Adds focus also on 2Q bond issuance plan due tomorrow, expects total of NT$100 billion-NT$120 billion new supply in 2Q. Yield on benchmark 10-year bond edges up to 1.4333% vs 1.4293%, tipped in 1.43%-1.45% band today. (alex.pevzner@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0207 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares open higher with banks leading gains as concerns over credit tightening recede but traders say focus now on timing of yuan revaluation. "The index will likely to stay in a narrow range, as investors look for clues on (potential) yuan revaluation," says Xu Yinhui, analyst at Guotai Junan Securities. Shanghai Composite Index +0.4% at 3080.37, expected to test psychological resistance at 3100. Bank of China (601988.SH) +0.5% at CNY4.17, after lender says Saturday its shareholders approved its plan to issue up to CNY40 billion worth of bonds that are convertible into yuan-denominated A shares. ICBC (601398.SH) +0.4% at CNY4.88. Shenzhen Composite Index +0.2% at 1183.62.(esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0205 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares tad lower in thin trade, with benchmark PSEi off 0.2% to 3092.32 after closing 0.1% lower to 3097.23 Friday; market expected to consolidate with downward bias as investors prepare for IPOs ahead, four-day Easter weekend (starting April 1), says Erwin Balita, research head at SB Equities; "We're taking cues from the U.S. and until the Dow breaks out of the 10,800 level, we'll probably stay below the 3133 level. Besides we have upcoming IPOs and the Holy Week," he says; expected trading range 3080-3120. Leading market slide is port operator International Container Terminal (ICT.PH), down 1.1% to PHP21.75, while \uc2 Aboitiz Power\uc2  (AP.PH) +2.0% to PHP12.50 on prospects for electricity producers amid power shortage. Large IPOs usually sap liquidity from market, while investors historically lock in profit ahead of extended holidays during Easter; impending IPOs include that of budget carrier Cebu Pacific, property developer Globe Asiatique, which could raise a total of over PHP30 billion ($658 million). (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0201 GMT [Dow Jones] China, U.S. ratchet up tensions over Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but worries dispute may hamper global economy recovery if cross-border trade stymied could be overdone amid some signs both sides trying to avoid all-out trade war, with Chinese vice commerce minister visiting U.S. this week to try smooth differences. China's commerce minister Chen Deming Sunday cautioned U.S. against citing CNY as reason for imposing trade sanctions, saying Beijing "won't turn a blind eye;" comment may be effort to deter punitive action against China after some U.S. senators last week introduced legislation to require U.S. to slap tariffs, penalties on nations that fail to deal with misaligned \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; bill viewed as being aimed at China. Both sides also have incentive to keep the trade battle as just war of words as China among U.S.'s largest creditors, owning around $889 billion in U.S.'s foreign-held debt, also is increasingly one of the U.S.'s most significant trading partners. (terence.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0157 GMT [Dow Jones] China finalized February metal import data entirely in line with preliminary reports earlier this month, customs data show. China imported 220,530 tons of refined copper, 566,522 tons of copper concentrate in February; iron ore import data unchanged from preliminary data at 49.38 million tons, up 6% on January on unabated downstream demand. Refined copper imports +12% on-month due to lucrative arbitrage window between Shanghai Futures Exchange and London Metal Exchange early January, restocking before high-consumption season, analysts say. On-year decline of 18.6% unsurprising as China in February last year had just begun massive government stockpiling and enjoyed high \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates at cable- and wire-makers on economic stimulus. Copper imports likely to rise in months ahead as "copper restocked before the Lunar New Year holiday (has been) mostly consumed," says Wang Zhouyi, analyst with Shanghai Cifco Futures.(chuin-wei.yap@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0156 GMT [Dow Jones\} Korea bond market remains muted to Vice Finance Minister Hur Kyung-wook comments in NY Friday that Korea treasurys likely to be included in \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  World Bond Index within 2-3 months. Woori Investment & Securities analyst Park Jong-youn says "it's good news. But we've had similar talk for months now. So, for bonds to get a more significant boost, we'll need to see an official announcement saying that the inclusion has been decided." Analysts say Korea bonds' inclusion in WGBI to bring in some $10 billion in fresh funds into Korea. 3-year yield down 1 bp at 3.78%, 5-year yield off 2 bps at 4.30%, June futures +5 ticks at 110.92. USD/KRW higher at 1,136.2 vs 1,132.7 late Friday in Seoul.(in-soo.nam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0155 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI down 0.1% at 1294.96, weighed by mild selling in select blue chips, says dealer with local brokerage; tips index to trade in narrow range of 1290-1300 on absence of market moving news. "Thus far, the index is drifting within a two-point range as there are no incentives to buy or sell aggressively," says dealer; thinks investors could be waiting for Bank Negara's annual report (expected to be released 1000 GMT Wednesday), keeping close eye on revised official GDP forecast for this year. Volume thin with 36 million shares worth MYR42 million traded. Market breadth negative with losers leading gainers 125 to 89. Among losers, Bursa (1818.KU) down 1.2% at MYR7.60, Public Bank off 0.7% at MYR11.56, CIMB slipped 0.4% at MYR13.38, Genting (3182.KU) down 0.3% at MYR6.58.(elffie.chew@dowjones.com) [ 03-21-10 2221ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100322e63m0001t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
