\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-Pressure mounts on BOJ for policy loosening next week\b0\par\par\uc2 980 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2010\par 04:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Pressure mounts on BOJ to ease policy next week\par \par \uc2 * PM, finance minister say ready to act if yen moves sharp\par \par \uc2 * But they emphasise markets should set \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates\par \par \uc2 * BOJ to double 3-mth funding in policy review - report\par \par \uc2 * Yen may be real target of BOJ easing - analysts\par \par \uc2 By Leika Kihara\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 12 (Reuters) - Japan's prime minister said the government and the Bank of Japan should work together to beat deflation as he fended off mounting political pressure for action on the economy and the yen, raising expectations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will ease monetary policy next week.\par \par \uc2 The prime minister, finance minister and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor were grilled by lawmakers on Friday on what they intend to do to defeat deflation and prevent yen strength from harming an economy struggling to recover from the global downturn.\par \par \uc2 Although the economy is growing slowly, weak domestic demand is contributing to deflation, which many policymakers fear could push Japan back into a damaging downturn ahead of upper house elections expected in July.\par \par \uc2 The yen rose last week to a three-month high against the dollar and speculators are gearing up for a yen rally, raising concerns that exports could take a hit and deflation deepen.\par \par \uc2 Both Prime Minister Yukio Hatoyama and Finance Minister Naoto Kan said the government was ready to act if the yen moved excessively.\par \par \uc2 But their comments were seen as adding pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ease policy further, rather than as a call for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that would set the government up for a potentially costly fight with financial markets and put Japan at odds with G7 efforts to promote market-based exchange rates.\par \par \uc2 "The comments from the government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor lift market expectations that there will be some kind of easing steps by the BOJ next week," said Hideki Hayashi, global economist at Mizuho Securities in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 "It is the market consensus that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan interventing\b0 \highlight\uc2  solo in the market won't be effective, so the government will have to depend on the BOJ's further easing policy," said Hayashi.\par \par \uc2 "The BOJ's expected loosening of policy will push down yen interest rates further, thereby supporting dollar/yen at the 90 yen level," he said.\par \par \uc2 Sources told Reuters this week that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was considering expanding a three-month lending facility it introduced in December following an emergency meeting, either by extending the period or increasing the pool of funds available.\par \par \uc2 But they also said board members were split, with some worried a further easing in policy was not economically justified, especially as the economy was developing along the lines of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's official expectations.\par \par \uc2 "The decision will be made based on discussions among the seven board members," BOJ Governor Masaaki Shirakawa told the upper house budget committee, referring to those on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy setting board.\par \par \uc2 "We hope to discuss what we can do in light of the goals stipulated in the BOJ Law," he said.\par \par \uc2 The Nikkei newspaper said the BOJ would double its 10 trillion yen ($110.4 billion) in funds offered under the lending \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a policy review on Tuesday and Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 IS YEN REAL TARGET OF POLICY TIGHTENING?\par \par \uc2 Neither the BOJ nor the government have made an explicit link between \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts to loosen policy and the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 But the strength of the yen against the dollar during the global downturn prompted complaints from industry that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's level was harming their export prospects.\par \par \uc2 With the government's ability to support the economy shackled by a debt load approaching 200 percent of GDP and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's interest rates already at rock-bottom levels, some analysts argue that reducing the yen's strength may be the real target of policy loosening.\par \par \uc2 The December policy easing helped pull the yen back from a 14-year high of 84.82 per dollar, raising market speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be a factor in BOJ thinking now.\par \par \uc2 The yen rose last week to a three-month high and market data shows speculators are building long positions in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It was trading around 90 per dollar on Friday.\par \par \uc2 In answer to lawmaker concerns over the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Finance Minister Naoto Kan underlined that sharp yen moves were not desirable by saying that authorities could use \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements were excessive.\par \par \uc2 However, he emphasised that markets should set \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates.\par \par \uc2 "Basically it's desirable for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates to move stably," Kan said. "I'm aware that the government has the option of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves are rapid. But as long as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves are stable, it's basically up to the market to determine (levels)."\par \par \uc2 Shirakawa said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's loose policy was helping to curb the yen's strength, while Prime Minister Yukio Hatoyama also said steps could be taken.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  should basically be determined appropriately by the market," he said.\par \par \uc2 "But there was the sovereign debt risk, the Dubai shock and the Lehman shock ... There seem to be yen rises that don't reflect the strength of the Japanese economy and industry," he said.\par \par \uc2 "Against such yen rises, we need to take resolute action ... Although we can't return to a fixed rate system, there needs to be global cooperation on this front."\par \par \uc2 Analysts said his comments didn't suggest any action was planned or imminent, but rather that he was playing to his domestic political audience.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities have not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets since spending 35 trillion yen over a 15 month period up to March 2004. ($1=90.59 Yen) (Additional reporting by Tetsushi Kajimoto; Writing by Neil Fullick; Editing by Hugh Lawson)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/ (WRAPUP 1)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100312e63c00054\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
