\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Focus: Bank of Japan Prepares For The Inevitable\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicholas Hastings   \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN   \par \uc2 798 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2010\par 09:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--The Bank of Japan is wise to prepare for battle.\par \par \uc2 The increase in its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  war-chest by Y5 trillion to Y145 trillion--the first increase in six years--may yet be needed to halt the yen's relentless rise.\par \par \uc2 For all practical purposes, the yen shouldn't be so strong.\par \par \uc2 Weak economic fundamentals in Japan, where deflation remains a threat to recovery, suggests that the dollar should be trading well above Y100 rather than heading back under Y88 as it is now.\par \par \uc2 See the dollar's recent slide against the yen:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/3580"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/3580}}}\par \par \uc2 In fact, a dollar down at this level against the yen is the last thing Japan needs as it struggles to gain a competitive foothold against the somewhat more robust economies of China and South Korea.\par \par \uc2 The problem is that in this topsy-turvy world where confidence in the global recovery remains very fragile and where the threat of a sovereign default in Europe remains a real threat, investors are still looking at the yen as a better bet.\par \par \uc2 And there is little indication that this sentiment will change just now.\par \par \uc2 Initial optimism that growth in the major economies would be enough to bring early increases in interest rates, a trend that the Bank of Japan would hardly be able to follow just yet, have faded fast as economic data have failed to live up to expectations.\par \par \uc2 A harsh winter in the northern hemisphere has only made matters worse. Instead of U.S. bond yields continuing to rise with hopes of an early rise in U.S. rates, they have come under pressure in recent days, making the dollar even less attractive against the yen.\par \par \uc2 Greece's debt problems certainly aren't helping matters either. Although the country has produced the austerity package financial markets were looking for, there is no reassurance that the package will be implemented in full or that the measures are enough to convince Germany and France to come to the country's aid.\par \par \uc2 With the risk of a credit downgrade remaining and the threat of contagion to other debtors not going away, investors are expected to continue shying away from risky assets for now.\par \par \uc2 Japanese investors, meanwhile, are only making things more difficult with their annual end-year repatriation of funds. New weekly portfolio data showed another net inflow of Y717.2 billion, providing extra fuel for the yen's advance.\par \par \uc2 So the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's preparations not only signal to the market that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains an option, but also that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is fully prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it has to--for the first time since 2005--given that market conditions are hardly likely to change.\par \par \uc2 Early Friday in Europe, the yen was a little lower after reports in Japanese newspapers that the Bank of Japan could take additional steps to ease monetary policy by expanding the volume of loans at a fixed rate.\par \par \uc2 However, any such move is seen as having a limited impact and the yen soon stabilized again as market attention started to focus on the release of U.S. non-farm payrolls later in the day.\par \par \uc2 By 0745 GMT, the dollar was up at Y89.41 from Y89.09 late on Thursday in New York, according to EBS. The euro was also up at Y121.44 from Y120.98 even though the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  got hit by a warning from Jean-Claude Juncker, head of the Eurogroup forum of finance ministers, that the German taxpayer isn't about to come to Greece's rescue.\par \par \uc2 The euro, meanwhile, was trading marginally higher at $1.3581 from $1.3576 as the market waits to see how German Chancellor Angela Merkel responds to Greece's recent austerity package when she meets with Greek Prime Minister George Papandreou in Berlin later in the day.\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg TNI FRX POV\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Reuters   USD/DJ \par    Thomson   P/1066 or P/1074 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Nick Hastings has covered the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets and industry for over 20 years. Apart from his written commentary and analysis, he also appears on Fox Business News and CNBC television in Europe, Asia and the U.S. He can be contacted on +44-20-7842-9493 or by email: nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkBackEurope@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 05-03-10 0818GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1066   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100305e635000mb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
