\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  to boost  \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention \highlight\uc2  war chest\b0\par\par\uc2 782 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2010\par 03:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  account borrowing cap by Y5 trln\par \par \uc2 * Under 2010/11 draft budget, cap to be raised to Y145 trln\par \par \uc2 * Last rise was in April 2004, after massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Adds comments, details)\par \par \uc2 By Shinji Kitamura and Sumio Ito\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 4 (Reuters) - Japan is set to raise its borrowing limit for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time in six years, stirring talk the government may be signalling its willingness to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if needed to curb yen strength.\par \par \uc2 The budget for fiscal 2010/11, starting on April 1, will raise the borrowing ceiling for the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  special account, the war chest for yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , by 5 trillion yen ($56.5 billion) to 145 trillion yen.\par \par \uc2 The increase is included in the budget sent by the government to parliament in January, which was passed by the powerful lower house this week. It is now expected to be enacted before the new fiscal year starts.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for six years, and the last time it raised the borrowing ceiling was in April 2004, right after it ended an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  spree in 2003 and early 2004 in which it sold more than 30 trillion yen.\par \par \uc2 A chief trader at a major bank said the government may be trying to achieve an announcement effect by raising the borrowing cap.\par \par \uc2 "If there is a situation where \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  actually takes place, a 5 trillion yen increase is not all that significant. I think they may just be trying to spread a message that there is a sufficient possibility they could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," the trader said.\par \par \uc2 "It's the type of topic that overseas players who do not have a lot of knowledge about the yen market may get excited about. They may say the increase was conducted with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in mind," the trader said, adding that the government may be aiming to stoke such speculation by increasing the borrowing ceiling.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  finances its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by issuing short-term government bills.\par \par \uc2 The outstanding amount of such bills -- called \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund financing bills -- is estimated to total 110.4 trillion yen at the end of this month, meaning the government could have issued another 30 trillion yen in bills to fund \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , had it wanted to sell yen, even without raising the ceiling from the previous limit of 140 trillion yen.\par \par \uc2 Asked about the rise in the borrowing cap, a \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said: "The upper limit is set so as to allow responses to any type of move in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, and also by taking into account past increases in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund financing bills."\par \par \uc2 HIGH HURDLES\par \par \uc2 In late November, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped closer to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  than at any time since it last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March 2004, by checking exchange rates with commercial banks as the yen rallied to a 14-year high against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ conducts \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which makes decisions on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Naoto Kan also strengthened market views that the government will be less tolerant of a rising yen when he called for a weaker yen after taking the post in January. He later backed off those views but suggested that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained in his tool kit.\par \par \uc2 Still, the hurdles for Japanese authorities to actually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market are seen as being high. For example, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could send the wrong signal at a time when the Group of Seven is encouraging flexibility in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, particularly in China.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, some market players said the implications of the planned increase in the borrowing cap were not clear-cut.\par \par \uc2 Daisaku Ueno, chief analyst at Gaitame.Com Research Institute, said he still thinks Japanese authorities will hold off from yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless the yen surges beyond 85 yen to the dollar toward the peak hit in November and such a jump in the yen takes place amid a sharp fall in Tokyo share prices.\par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities sold a record 20 trillion yen in 2003 and another 15 trillion yen in the first three months of 2004 as they feared the yen's strength would harm the economy and increase deflationary pressure.\par \par \uc2 But they have stayed out of the market since then.\par \par \uc2 The yen stood near 88.15 to the dollar on Thursday. In late November, the yen hit a 14-year high against the dollar of 84.82 yen on trading platform EBS. ($1=88.44 Yen) (Writing by Masayuki Kitano; Editing by Michael Watson)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100304e6340004m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
